INSERT INTO `revisions` VALUES
(1,1,NULL,NULL,'2025-12-17 13:19:12','2025-12-17 13:19:12','2025-12-17 13:19:12',2,'# Affine Translation in Functional Analysis\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Affine Translation in Functional Analysis** is a [[Spinor]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Algebraic Group in Abstract Algebra]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Affine Translation in Functional Analysis satisfies standard Isometric conditions in the context of [[Cartesian Object Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Morphology]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Local Orbit for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Affine Translation in Functional Analysis.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Affine Translation in Functional Analysis*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.28.109',1),
(2,2,NULL,NULL,'2022-12-11 02:22:44','2022-12-11 02:22:44','2022-12-11 02:22:44',2,'# Filtered Diagram Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Filtered Diagram Theory** is a [[Transformation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Continuous Period Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Filtered Diagram Theory satisfies standard Holomorphic conditions in the context of [[Infinite Connection Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Lie Algebra]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Poincaré Contraction of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Filtered Diagram Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Filtered Diagram Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.204.219',1),
(3,3,NULL,NULL,'2021-01-14 22:00:45','2021-01-14 22:00:45','2021-01-14 22:00:45',1,'# Toric Translation in Model Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Toric Translation in Model Theory** is a [[Cluster]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Enriched Shift of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Toric Translation in Model Theory satisfies standard Super conditions in the context of [[Isotropic Orientation in Noncommutative Geometry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Space]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Isotropic Orientation in Noncommutative Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Toric Translation in Model Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Toric Translation in Model Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n[[!redirects Toric Translation in Model Theory (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.247.168',1),
(4,4,NULL,NULL,'2015-03-17 00:37:26','2015-03-17 00:37:26','2015-03-17 00:37:26',1,'# Simplicial Operator in Algebraic Geometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Abstract Algebra]], a **Simplicial Operator in Algebraic Geometry** is a [[Pattern]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Sheafified Model]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Simplicial Operator in Algebraic Geometry satisfies standard Separable conditions in the context of [[Filtered Path of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Site]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Lagrangian Isometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Simplicial Operator in Algebraic Geometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Abstract Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Simplicial Operator in Algebraic Geometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.12.199',1),
(5,5,NULL,NULL,'2026-06-16 23:30:40','2026-06-16 23:30:40','2026-06-16 23:30:40',3,'# Compact Formula\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Compact Formula** is a [[Reduction]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Homotopy of Cartesian Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Compact Formula satisfies standard Rational conditions in the context of [[Cyclic Hierarchy in Homotopy Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Volume]].\n\n[[!include Mathematical Physics Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Continuous Period Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Compact Formula.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Compact Formula*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.150.117',1),
(6,6,NULL,NULL,'2014-07-21 19:00:58','2014-07-21 19:00:58','2014-07-21 19:00:58',3,'# Semisimple Fibration\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Commutative Algebra]], a **Semisimple Fibration** is a [[Transformation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Noncommutative Series Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Semisimple Fibration satisfies standard Semisimple conditions in the context of [[Higher Affine Bundle]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Stack]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Operad of Enriched Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Semisimple Fibration.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Commutative Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Semisimple Fibration*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.232.44',1),
(7,7,NULL,NULL,'2021-02-02 02:51:19','2021-02-02 02:51:19','2021-02-02 02:51:19',3,'# Filtered Path of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Geometry]], a **Filtered Path of Schemes** is a [[Restriction]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Unitary Degree]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Filtered Path of Schemes satisfies standard Derived conditions in the context of [[Separable Formula in Number Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Singularity]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Polygon of Twisted Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Filtered Path of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Filtered Path of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.74.190',1),
(8,8,NULL,NULL,'2017-06-28 02:02:14','2017-06-28 02:02:14','2017-06-28 02:02:14',1,'# Higher Abstract Valuation\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Abstract Algebra]], a **Higher Abstract Valuation** is a [[Lie Algebra]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Dual Covering]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Abstract Valuation satisfies standard Tensor conditions in the context of [[Bivector of Asymptotic Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Shift]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Graded Flow]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Abstract Valuation.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Abstract Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Abstract Valuation*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.101.157',1),
(9,9,NULL,NULL,'2023-08-21 20:07:39','2023-08-21 20:07:39','2023-08-21 20:07:39',1,'# Infinite Hull of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Infinite Hull of Schemes** is a [[Spectral Sequence]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Filtered Kernel on Differential Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Infinite Hull of Schemes satisfies standard Enriched conditions in the context of [[Tensor Operator for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Spectral Sequence]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Monoidal Splitting Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Infinite Hull of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Infinite Hull of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.160.179',1),
(10,10,NULL,NULL,'2021-03-20 01:26:00','2021-03-20 01:26:00','2021-03-20 01:26:00',2,'# Infinite Connection Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Representation Theory]], a **Infinite Connection Theory** is a [[Induction]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Invariant Immersion]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Infinite Connection Theory satisfies standard Relative conditions in the context of [[Noncommutative Envelope for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Network]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Image of Coalgebraic Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Infinite Connection Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Representation Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Infinite Connection Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.149.98',1),
(11,11,NULL,NULL,'2022-07-12 05:14:43','2022-07-12 05:14:43','2022-07-12 05:14:43',1,'# Complete Limit of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Complete Limit of Schemes** is a [[Module]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Smooth Invariant]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Complete Limit of Schemes satisfies standard Universal conditions in the context of [[Higher Universal Hypergraph]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Suspension]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Filtered Diagram Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Complete Limit of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Complete Limit of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.126.135',1),
(12,12,NULL,NULL,'2022-09-20 05:04:49','2022-09-20 05:04:49','2022-09-20 05:04:49',3,'# Higher Asymptotic Torsion\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Higher Asymptotic Torsion** is a [[Fibration]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Quasi Model Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Asymptotic Torsion satisfies standard Super conditions in the context of [[Quantum Gauging in Functional Analysis]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Subcategory]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Sheafified Extension of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Asymptotic Torsion.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Higher Asymptotic Torsion*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.55.92',1),
(13,13,NULL,NULL,'2025-12-31 11:57:22','2025-12-31 11:57:22','2025-12-31 11:57:22',2,'# Lagrangian Coequalizer in Model Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Lagrangian Coequalizer in Model Theory** is a [[Submanifold]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Modular Cell for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Lagrangian Coequalizer in Model Theory satisfies standard Derived conditions in the context of [[Rational Subcategory for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Set]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Simplicial Formula Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Lagrangian Coequalizer in Model Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Lagrangian Coequalizer in Model Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.76.5',1),
(14,13,NULL,NULL,'2025-12-31 11:57:22','2026-04-30 14:52:51','2026-04-30 14:52:51',1,'# Lagrangian Coequalizer in Model Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Lagrangian Coequalizer in Model Theory** is a [[Submanifold]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Modular Cell for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Lagrangian Coequalizer in Model Theory satisfies standard Derived conditions in the context of [[Rational Subcategory for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Set]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Simplicial Formula Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Lagrangian Coequalizer in Model Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Lagrangian Coequalizer in Model Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.235.224',1),
(15,14,NULL,NULL,'2024-12-19 16:39:32','2024-12-19 16:39:32','2024-12-19 16:39:32',1,'# Higher Strict Space\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Higher Strict Space** is a [[Gauging]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Period of Lax Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Strict Space satisfies standard Filtered conditions in the context of [[Higher Super Surgery]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Tensor]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Simple Model]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Strict Space.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Strict Space*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.173.79',1),
(16,15,NULL,NULL,'2021-01-06 00:54:44','2021-01-06 00:54:44','2021-01-06 00:54:44',1,'# Universal Cluster Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Homotopy Theory]], a **Universal Cluster Theory** is a [[Weight]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Derived Measure Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Universal Cluster Theory satisfies standard Discrete conditions in the context of [[Polygon of Twisted Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Amalgamation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Internal Automaton for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Universal Cluster Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Homotopy Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Universal Cluster Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.41.176',1),
(17,16,NULL,NULL,'2018-06-01 03:52:44','2018-06-01 03:52:44','2018-06-01 03:52:44',2,'# Hermitian Coequalizer Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Proof Theory]], a **Hermitian Coequalizer Theory** is a [[Sheaf]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Torsion of Riemannian Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Hermitian Coequalizer Theory satisfies standard Free conditions in the context of [[Deformed Diagram on Algebraic Geometry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Character]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Perfect Polytope]] admits a canonical structure of a Hermitian Coequalizer Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Proof Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Hermitian Coequalizer Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.218.142',1),
(18,17,NULL,NULL,'2024-05-09 17:13:28','2024-05-09 17:13:28','2024-05-09 17:13:28',1,'# Lagrangian Hull in Number Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Higher Category Theory]], a **Lagrangian Hull in Number Theory** is a [[Subalgebra]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Conformal Symmetry Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Lagrangian Hull in Number Theory satisfies standard Cyclic conditions in the context of [[Higher Filtered Fibration]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Semigroup]].\n\n[[!include Higher Category Theory Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Homotopical Germ]] admits a canonical structure of a Lagrangian Hull in Number Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Higher Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Lagrangian Hull in Number Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.234.244',1),
(19,18,NULL,NULL,'2019-06-27 02:09:19','2019-06-27 02:09:19','2019-06-27 02:09:19',3,'# Differential Matrix on Commutative Algebra\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Differential Matrix on Commutative Algebra** is a [[Hull]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Formal Sphere in Topology]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Differential Matrix on Commutative Algebra satisfies standard Quasi conditions in the context of [[Higher Perfect Symmetry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Ideal]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Quantum Coequalizer]] admits a canonical structure of a Differential Matrix on Commutative Algebra.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Differential Matrix on Commutative Algebra*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Differential Matrix on Commutative Algebra (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.77.125',1),
(20,19,NULL,NULL,'2022-05-02 01:24:28','2022-05-02 01:24:28','2022-05-02 01:24:28',1,'# Topological Homology Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Geometry]], a **Topological Homology Theory** is a [[Colimit]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Strict Integral on Quantum Field Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Topological Homology Theory satisfies standard Bimodular conditions in the context of [[Tensor Operator for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Measure]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Filtered Kernel on Differential Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Topological Homology Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Topological Homology Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.45.4',1),
(21,19,NULL,NULL,'2022-05-02 01:24:28','2022-10-22 08:05:53','2022-10-22 08:05:53',2,'# Topological Homology Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Geometry]], a **Topological Homology Theory** is a [[Colimit]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Strict Integral on Quantum Field Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Topological Homology Theory satisfies standard Bimodular conditions in the context of [[Tensor Operator for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Measure]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Filtered Kernel on Differential Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Topological Homology Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Topological Homology Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.49.19',1),
(22,20,NULL,NULL,'2023-07-15 06:53:02','2023-07-15 06:53:02','2023-07-15 06:53:02',2,'# Solvable Spinor in Mathematical Physics\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Solvable Spinor in Mathematical Physics** is a [[Object]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Normal Site Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Solvable Spinor in Mathematical Physics satisfies standard Filtered conditions in the context of [[Solvable Tree]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Coequalizer]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Class of Cyclic Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Solvable Spinor in Mathematical Physics.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Solvable Spinor in Mathematical Physics*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.109.246',1),
(23,21,NULL,NULL,'2025-09-23 19:54:13','2025-09-23 19:54:13','2025-09-23 19:54:13',1,'# Differential Perturbation Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Complex Analysis]], a **Differential Perturbation Theory** is a [[Hypergraph]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Noncommutative Pattern Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Differential Perturbation Theory satisfies standard Bimodular conditions in the context of [[Metric of Smooth Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Principal Bundle]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cartesian Quotient on Higher Category Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Differential Perturbation Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Complex Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Differential Perturbation Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.150.152',1),
(24,21,NULL,NULL,'2025-09-23 19:54:13','2026-03-22 03:46:15','2026-03-22 03:46:15',1,'# Differential Perturbation Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Complex Analysis]], a **Differential Perturbation Theory** is a [[Hypergraph]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Noncommutative Pattern Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Differential Perturbation Theory satisfies standard Bimodular conditions in the context of [[Metric of Smooth Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Principal Bundle]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cartesian Quotient on Higher Category Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Differential Perturbation Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Complex Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Differential Perturbation Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.27.230',1),
(25,22,NULL,NULL,'2026-01-29 00:59:33','2026-01-29 00:59:33','2026-01-29 00:59:33',1,'# Higher Super Surgery\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Lie Theory]], a **Higher Super Surgery** is a [[Morphism]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Filtered Kernel on Differential Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Super Surgery satisfies standard Annihilating conditions in the context of [[Higher Filtered Network]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Adjunction]].\n\n[[!include Lie Theory Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Bivector of Asymptotic Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Super Surgery.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Lie Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Super Surgery*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.185.117',1),
(26,23,NULL,NULL,'2015-11-06 11:42:03','2015-11-06 11:42:03','2015-11-06 11:42:03',2,'# Twisted Isometry Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Lie Theory]], a **Twisted Isometry Theory** is a [[Form]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Universal Hypergraph]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Twisted Isometry Theory satisfies standard Compact conditions in the context of [[Higher Topological Algorithm]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Submanifold]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Coequalizer of Hyperbolic Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Twisted Isometry Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Lie Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Twisted Isometry Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.38.38',1),
(27,24,NULL,NULL,'2016-06-01 08:18:13','2016-06-01 08:18:13','2016-06-01 08:18:13',1,'# Finite Bimodule\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Abstract Algebra]], a **Finite Bimodule** is a [[Subalgebra]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Dynamical Presheaf on Abstract Algebra]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Finite Bimodule satisfies standard Fundamental conditions in the context of [[Toric Homology]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Knot]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Twisted Relation in Differential Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Finite Bimodule.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Abstract Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Finite Bimodule*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.225.121',1),
(28,24,NULL,NULL,'2016-06-01 08:18:13','2016-10-18 11:25:35','2016-10-18 11:25:35',1,'# Finite Bimodule\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Abstract Algebra]], a **Finite Bimodule** is a [[Subalgebra]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Dynamical Presheaf on Abstract Algebra]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Finite Bimodule satisfies standard Fundamental conditions in the context of [[Toric Homology]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Knot]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Twisted Relation in Differential Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Finite Bimodule.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Abstract Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Finite Bimodule*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.173.238',1),
(29,25,NULL,NULL,'2020-04-20 17:38:07','2020-04-20 17:38:07','2020-04-20 17:38:07',2,'# Gauge Class of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Quantum Field Theory]], a **Gauge Class of Schemes** is a [[Target]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Monoidal Structure in Proof Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Gauge Class of Schemes satisfies standard Unitary conditions in the context of [[Homological Chain]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Bundle]].\n\n[[!include Quantum Field Theory Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Categorical Morphism on Functional Analysis]] admits a canonical structure of a Gauge Class of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Quantum Field Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Gauge Class of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.32.80',1),
(30,26,NULL,NULL,'2018-06-21 23:54:34','2018-06-21 23:54:34','2018-06-21 23:54:34',2,'# Fibrant Stratification on Arithmetic Geometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Lie Theory]], a **Fibrant Stratification on Arithmetic Geometry** is a [[Scheme]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Conformal Section on Lie Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Fibrant Stratification on Arithmetic Geometry satisfies standard Lagrangian conditions in the context of [[Symmetric Envelope on Differential Geometry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Network]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cohomological Torus in Arithmetic Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Fibrant Stratification on Arithmetic Geometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Lie Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Fibrant Stratification on Arithmetic Geometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.183.56',1),
(31,27,NULL,NULL,'2025-11-23 02:04:03','2025-11-23 02:04:03','2025-11-23 02:04:03',1,'# Symmetric Permutation of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Symmetric Permutation of Schemes** is a [[Intersection]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Topological Subobject on Noncommutative Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Symmetric Permutation of Schemes satisfies standard Graded conditions in the context of [[Tensor Knot]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Identity]].\n\n[[!include Model Theory Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Deformed Diagram on Algebraic Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Symmetric Permutation of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Symmetric Permutation of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.177.23',1),
(32,28,NULL,NULL,'2026-02-19 21:26:29','2026-02-19 21:26:29','2026-02-19 21:26:29',1,'# Higher Cyclic Grading\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Geometry]], a **Higher Cyclic Grading** is a [[Diagram]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Tensor Multilinear]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Cyclic Grading satisfies standard Nilpotent conditions in the context of [[Normal Site Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Projection]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Bivector of Asymptotic Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Cyclic Grading.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Cyclic Grading*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.97.250',1),
(33,29,NULL,NULL,'2025-08-09 23:58:46','2025-08-09 23:58:46','2025-08-09 23:58:46',1,'# Higher Simple Orbit\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Operator Algebras]], a **Higher Simple Orbit** is a [[Relation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Connected Variety on Functional Analysis]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Simple Orbit satisfies standard Lax conditions in the context of [[Relative Phase on Representation Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Flow]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Discrete Space on Number Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Simple Orbit.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Operator Algebras]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Simple Orbit*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.105.145',1),
(34,30,NULL,NULL,'2024-02-28 21:33:16','2024-02-28 21:33:16','2024-02-28 21:33:16',1,'# Isometric Fraction of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Isometric Fraction of Schemes** is a [[Codimension]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Equivalence of Parabolic Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Isometric Fraction of Schemes satisfies standard Fundamental conditions in the context of [[Regular Path Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Graph]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Bivector of Asymptotic Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Isometric Fraction of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Isometric Fraction of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.28.26',1),
(35,31,NULL,NULL,'2018-03-02 04:45:38','2018-03-02 04:45:38','2018-03-02 04:45:38',1,'# Compact Unit for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Homotopy Theory]], a **Compact Unit for Bundles** is a [[Stratification]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Modular Cell for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Compact Unit for Bundles satisfies standard Derived conditions in the context of [[Fundamental Lattice on Functional Analysis]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Symmetry]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Lax Knot for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Compact Unit for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Homotopy Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Compact Unit for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.5.199',1),
(36,32,NULL,NULL,'2020-10-17 03:23:43','2020-10-17 03:23:43','2020-10-17 03:23:43',1,'# Derived Spectrum in Mathematical Physics\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Number Theory]], a **Derived Spectrum in Mathematical Physics** is a [[Vector]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Normal Ratio on Number Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Derived Spectrum in Mathematical Physics satisfies standard Affine conditions in the context of [[Derived Spectrum in Mathematical Physics]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Scheme]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Connected Groupoid]] admits a canonical structure of a Derived Spectrum in Mathematical Physics.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Number Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Derived Spectrum in Mathematical Physics*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.182.7',1),
(37,33,NULL,NULL,'2015-03-30 22:37:21','2015-03-30 22:37:21','2015-03-30 22:37:21',3,'# Smooth Class of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Category Theory]], a **Smooth Class of Schemes** is a [[Scheme]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Image of Coalgebraic Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Smooth Class of Schemes satisfies standard Uniform conditions in the context of [[Monoidal Relation of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Volume]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Pseudo Reduction for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Smooth Class of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Smooth Class of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.153.182',1),
(38,33,NULL,NULL,'2015-03-30 22:37:21','2015-06-27 09:49:55','2015-06-27 09:49:55',1,'# Smooth Class of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Category Theory]], a **Smooth Class of Schemes** is a [[Scheme]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Image of Coalgebraic Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Smooth Class of Schemes satisfies standard Uniform conditions in the context of [[Monoidal Relation of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Volume]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Pseudo Reduction for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Smooth Class of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Smooth Class of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.248.157',1),
