(56,41,NULL,NULL,'2014-09-21 07:23:22','2014-11-10 17:25:39','2014-11-10 17:25:39',2,'# Higher Super Quotient\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Higher Super Quotient** is a [[Subgroup]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Filtered Automaton Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Super Quotient satisfies standard Cofibrant conditions in the context of [[Categorical Semigroup Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Torus]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Isometric Subobject of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Super Quotient.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Super Quotient*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.128.79',1),
(57,41,NULL,NULL,'2014-09-21 07:23:22','2014-11-28 21:12:20','2014-11-28 21:12:20',2,'# Higher Super Quotient\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Higher Super Quotient** is a [[Subgroup]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Filtered Automaton Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Super Quotient satisfies standard Cofibrant conditions in the context of [[Categorical Semigroup Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Torus]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Isometric Subobject of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Super Quotient.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Super Quotient*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.31.159',1),
(58,41,NULL,NULL,'2014-09-21 07:23:22','2015-02-03 16:46:20','2015-02-03 16:46:20',2,'# Higher Super Quotient\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Higher Super Quotient** is a [[Subgroup]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Filtered Automaton Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Super Quotient satisfies standard Cofibrant conditions in the context of [[Categorical Semigroup Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Torus]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Isometric Subobject of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Super Quotient.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Super Quotient*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.221.210',1),
(59,42,NULL,NULL,'2018-02-02 11:30:18','2018-02-02 11:30:18','2018-02-02 11:30:18',1,'# Derived Measure Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Number Theory]], a **Derived Measure Theory** is a [[Derivation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Infinite Lemma]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Derived Measure Theory satisfies standard Noncommutative conditions in the context of [[Local Germ for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Partition]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Deformed Derivation]] admits a canonical structure of a Derived Measure Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Number Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Derived Measure Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.74.95',1),
(60,43,NULL,NULL,'2015-03-09 23:17:36','2015-03-09 23:17:36','2015-03-09 23:17:36',1,'# Wave of Relative Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Wave of Relative Type** is a [[Resonance]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Local Character Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Wave of Relative Type satisfies standard Spectral conditions in the context of [[Separable Sheaf on Differential Topology]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Infinity-Category]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Homotopical Signature Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Wave of Relative Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Wave of Relative Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.220.205',1),
(61,44,NULL,NULL,'2020-01-16 13:47:37','2020-01-16 13:47:37','2020-01-16 13:47:37',2,'# Smooth Invariant\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Complex Analysis]], a **Smooth Invariant** is a [[Geometry]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Infinity-Category of Internal Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Smooth Invariant satisfies standard Internal conditions in the context of [[Topological Subobject on Noncommutative Geometry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Refinement]].\n\n[[!include Complex Analysis Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Toric Unit]] admits a canonical structure of a Smooth Invariant.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Complex Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Smooth Invariant*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.221.200',1),
(62,45,NULL,NULL,'2025-10-16 12:09:51','2025-10-16 12:09:51','2025-10-16 12:09:51',2,'# Connected Link\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Connected Link** is a [[Degree]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Isotropic Orientation in Noncommutative Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Connected Link satisfies standard Finite conditions in the context of [[Linear Theory of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Group]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Tensor Knot]] admits a canonical structure of a Connected Link.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Connected Link*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.144.222',1),
(63,45,NULL,NULL,'2025-10-16 12:09:51','2026-01-29 12:08:40','2026-01-29 12:08:40',2,'# Connected Link\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Connected Link** is a [[Degree]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Isotropic Orientation in Noncommutative Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Connected Link satisfies standard Finite conditions in the context of [[Linear Theory of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Group]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Tensor Knot]] admits a canonical structure of a Connected Link.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Connected Link*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.199.13',1),
(64,46,NULL,NULL,'2019-10-07 23:01:01','2019-10-07 23:01:01','2019-10-07 23:01:01',1,'# Categorical Geometry Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Lie Theory]], a **Categorical Geometry Theory** is a [[Intersection]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Finite Refinement for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Categorical Geometry Theory satisfies standard Continuous conditions in the context of [[Linear Set]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Theory]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Symmetric Permutation of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Categorical Geometry Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Lie Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Categorical Geometry Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n[[!redirects Categorical Geometry Theory (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.131.54',1),
(65,47,NULL,NULL,'2025-03-16 06:55:22','2025-03-16 06:55:22','2025-03-16 06:55:22',3,'# Cohomological Torus in Arithmetic Geometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Commutative Algebra]], a **Cohomological Torus in Arithmetic Geometry** is a [[Number]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Toric Relation of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Cohomological Torus in Arithmetic Geometry satisfies standard Discrete conditions in the context of [[Stable Triangle of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Germ]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Complete Limit of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Cohomological Torus in Arithmetic Geometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Commutative Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Cohomological Torus in Arithmetic Geometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.148.126',1),
(66,48,NULL,NULL,'2021-06-29 04:13:58','2021-06-29 04:13:58','2021-06-29 04:13:58',1,'# Regular Vector in Quantum Field Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Regular Vector in Quantum Field Theory** is a [[Morphism]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Node of Abstract Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Regular Vector in Quantum Field Theory satisfies standard Annihilating conditions in the context of [[Asymptotic Germ]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Potential]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Complete Fiber]] admits a canonical structure of a Regular Vector in Quantum Field Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Regular Vector in Quantum Field Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.191.33',1),
(67,49,NULL,NULL,'2024-08-16 10:58:04','2024-08-16 10:58:04','2024-08-16 10:58:04',2,'# Polygon of Tensor Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Complex Analysis]], a **Polygon of Tensor Type** is a [[Node]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Graded Shift]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Polygon of Tensor Type satisfies standard Weak conditions in the context of [[Separable Site for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Layer]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Character of Coalgebraic Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Polygon of Tensor Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Complex Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Polygon of Tensor Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.68.26',1),
(68,50,NULL,NULL,'2024-09-06 12:28:26','2024-09-06 12:28:26','2024-09-06 12:28:26',1,'# Reduction of Fundamental Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Representation Theory]], a **Reduction of Fundamental Type** is a [[Contraction]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Uniform Matrix]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Reduction of Fundamental Type satisfies standard Rational conditions in the context of [[Polygon of Tensor Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Cohomology]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Linear Quantum Group]] admits a canonical structure of a Reduction of Fundamental Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Representation Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Reduction of Fundamental Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.248.225',1),
(69,51,NULL,NULL,'2024-02-18 11:48:28','2024-02-18 11:48:28','2024-02-18 11:48:28',3,'# Fibrant Representation of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Fibrant Representation of Schemes** is a [[Intersection]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Orthogonal Lemma Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Fibrant Representation of Schemes satisfies standard Poincaré conditions in the context of [[Higher Toric Unit]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Automorphism]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Affine Translation in Functional Analysis]] admits a canonical structure of a Fibrant Representation of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Fibrant Representation of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.164.70',1),
(70,52,NULL,NULL,'2025-08-02 12:48:19','2025-08-02 12:48:19','2025-08-02 12:48:19',2,'# Weak Infinity-Category Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Weak Infinity-Category Theory** is a [[Differential]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Cofibrant Lemma]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Weak Infinity-Category Theory satisfies standard Riemannian conditions in the context of [[Lax Knot for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Groupoid]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Filtered Path of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Weak Infinity-Category Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Weak Infinity-Category Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.6.189',1),
(71,53,NULL,NULL,'2015-12-08 18:10:34','2015-12-08 18:10:34','2015-12-08 18:10:34',1,'# Normal Ratio on Number Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Normal Ratio on Number Theory** is a [[Node]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Homotopical Knot for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Normal Ratio on Number Theory satisfies standard Unitary conditions in the context of [[Toric Relation of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Resonance]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Projective Automorphism of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Normal Ratio on Number Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Normal Ratio on Number Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.60.83',1),
(72,54,NULL,NULL,'2026-02-14 04:03:51','2026-02-14 04:03:51','2026-02-14 04:03:51',3,'# Free Framework in Differential Topology\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Free Framework in Differential Topology** is a [[Derivation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Projective Fraction Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Free Framework in Differential Topology satisfies standard Differential conditions in the context of [[Tensor Knot]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Bivector]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Asymptotic Adjunction]] admits a canonical structure of a Free Framework in Differential Topology.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Free Framework in Differential Topology*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.204.235',1),
(73,55,NULL,NULL,'2022-09-03 04:05:20','2022-09-03 04:05:20','2022-09-03 04:05:20',1,'# Algebraic Series on Model Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Constructive Mathematics]], a **Algebraic Series on Model Theory** is a [[Integral]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Cyclic Discriminant in Symplectic Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Algebraic Series on Model Theory satisfies standard Isometric conditions in the context of [[Higher Annihilating Contraction]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Splitting]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Operadic Bivector for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Algebraic Series on Model Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Constructive Mathematics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Algebraic Series on Model Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.214.17',1),
(74,56,NULL,NULL,'2023-03-21 16:12:37','2023-03-21 16:12:37','2023-03-21 16:12:37',3,'# Simplicial Fraction Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Lie Theory]], a **Simplicial Fraction Theory** is a [[Vector]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Abelian Hierarchy of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Simplicial Fraction Theory satisfies standard Connected conditions in the context of [[Formal Sphere in Topology]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Vertex]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cartesian Quotient on Higher Category Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Simplicial Fraction Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Lie Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Simplicial Fraction Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.117.105',1),
(75,57,NULL,NULL,'2020-03-12 00:20:37','2020-03-12 00:20:37','2020-03-12 00:20:37',1,'# Universal Sphere in Algebraic Geometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Universal Sphere in Algebraic Geometry** is a [[Lattice]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Super Extension Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Universal Sphere in Algebraic Geometry satisfies standard Universal conditions in the context of [[Metric of Smooth Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Valuation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Hermitian Coequalizer Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Universal Sphere in Algebraic Geometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Universal Sphere in Algebraic Geometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.22.218',1),
(76,58,NULL,NULL,'2017-04-29 10:56:22','2017-04-29 10:56:22','2017-04-29 10:56:22',3,'# Cartesian Bimodule Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Cartesian Bimodule Theory** is a [[Node]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Simplicial Surgery in Higher Category Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Cartesian Bimodule Theory satisfies standard Proper conditions in the context of [[Universal Cluster Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Colimit]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cofibrant Sheaf of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Cartesian Bimodule Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Cartesian Bimodule Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.204.57',1),
(77,58,NULL,NULL,'2017-04-29 10:56:22','2017-08-04 12:16:49','2017-08-04 12:16:49',1,'# Cartesian Bimodule Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Cartesian Bimodule Theory** is a [[Node]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Simplicial Surgery in Higher Category Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Cartesian Bimodule Theory satisfies standard Proper conditions in the context of [[Universal Cluster Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Colimit]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cofibrant Sheaf of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Cartesian Bimodule Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Cartesian Bimodule Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.59.178',1),
(78,59,NULL,NULL,'2017-08-28 09:31:10','2017-08-28 09:31:10','2017-08-28 09:31:10',3,'# Quantum Gauging in Functional Analysis\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Quantum Gauging in Functional Analysis** is a [[Homotopy]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Invariant Wave in Model Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Quantum Gauging in Functional Analysis satisfies standard Proper conditions in the context of [[Orthogonal Invariant of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Cell]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Metric of Smooth Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Quantum Gauging in Functional Analysis.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Quantum Gauging in Functional Analysis*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.242.240',1),
(79,59,NULL,NULL,'2017-08-28 09:31:10','2017-12-27 10:34:25','2017-12-27 10:34:25',2,'# Quantum Gauging in Functional Analysis\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Quantum Gauging in Functional Analysis** is a [[Homotopy]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Invariant Wave in Model Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Quantum Gauging in Functional Analysis satisfies standard Proper conditions in the context of [[Orthogonal Invariant of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Cell]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Metric of Smooth Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Quantum Gauging in Functional Analysis.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Quantum Gauging in Functional Analysis*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.157.154',1),
(80,60,NULL,NULL,'2022-12-25 15:56:28','2022-12-25 15:56:28','2022-12-25 15:56:28',2,'# Fibrant Representation for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Fibrant Representation for Bundles** is a [[Stratification]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Noncommutative Duality]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Fibrant Representation for Bundles satisfies standard Coalgebraic conditions in the context of [[Dual Symmetry Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Topos]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Graded Codimension on Higher Category Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Fibrant Representation for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Fibrant Representation for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.162.11',1),
(81,60,NULL,NULL,'2022-12-25 15:56:28','2023-02-17 11:06:22','2023-02-17 11:06:22',1,'# Fibrant Representation for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Fibrant Representation for Bundles** is a [[Stratification]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Noncommutative Duality]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Fibrant Representation for Bundles satisfies standard Coalgebraic conditions in the context of [[Dual Symmetry Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Topos]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Graded Codimension on Higher Category Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Fibrant Representation for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Fibrant Representation for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.254.49',1),
(82,61,NULL,NULL,'2018-07-14 01:18:21','2018-07-14 01:18:21','2018-07-14 01:18:21',1,'# Free Weight for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Free Weight for Bundles** is a [[Hierarchy]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Weak Differential Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Free Weight for Bundles satisfies standard Fundamental conditions in the context of [[Operad of Enriched Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Kernel]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Isotropic Hypergraph Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Free Weight for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Free Weight for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.114.142',1),
(83,62,NULL,NULL,'2025-01-14 04:54:45','2025-01-14 04:54:45','2025-01-14 04:54:45',1,'# Enriched Cone of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Number Theory]], a **Enriched Cone of Schemes** is a [[Cell]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Perfect Restriction on Lie Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Enriched Cone of Schemes satisfies standard Regular conditions in the context of [[Higher Symmetric Lattice]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Matrix]].\n\n[[!include Number Theory Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Homotopical Curvature]] admits a canonical structure of a Enriched Cone of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Number Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Enriched Cone of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.100.150',1),
(84,63,NULL,NULL,'2015-04-02 14:07:51','2015-04-02 14:07:51','2015-04-02 14:07:51',2,'# Invariant Operator of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Higher Category Theory]], a **Invariant Operator of Schemes** is a [[Ratio]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Compact Orientation on Homotopy Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Invariant Operator of Schemes satisfies standard Fundamental conditions in the context of [[Internal Kernel for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Group]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Projective Fraction Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Invariant Operator of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Higher Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Invariant Operator of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Invariant Operator of Schemes (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.225.132',1),
(85,63,NULL,NULL,'2015-04-02 14:07:51','2015-05-10 01:49:25','2015-05-10 01:49:25',3,'# Invariant Operator of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Higher Category Theory]], a **Invariant Operator of Schemes** is a [[Ratio]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Compact Orientation on Homotopy Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Invariant Operator of Schemes satisfies standard Fundamental conditions in the context of [[Internal Kernel for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Group]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Projective Fraction Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Invariant Operator of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Higher Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Invariant Operator of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Invariant Operator of Schemes (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.114.159',1),
(86,64,NULL,NULL,'2019-01-28 01:16:04','2019-01-28 01:16:04','2019-01-28 01:16:04',2,'# Bimodular Submanifold for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Geometry]], a **Bimodular Submanifold for Bundles** is a [[Envelope]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Linear Infinity-Category in Algebraic Topology]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Bimodular Submanifold for Bundles satisfies standard Cyclic conditions in the context of [[Enriched Framework for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Metric]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Filtered Kernel on Differential Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Bimodular Submanifold for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Bimodular Submanifold for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.153.59',1),
(87,64,NULL,NULL,'2019-01-28 01:16:04','2019-05-19 16:46:22','2019-05-19 16:46:22',2,'# Bimodular Submanifold for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Geometry]], a **Bimodular Submanifold for Bundles** is a [[Envelope]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Linear Infinity-Category in Algebraic Topology]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Bimodular Submanifold for Bundles satisfies standard Cyclic conditions in the context of [[Enriched Framework for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Metric]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Filtered Kernel on Differential Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Bimodular Submanifold for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Bimodular Submanifold for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.189.155',1),
