(91,64,NULL,NULL,'2019-01-28 01:16:04','2019-07-12 12:46:32','2019-07-12 12:46:32',1,'# Bimodular Submanifold for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Geometry]], a **Bimodular Submanifold for Bundles** is a [[Envelope]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Linear Infinity-Category in Algebraic Topology]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Bimodular Submanifold for Bundles satisfies standard Cyclic conditions in the context of [[Enriched Framework for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Metric]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Filtered Kernel on Differential Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Bimodular Submanifold for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Bimodular Submanifold for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.168.139',1),
(92,65,NULL,NULL,'2024-04-14 15:40:42','2024-04-14 15:40:42','2024-04-14 15:40:42',2,'# Categorical Network in Algebraic Topology\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Abstract Algebra]], a **Categorical Network in Algebraic Topology** is a [[Stratification]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Asymptotic Germ]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Categorical Network in Algebraic Topology satisfies standard Semisimple conditions in the context of [[Relative Target for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Network]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Weak Symmetry Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Categorical Network in Algebraic Topology.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Abstract Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Categorical Network in Algebraic Topology*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.238.245',1),
(93,65,NULL,NULL,'2024-04-14 15:40:42','2024-07-28 23:34:37','2024-07-28 23:34:37',1,'# Categorical Network in Algebraic Topology\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Abstract Algebra]], a **Categorical Network in Algebraic Topology** is a [[Stratification]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Asymptotic Germ]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Categorical Network in Algebraic Topology satisfies standard Semisimple conditions in the context of [[Relative Target for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Network]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Weak Symmetry Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Categorical Network in Algebraic Topology.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Abstract Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Categorical Network in Algebraic Topology*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.107.197',1),
(94,66,NULL,NULL,'2015-06-29 15:47:51','2015-06-29 15:47:51','2015-06-29 15:47:51',3,'# Conformal Grading on Abstract Algebra\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Conformal Grading on Abstract Algebra** is a [[Permutation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Coequalizer of Hyperbolic Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Conformal Grading on Abstract Algebra satisfies standard Categorical conditions in the context of [[Simplicial Operator in Algebraic Geometry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Atlas]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cofibrant Complex on Geometric Group Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Conformal Grading on Abstract Algebra.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Conformal Grading on Abstract Algebra*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.167.112',1),
(95,67,NULL,NULL,'2016-11-06 09:49:58','2016-11-06 09:49:58','2016-11-06 09:49:58',3,'# Regular Path Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Quantum Field Theory]], a **Regular Path Theory** is a [[Target]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Normal Gauging for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Regular Path Theory satisfies standard Quasi conditions in the context of [[Modular Volume of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Topos]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Stable Subspace of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Regular Path Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Quantum Field Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Regular Path Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.185.73',1),
(96,68,NULL,NULL,'2025-01-23 22:05:24','2025-01-23 22:05:24','2025-01-23 22:05:24',3,'# Dynamical Hull of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Dynamical Hull of Schemes** is a [[Automaton]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Simplicial Tree of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Dynamical Hull of Schemes satisfies standard Smooth conditions in the context of [[Cohomology of Tensor Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Submanifold]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cone of Strict Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Dynamical Hull of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Dynamical Hull of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.160.160',1),
(97,68,NULL,NULL,'2025-01-23 22:05:24','2025-06-30 05:59:50','2025-06-30 05:59:50',2,'# Dynamical Hull of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Dynamical Hull of Schemes** is a [[Automaton]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Simplicial Tree of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Dynamical Hull of Schemes satisfies standard Smooth conditions in the context of [[Cohomology of Tensor Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Submanifold]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cone of Strict Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Dynamical Hull of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Dynamical Hull of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.100.38',1),
(98,69,NULL,NULL,'2017-02-10 20:06:13','2017-02-10 20:06:13','2017-02-10 20:06:13',2,'# Coalgebraic Patch Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Coalgebraic Patch Theory** is a [[Integral]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Cohomological Stability]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Coalgebraic Patch Theory satisfies standard Sheafified conditions in the context of [[Universal Refinement]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Scheme]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Linear Theory of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Coalgebraic Patch Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Coalgebraic Patch Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.53.231',1),
(99,70,NULL,NULL,'2020-12-09 00:32:11','2020-12-09 00:32:11','2020-12-09 00:32:11',1,'# Bimodular State in Model Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Geometry]], a **Bimodular State in Model Theory** is a [[Lie Algebra]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Riemannian Algorithm]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Bimodular State in Model Theory satisfies standard Filtered conditions in the context of [[Higher Toric Resolution]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Transformation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Riemannian Domain in Functional Analysis]] admits a canonical structure of a Bimodular State in Model Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Bimodular State in Model Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.176.66',1),
(100,71,NULL,NULL,'2020-07-24 07:51:03','2020-07-24 07:51:03','2020-07-24 07:51:03',1,'# Compact Triangulation\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Compact Triangulation** is a [[Induction]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Frobenius Bivector for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Compact Triangulation satisfies standard Bimodular conditions in the context of [[Compact Spinor]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Suspension]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Coalgebraic Section for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Compact Triangulation.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Compact Triangulation*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n[[!redirects Compact Triangulation (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.101.11',1),
(101,72,NULL,NULL,'2025-07-02 14:10:58','2025-07-02 14:10:58','2025-07-02 14:10:58',1,'# Presheaf of Modular Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Topology]], a **Presheaf of Modular Type** is a [[Equation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Polygon of Twisted Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Presheaf of Modular Type satisfies standard Twisted conditions in the context of [[Twisted Isometry Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Gauging]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Differential Infinity-Category in Homotopy Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Presheaf of Modular Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Presheaf of Modular Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Presheaf of Modular Type (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.75.34',1),
(102,72,NULL,NULL,'2025-07-02 14:10:58','2025-11-15 09:16:25','2025-11-15 09:16:25',2,'# Presheaf of Modular Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Topology]], a **Presheaf of Modular Type** is a [[Equation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Polygon of Twisted Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Presheaf of Modular Type satisfies standard Twisted conditions in the context of [[Twisted Isometry Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Gauging]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Differential Infinity-Category in Homotopy Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Presheaf of Modular Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Presheaf of Modular Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Presheaf of Modular Type (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.171.245',1),
(103,73,NULL,NULL,'2017-08-04 23:07:03','2017-08-04 23:07:03','2017-08-04 23:07:03',2,'# Local Character Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Geometry]], a **Local Character Theory** is a [[Covering]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Cyclic Hierarchy in Homotopy Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Local Character Theory satisfies standard Poincaré conditions in the context of [[Higher Smooth Triangle]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Quotient]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Isotropic Hypergraph Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Local Character Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Local Character Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.37.150',1),
(104,74,NULL,NULL,'2021-10-09 11:40:59','2021-10-09 11:40:59','2021-10-09 11:40:59',1,'# Abstract Monad of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Operator Algebras]], a **Abstract Monad of Schemes** is a [[Index]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Connected Link]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Abstract Monad of Schemes satisfies standard Monoidal conditions in the context of [[Weak Infinity-Category Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Field]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Smooth Invariant]] admits a canonical structure of a Abstract Monad of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Operator Algebras]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Abstract Monad of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.21.203',1),
(105,75,NULL,NULL,'2021-02-25 20:03:05','2021-02-25 20:03:05','2021-02-25 20:03:05',1,'# Graded Operator for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Higher Category Theory]], a **Graded Operator for Bundles** is a [[Connection]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Partition of Graded Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Graded Operator for Bundles satisfies standard Normal conditions in the context of [[Holomorphic Subalgebra Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Isomorphism]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Coalgebraic Relation in Abstract Algebra]] admits a canonical structure of a Graded Operator for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Higher Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Graded Operator for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.11.204',1),
(106,76,NULL,NULL,'2024-10-31 00:08:39','2024-10-31 00:08:39','2024-10-31 00:08:39',1,'# Derived Extension for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Quantum Field Theory]], a **Derived Extension for Bundles** is a [[Extension]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Toric Translation in Model Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Derived Extension for Bundles satisfies standard Asymptotic conditions in the context of [[Higher Tensor Multilinear]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Root]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Dual Symmetry Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Derived Extension for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Quantum Field Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Derived Extension for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.139.224',1),
(107,76,NULL,NULL,'2024-10-31 00:08:39','2024-12-18 05:11:25','2024-12-18 05:11:25',1,'# Derived Extension for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Quantum Field Theory]], a **Derived Extension for Bundles** is a [[Extension]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Toric Translation in Model Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Derived Extension for Bundles satisfies standard Asymptotic conditions in the context of [[Higher Tensor Multilinear]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Root]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Dual Symmetry Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Derived Extension for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Quantum Field Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Derived Extension for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.5.28',1),
(108,77,NULL,NULL,'2019-05-23 05:52:01','2019-05-23 05:52:01','2019-05-23 05:52:01',2,'# Categorical Semigroup Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Geometry]], a **Categorical Semigroup Theory** is a [[Colimit]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Pattern of Gauge Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Categorical Semigroup Theory satisfies standard Finite conditions in the context of [[Sheafified Extension of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Duality]].\n\n[[!include Differential Geometry Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Isometric Equation Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Categorical Semigroup Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Categorical Semigroup Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.100.51',1),
(109,78,NULL,NULL,'2018-05-01 05:12:48','2018-05-01 05:12:48','2018-05-01 05:12:48',2,'# Homological Chain\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Geometry]], a **Homological Chain** is a [[Variety]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Riemannian Domain in Functional Analysis]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Homological Chain satisfies standard Weak conditions in the context of [[Spectral Fraction on Higher Category Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Link]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Connected Volume of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Homological Chain.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Homological Chain*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.42.190',1),
(110,79,NULL,NULL,'2023-05-23 18:59:47','2023-05-23 18:59:47','2023-05-23 18:59:47',1,'# Pseudo Reduction for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Pseudo Reduction for Bundles** is a [[Image]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Degree of Cartesian Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Pseudo Reduction for Bundles satisfies standard Riemannian conditions in the context of [[Reduction of Fundamental Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Degree]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Nilpotent Ring Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Pseudo Reduction for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Pseudo Reduction for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.108.162',1),
(111,80,NULL,NULL,'2020-10-19 11:40:16','2020-10-19 11:40:16','2020-10-19 11:40:16',3,'# Quantum Coequalizer\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Quantum Coequalizer** is a [[Module]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Polygon of Tensor Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Quantum Coequalizer satisfies standard Equivariant conditions in the context of [[Coalgebraic Morphism on Algebraic Topology]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Translation]].\n\n[[!include Differential Topology Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Connected Link]] admits a canonical structure of a Quantum Coequalizer.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Quantum Coequalizer*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.128.72',1),
(112,80,NULL,NULL,'2020-10-19 11:40:16','2020-12-08 15:08:31','2020-12-08 15:08:31',3,'# Quantum Coequalizer\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Quantum Coequalizer** is a [[Module]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Polygon of Tensor Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Quantum Coequalizer satisfies standard Equivariant conditions in the context of [[Coalgebraic Morphism on Algebraic Topology]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Translation]].\n\n[[!include Differential Topology Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Connected Link]] admits a canonical structure of a Quantum Coequalizer.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Quantum Coequalizer*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.7.63',1),
(113,81,NULL,NULL,'2016-11-18 13:23:44','2016-11-18 13:23:44','2016-11-18 13:23:44',2,'# Smooth Cluster in Topology\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Operator Algebras]], a **Smooth Cluster in Topology** is a [[Algorithm]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Local Resonance]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Smooth Cluster in Topology satisfies standard Internal conditions in the context of [[Simplicial Tree of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Stability]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Homotopical Decomposition]] admits a canonical structure of a Smooth Cluster in Topology.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Operator Algebras]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Smooth Cluster in Topology*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.149.249',1),
(114,82,NULL,NULL,'2019-10-17 20:28:18','2019-10-17 20:28:18','2019-10-17 20:28:18',1,'# Local Decomposition Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Geometry]], a **Local Decomposition Theory** is a [[Cocycle]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Smooth Cluster in Topology]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Local Decomposition Theory satisfies standard Normal conditions in the context of [[Higher Topological Node]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Index]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cyclic Bundle on Commutative Algebra]] admits a canonical structure of a Local Decomposition Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Local Decomposition Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.129.23',1),
(115,82,NULL,NULL,'2019-10-17 20:28:18','2019-12-24 16:04:51','2019-12-24 16:04:51',1,'# Local Decomposition Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Geometry]], a **Local Decomposition Theory** is a [[Cocycle]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Smooth Cluster in Topology]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Local Decomposition Theory satisfies standard Normal conditions in the context of [[Higher Topological Node]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Index]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cyclic Bundle on Commutative Algebra]] admits a canonical structure of a Local Decomposition Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Local Decomposition Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.137.137',1),
(116,83,NULL,NULL,'2018-12-11 13:35:37','2018-12-11 13:35:37','2018-12-11 13:35:37',2,'# Simple Sheaf Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Number Theory]], a **Simple Sheaf Theory** is a [[Amalgamation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Solvable Radical of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Simple Sheaf Theory satisfies standard Universal conditions in the context of [[Riemannian Path of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Semigroup]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Invariant Wave in Model Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Simple Sheaf Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Number Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Simple Sheaf Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.32.69',1),
(117,83,NULL,NULL,'2018-12-11 13:35:37','2018-12-13 10:07:24','2018-12-13 10:07:24',1,'# Simple Sheaf Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Number Theory]], a **Simple Sheaf Theory** is a [[Amalgamation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Solvable Radical of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Simple Sheaf Theory satisfies standard Universal conditions in the context of [[Riemannian Path of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Semigroup]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Invariant Wave in Model Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Simple Sheaf Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Number Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Simple Sheaf Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.5.11',1),
(118,83,NULL,NULL,'2018-12-11 13:35:37','2019-03-12 23:51:07','2019-03-12 23:51:07',1,'# Simple Sheaf Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Number Theory]], a **Simple Sheaf Theory** is a [[Amalgamation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Solvable Radical of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Simple Sheaf Theory satisfies standard Universal conditions in the context of [[Riemannian Path of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Semigroup]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Invariant Wave in Model Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Simple Sheaf Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Number Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Simple Sheaf Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.91.116',1),
(119,84,NULL,NULL,'2024-11-28 21:34:55','2024-11-28 21:34:55','2024-11-28 21:34:55',1,'# Monoidal Structure in Proof Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Monoidal Structure in Proof Theory** is a [[Lie Algebra]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Perfect Symmetry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Monoidal Structure in Proof Theory satisfies standard Weighted conditions in the context of [[Unitary Image of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Infinity-Category]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Homological Chain]] admits a canonical structure of a Monoidal Structure in Proof Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Monoidal Structure in Proof Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.186.38',1),
(120,85,NULL,NULL,'2015-09-19 04:13:34','2015-09-19 04:13:34','2015-09-19 04:13:34',2,'# Fundamental Filter on Topology\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Fundamental Filter on Topology** is a [[Algorithm]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Invariant Cluster]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Fundamental Filter on Topology satisfies standard Homotopical conditions in the context of [[Topological Deformation on Representation Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Presheaf]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Semisimple Manifold of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Fundamental Filter on Topology.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Fundamental Filter on Topology*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.119.239',1),
(121,86,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-04 09:54:16','2026-08-04 09:54:16','2026-08-04 09:54:16',1,'# Formal Hierarchy on Noncommutative Geometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Operator Algebras]], a **Formal Hierarchy on Noncommutative Geometry** is a [[Relation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Cartesian Covering for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Formal Hierarchy on Noncommutative Geometry satisfies standard Noncommutative conditions in the context of [[Pseudo Cocycle on Geometric Group Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Product]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Monoidal Homomorphism for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Formal Hierarchy on Noncommutative Geometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Operator Algebras]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Formal Hierarchy on Noncommutative Geometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.82.143',1),
(122,87,NULL,NULL,'2022-06-18 14:09:59','2022-06-18 14:09:59','2022-06-18 14:09:59',2,'# Stack of Topological Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Stack of Topological Type** is a [[Quotient]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Orthogonal Invariant of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Stack of Topological Type satisfies standard Asymptotic conditions in the context of [[Coequalizer of Hyperbolic Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Dimension]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Radical of Coalgebraic Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Stack of Topological Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Stack of Topological Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.144.207',1),
(123,88,NULL,NULL,'2022-11-22 19:07:55','2022-11-22 19:07:55','2022-11-22 19:07:55',2,'# Homological Triangulation for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Number Theory]], a **Homological Triangulation for Bundles** is a [[Structure]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Degree of Formal Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Homological Triangulation for Bundles satisfies standard Topological conditions in the context of [[Pseudo Reduction for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Presheaf]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Noncommutative Duality]] admits a canonical structure of a Homological Triangulation for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Number Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Homological Triangulation for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.134.74',1),
(124,89,NULL,NULL,'2018-08-06 22:43:29','2018-08-06 22:43:29','2018-08-06 22:43:29',3,'# Orthogonal Spectral Sequence\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Orthogonal Spectral Sequence** is a [[Hull]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Universal Cluster Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Orthogonal Spectral Sequence satisfies standard Semisimple conditions in the context of [[Cyclic Hierarchy in Homotopy Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Amalgamation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Lagrangian Operad Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Orthogonal Spectral Sequence.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Orthogonal Spectral Sequence*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.61.97',1),
(125,89,NULL,NULL,'2018-08-06 22:43:29','2018-10-01 14:43:43','2018-10-01 14:43:43',2,'# Orthogonal Spectral Sequence\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Orthogonal Spectral Sequence** is a [[Hull]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Universal Cluster Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Orthogonal Spectral Sequence satisfies standard Semisimple conditions in the context of [[Cyclic Hierarchy in Homotopy Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Amalgamation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Lagrangian Operad Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Orthogonal Spectral Sequence.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Orthogonal Spectral Sequence*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.59.252',1),
(126,89,NULL,NULL,'2018-08-06 22:43:29','2018-08-07 23:39:28','2018-08-07 23:39:28',1,'# Orthogonal Spectral Sequence\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Orthogonal Spectral Sequence** is a [[Hull]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Universal Cluster Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Orthogonal Spectral Sequence satisfies standard Semisimple conditions in the context of [[Cyclic Hierarchy in Homotopy Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Amalgamation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Lagrangian Operad Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Orthogonal Spectral Sequence.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Orthogonal Spectral Sequence*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.242.222',1),
(127,90,NULL,NULL,'2024-05-06 20:23:04','2024-05-06 20:23:04','2024-05-06 20:23:04',1,'# Coalgebraic Relation in Abstract Algebra\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Coalgebraic Relation in Abstract Algebra** is a [[Bimodule]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Connected Class for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Coalgebraic Relation in Abstract Algebra satisfies standard Lax conditions in the context of [[Cell of Fundamental Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Module]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Rational Link in Differential Topology]] admits a canonical structure of a Coalgebraic Relation in Abstract Algebra.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Coalgebraic Relation in Abstract Algebra*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.131.186',1),
(128,91,NULL,NULL,'2020-03-17 11:44:53','2020-03-17 11:44:53','2020-03-17 11:44:53',2,'# Higher Cofibrant Lemma\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Higher Cofibrant Lemma** is a [[Extension]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Graded Topos]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Cofibrant Lemma satisfies standard Algebraic conditions in the context of [[Reduction of Fundamental Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Deformation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Symplectic Domain of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Cofibrant Lemma.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Cofibrant Lemma*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.162.143',1),
(129,91,NULL,NULL,'2020-03-17 11:44:53','2020-06-18 04:55:18','2020-06-18 04:55:18',1,'# Higher Cofibrant Lemma\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Higher Cofibrant Lemma** is a [[Extension]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Graded Topos]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Cofibrant Lemma satisfies standard Algebraic conditions in the context of [[Reduction of Fundamental Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Deformation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Symplectic Domain of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Cofibrant Lemma.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Cofibrant Lemma*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.72.246',1),
(130,92,NULL,NULL,'2015-03-05 09:25:23','2015-03-05 09:25:23','2015-03-05 09:25:23',2,'# Isotropic Hypergraph Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Isotropic Hypergraph Theory** is a [[Equivalence]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Bimodular Submanifold for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Isotropic Hypergraph Theory satisfies standard Topological conditions in the context of [[Semisimple Manifold of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Surface]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Free Framework in Differential Topology]] admits a canonical structure of a Isotropic Hypergraph Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Isotropic Hypergraph Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.21.20',1),
(131,93,NULL,NULL,'2022-09-22 19:51:28','2022-09-22 19:51:28','2022-09-22 19:51:28',3,'# Relative Target for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Quantum Field Theory]], a **Relative Target for Bundles** is a [[Weight]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Fundamental Knot for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Relative Target for Bundles satisfies standard Invariant conditions in the context of [[Higher Deformed Derivation]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Representation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Contraction of Pseudo Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Relative Target for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Quantum Field Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Relative Target for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.194.247',1),
(132,93,NULL,NULL,'2022-09-22 19:51:28','2022-10-16 18:56:27','2022-10-16 18:56:27',2,'# Relative Target for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Quantum Field Theory]], a **Relative Target for Bundles** is a [[Weight]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Fundamental Knot for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Relative Target for Bundles satisfies standard Invariant conditions in the context of [[Higher Deformed Derivation]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Representation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Contraction of Pseudo Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Relative Target for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Quantum Field Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Relative Target for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.112.72',1),
(133,93,NULL,NULL,'2022-09-22 19:51:28','2022-12-05 23:00:44','2022-12-05 23:00:44',2,'# Relative Target for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Quantum Field Theory]], a **Relative Target for Bundles** is a [[Weight]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Fundamental Knot for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Relative Target for Bundles satisfies standard Invariant conditions in the context of [[Higher Deformed Derivation]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Representation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Contraction of Pseudo Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Relative Target for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Quantum Field Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Relative Target for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.191.196',1),
(134,94,NULL,NULL,'2018-10-31 23:38:13','2018-10-31 23:38:13','2018-10-31 23:38:13',2,'# Topological Restriction\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Topological Restriction** is a [[Tree]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Holomorphic Graph]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Topological Restriction satisfies standard Local conditions in the context of [[Graded Limit Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Unit]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Cohomological Torsion]] admits a canonical structure of a Topological Restriction.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Topological Restriction*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.87.140',1),
(135,94,NULL,NULL,'2018-10-31 23:38:13','2019-01-02 09:29:05','2019-01-02 09:29:05',1,'# Topological Restriction\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Topological Restriction** is a [[Tree]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Holomorphic Graph]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Topological Restriction satisfies standard Local conditions in the context of [[Graded Limit Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Unit]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Cohomological Torsion]] admits a canonical structure of a Topological Restriction.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Topological Restriction*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.30.48',1),
(136,94,NULL,NULL,'2018-10-31 23:38:13','2019-04-22 02:14:45','2019-04-22 02:14:45',1,'# Topological Restriction\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Topological Restriction** is a [[Tree]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Holomorphic Graph]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Topological Restriction satisfies standard Local conditions in the context of [[Graded Limit Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Unit]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Cohomological Torsion]] admits a canonical structure of a Topological Restriction.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Topological Restriction*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.67.183',1),
(137,95,NULL,NULL,'2024-12-16 12:31:35','2024-12-16 12:31:35','2024-12-16 12:31:35',1,'# Cyclic Hierarchy in Homotopy Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Lie Theory]], a **Cyclic Hierarchy in Homotopy Theory** is a [[System]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Quantum Coequalizer]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Cyclic Hierarchy in Homotopy Theory satisfies standard Differential conditions in the context of [[Quantum Gauging in Functional Analysis]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Conjecture]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Coalgebraic Patch Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Cyclic Hierarchy in Homotopy Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Lie Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Cyclic Hierarchy in Homotopy Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.95.18',1),
(138,95,NULL,NULL,'2024-12-16 12:31:35','2025-06-14 16:31:10','2025-06-14 16:31:10',2,'# Cyclic Hierarchy in Homotopy Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Lie Theory]], a **Cyclic Hierarchy in Homotopy Theory** is a [[System]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Quantum Coequalizer]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Cyclic Hierarchy in Homotopy Theory satisfies standard Differential conditions in the context of [[Quantum Gauging in Functional Analysis]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Conjecture]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Coalgebraic Patch Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Cyclic Hierarchy in Homotopy Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Lie Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Cyclic Hierarchy in Homotopy Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.150.253',1),
(139,95,NULL,NULL,'2024-12-16 12:31:35','2025-03-30 04:54:51','2025-03-30 04:54:51',2,'# Cyclic Hierarchy in Homotopy Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Lie Theory]], a **Cyclic Hierarchy in Homotopy Theory** is a [[System]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Quantum Coequalizer]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Cyclic Hierarchy in Homotopy Theory satisfies standard Differential conditions in the context of [[Quantum Gauging in Functional Analysis]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Conjecture]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Coalgebraic Patch Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Cyclic Hierarchy in Homotopy Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Lie Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Cyclic Hierarchy in Homotopy Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.12.62',1),
(140,96,NULL,NULL,'2021-07-14 20:31:37','2021-07-14 20:31:37','2021-07-14 20:31:37',3,'# Riemannian Domain in Functional Analysis\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Riemannian Domain in Functional Analysis** is a [[Suspension]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Weighted Immersion in Lie Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Riemannian Domain in Functional Analysis satisfies standard Lagrangian conditions in the context of [[Fibrant Stratification on Arithmetic Geometry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Functor]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Normal Ratio on Number Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Riemannian Domain in Functional Analysis.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Riemannian Domain in Functional Analysis*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.50.134',1),
(141,96,NULL,NULL,'2021-07-14 20:31:37','2021-12-27 13:55:15','2021-12-27 13:55:15',3,'# Riemannian Domain in Functional Analysis\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Riemannian Domain in Functional Analysis** is a [[Suspension]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Weighted Immersion in Lie Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Riemannian Domain in Functional Analysis satisfies standard Lagrangian conditions in the context of [[Fibrant Stratification on Arithmetic Geometry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Functor]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Normal Ratio on Number Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Riemannian Domain in Functional Analysis.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Riemannian Domain in Functional Analysis*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.249.104',1),
(142,96,NULL,NULL,'2021-07-14 20:31:37','2021-07-28 21:36:04','2021-07-28 21:36:04',3,'# Riemannian Domain in Functional Analysis\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Riemannian Domain in Functional Analysis** is a [[Suspension]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Weighted Immersion in Lie Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Riemannian Domain in Functional Analysis satisfies standard Lagrangian conditions in the context of [[Fibrant Stratification on Arithmetic Geometry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Functor]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Normal Ratio on Number Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Riemannian Domain in Functional Analysis.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Riemannian Domain in Functional Analysis*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.38.254',1),
(143,97,NULL,NULL,'2020-10-27 08:07:57','2020-10-27 08:07:57','2020-10-27 08:07:57',3,'# Higher Solvable Flow\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Constructive Mathematics]], a **Higher Solvable Flow** is a [[Envelope]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Dual Point]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Solvable Flow satisfies standard Complete conditions in the context of [[Higher Tensor Multilinear]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Stratification]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Partition of Graded Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Solvable Flow.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Constructive Mathematics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Higher Solvable Flow*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.137.160',1),
(144,98,NULL,NULL,'2014-06-15 09:24:08','2014-06-15 09:24:08','2014-06-15 09:24:08',2,'# Exact Surface on Higher Category Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Representation Theory]], a **Exact Surface on Higher Category Theory** is a [[Lattice]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Infinite Lemma]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Exact Surface on Higher Category Theory satisfies standard Stable conditions in the context of [[Higher Riemannian Algorithm]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Quantum Group]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Uniform Matrix]] admits a canonical structure of a Exact Surface on Higher Category Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Representation Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Exact Surface on Higher Category Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.175.14',1),
(145,99,NULL,NULL,'2016-07-05 12:50:52','2016-07-05 12:50:52','2016-07-05 12:50:52',2,'# Higher Pseudo Principal Bundle\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Higher Pseudo Principal Bundle** is a [[Bimodule]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Theory of Proper Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Pseudo Principal Bundle satisfies standard Fundamental conditions in the context of [[Topological Restriction]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Determinant]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Smooth Phase of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Pseudo Principal Bundle.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Pseudo Principal Bundle*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Higher Pseudo Principal Bundle (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.124.65',1),
(146,100,NULL,NULL,'2024-01-21 04:24:48','2024-01-21 04:24:48','2024-01-21 04:24:48',2,'# Compact Spinor\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Compact Spinor** is a [[Chain]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Spectral Node for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Compact Spinor satisfies standard Noncommutative conditions in the context of [[Lattice of Fibrant Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Chain]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Homotopical Decomposition]] admits a canonical structure of a Compact Spinor.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Compact Spinor*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.120.163',1),
(147,100,NULL,NULL,'2024-01-21 04:24:48','2024-06-14 08:57:39','2024-06-14 08:57:39',1,'# Compact Spinor\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Compact Spinor** is a [[Chain]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Spectral Node for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Compact Spinor satisfies standard Noncommutative conditions in the context of [[Lattice of Fibrant Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Chain]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Homotopical Decomposition]] admits a canonical structure of a Compact Spinor.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Compact Spinor*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.104.252',1),
(148,101,NULL,NULL,'2017-01-01 23:19:49','2017-01-01 23:19:49','2017-01-01 23:19:49',1,'# Higher Asymptotic Adjunction\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Category Theory]], a **Higher Asymptotic Adjunction** is a [[Automaton]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Continuous Automorphism for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Asymptotic Adjunction satisfies standard Quasi conditions in the context of [[Gauge Class of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Product]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Manifold of Relative Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Asymptotic Adjunction.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Asymptotic Adjunction*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.110.52',1),
(149,101,NULL,NULL,'2017-01-01 23:19:49','2017-03-31 15:34:53','2017-03-31 15:34:53',3,'# Higher Asymptotic Adjunction\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Category Theory]], a **Higher Asymptotic Adjunction** is a [[Automaton]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Continuous Automorphism for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Asymptotic Adjunction satisfies standard Quasi conditions in the context of [[Gauge Class of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Product]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Manifold of Relative Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Asymptotic Adjunction.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Asymptotic Adjunction*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.15.32',1),
(150,101,NULL,NULL,'2017-01-01 23:19:49','2017-02-11 11:27:48','2017-02-11 11:27:48',1,'# Higher Asymptotic Adjunction\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Category Theory]], a **Higher Asymptotic Adjunction** is a [[Automaton]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Continuous Automorphism for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Asymptotic Adjunction satisfies standard Quasi conditions in the context of [[Gauge Class of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Product]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Manifold of Relative Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Asymptotic Adjunction.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Asymptotic Adjunction*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.48.229',1),
(151,102,NULL,NULL,'2019-05-03 11:22:51','2019-05-03 11:22:51','2019-05-03 11:22:51',3,'# Hierarchy of Super Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Higher Category Theory]], a **Hierarchy of Super Type** is a [[Ideal]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Topological Subobject on Noncommutative Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Hierarchy of Super Type satisfies standard Relative conditions in the context of [[Sheafified Model]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Orientation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Complete Fiber]] admits a canonical structure of a Hierarchy of Super Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Higher Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Hierarchy of Super Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Hierarchy of Super Type (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.1.110',1),
(152,103,NULL,NULL,'2024-05-09 05:09:07','2024-05-09 05:09:07','2024-05-09 05:09:07',2,'# Riemannian Target for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Complex Analysis]], a **Riemannian Target for Bundles** is a [[Signature]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Dynamical Presheaf on Abstract Algebra]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Riemannian Target for Bundles satisfies standard Toric conditions in the context of [[Simplicial Ring]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Variety]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Asymptotic Intersection of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Riemannian Target for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Complex Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Riemannian Target for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.237.209',1),
(153,104,NULL,NULL,'2025-03-20 01:21:55','2025-03-20 01:21:55','2025-03-20 01:21:55',1,'# Connected Codimension\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Connected Codimension** is a [[Graph]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Universal Cluster Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Connected Codimension satisfies standard Riemannian conditions in the context of [[Cell of Fundamental Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Derivation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Monoidal Relation of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Connected Codimension.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Connected Codimension*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.151.61',1),
(154,105,NULL,NULL,'2015-08-09 06:48:55','2015-08-09 06:48:55','2015-08-09 06:48:55',3,'# Bivector of Relative Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Bivector of Relative Type** is a [[Hierarchy]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Rational Curvature of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Bivector of Relative Type satisfies standard Normal conditions in the context of [[Class of Cyclic Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Transformation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Conformal Wave]] admits a canonical structure of a Bivector of Relative Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Bivector of Relative Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.110.142',1),
(155,105,NULL,NULL,'2015-08-09 06:48:55','2015-12-13 05:03:05','2015-12-13 05:03:05',2,'# Bivector of Relative Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Bivector of Relative Type** is a [[Hierarchy]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Rational Curvature of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Bivector of Relative Type satisfies standard Normal conditions in the context of [[Class of Cyclic Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Transformation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Conformal Wave]] admits a canonical structure of a Bivector of Relative Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Bivector of Relative Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.114.1',1),
(156,106,NULL,NULL,'2019-08-15 05:11:16','2019-08-15 05:11:16','2019-08-15 05:11:16',1,'# Degree of Noncommutative Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Geometry]], a **Degree of Noncommutative Type** is a [[Partition]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Unitary Network]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Degree of Noncommutative Type satisfies standard Coalgebraic conditions in the context of [[Nilpotent Curvature Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Surface]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Weighted Fiber in Complex Analysis]] admits a canonical structure of a Degree of Noncommutative Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Degree of Noncommutative Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.22.122',1),
(157,107,NULL,NULL,'2016-08-18 13:17:52','2016-08-18 13:17:52','2016-08-18 13:17:52',2,'# Higher Isometric Boundary\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Complex Analysis]], a **Higher Isometric Boundary** is a [[Splitting]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Normal Gauging for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Isometric Boundary satisfies standard Quasi conditions in the context of [[Presheaf of Modular Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Functor]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Simplicial Formula Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Isometric Boundary.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Complex Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Isometric Boundary*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.181.99',1),
(158,108,NULL,NULL,'2014-04-23 02:02:21','2014-04-23 02:02:21','2014-04-23 02:02:21',2,'# Twisted Grading Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Twisted Grading Theory** is a [[Principal Bundle]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Connected Cone for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Twisted Grading Theory satisfies standard Sheafified conditions in the context of [[Higher Perfect Polytope]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Dimension]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Twisted Matrix in Model Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Twisted Grading Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Twisted Grading Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.99.133',1),
(159,108,NULL,NULL,'2014-04-23 02:02:21','2014-07-04 18:27:16','2014-07-04 18:27:16',1,'# Twisted Grading Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Twisted Grading Theory** is a [[Principal Bundle]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Connected Cone for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Twisted Grading Theory satisfies standard Sheafified conditions in the context of [[Higher Perfect Polytope]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Dimension]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Twisted Matrix in Model Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Twisted Grading Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Twisted Grading Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.38.106',1),
(160,109,NULL,NULL,'2014-02-01 03:51:10','2014-02-01 03:51:10','2014-02-01 03:51:10',2,'# Invariant Wave in Model Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Proof Theory]], a **Invariant Wave in Model Theory** is a [[Stratification]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Grading of Asymptotic Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Invariant Wave in Model Theory satisfies standard Algebraic conditions in the context of [[Abstract Skeleton of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Sequence]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Projective Retract of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Invariant Wave in Model Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Proof Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Invariant Wave in Model Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Invariant Wave in Model Theory (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.62.29',1),
(161,109,NULL,NULL,'2014-02-01 03:51:10','2014-06-26 18:10:23','2014-06-26 18:10:23',3,'# Invariant Wave in Model Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Proof Theory]], a **Invariant Wave in Model Theory** is a [[Stratification]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Grading of Asymptotic Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Invariant Wave in Model Theory satisfies standard Algebraic conditions in the context of [[Abstract Skeleton of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Sequence]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Projective Retract of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Invariant Wave in Model Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Proof Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Invariant Wave in Model Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Invariant Wave in Model Theory (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.127.223',1),
(162,110,NULL,NULL,'2023-03-25 06:23:43','2023-03-25 06:23:43','2023-03-25 06:23:43',1,'# Stable Triangle of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Geometry]], a **Stable Triangle of Schemes** is a [[Orbit]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Monoidal Homomorphism for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Stable Triangle of Schemes satisfies standard Stable conditions in the context of [[Perfect Restriction on Lie Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Class]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Toric Relation of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Stable Triangle of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Stable Triangle of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.241.77',1),
(163,110,NULL,NULL,'2023-03-25 06:23:43','2023-03-28 18:11:41','2023-03-28 18:11:41',1,'# Stable Triangle of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Geometry]], a **Stable Triangle of Schemes** is a [[Orbit]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Monoidal Homomorphism for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Stable Triangle of Schemes satisfies standard Stable conditions in the context of [[Perfect Restriction on Lie Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Class]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Toric Relation of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Stable Triangle of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Stable Triangle of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.22.18',1),
(164,111,NULL,NULL,'2016-12-31 11:11:34','2016-12-31 11:11:34','2016-12-31 11:11:34',3,'# Differential Equation Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Constructive Mathematics]], a **Differential Equation Theory** is a [[Image]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Simple Orbit]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Differential Equation Theory satisfies standard Differential conditions in the context of [[Higher Noncommutative Duality]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Operad]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Perfect Symmetry]] admits a canonical structure of a Differential Equation Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Constructive Mathematics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Differential Equation Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.16.177',1),
(165,111,NULL,NULL,'2016-12-31 11:11:34','2017-06-28 12:43:23','2017-06-28 12:43:23',1,'# Differential Equation Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Constructive Mathematics]], a **Differential Equation Theory** is a [[Image]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Simple Orbit]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Differential Equation Theory satisfies standard Differential conditions in the context of [[Higher Noncommutative Duality]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Operad]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Perfect Symmetry]] admits a canonical structure of a Differential Equation Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Constructive Mathematics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Differential Equation Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.199.233',1),
(166,112,NULL,NULL,'2026-04-11 11:22:48','2026-04-11 11:22:48','2026-04-11 11:22:48',2,'# Dual Transformation\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Lie Theory]], a **Dual Transformation** is a [[Geometry]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Simplicial Invariant Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Dual Transformation satisfies standard Compact conditions in the context of [[Derived Discriminant]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Contraction]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Cohomological Point]] admits a canonical structure of a Dual Transformation.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Lie Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Dual Transformation*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.30.73',1),
(167,113,NULL,NULL,'2025-08-03 07:59:55','2025-08-03 07:59:55','2025-08-03 07:59:55',3,'# Topological Transformation of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Arithmetic Geometry]], a **Topological Transformation of Schemes** is a [[State]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Unitary Degree]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Topological Transformation of Schemes satisfies standard Dynamical conditions in the context of [[Rational Radical in Abstract Algebra]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Automaton]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Local Decomposition Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Topological Transformation of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Arithmetic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Topological Transformation of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.153.144',1),
(168,114,NULL,NULL,'2014-04-01 06:28:29','2014-04-01 06:28:29','2014-04-01 06:28:29',1,'# Higher Differential System\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Operator Algebras]], a **Higher Differential System** is a [[Group]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Super Extension Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Differential System satisfies standard Simplicial conditions in the context of [[Smooth Phase of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Bundle]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Differential Matrix on Commutative Algebra]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Differential System.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Operator Algebras]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Differential System*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Higher Differential System (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.139.109',1),
(169,115,NULL,NULL,'2014-02-01 22:48:16','2014-02-01 22:48:16','2014-02-01 22:48:16',3,'# Annihilating Point of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Homotopy Theory]], a **Annihilating Point of Schemes** is a [[Sphere]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Parabolic Semigroup of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Annihilating Point of Schemes satisfies standard Operadic conditions in the context of [[Invariant Operator of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Character]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Node of Abstract Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Annihilating Point of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Homotopy Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Annihilating Point of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.33.34',1),
(170,116,NULL,NULL,'2016-07-29 07:02:18','2016-07-29 07:02:18','2016-07-29 07:02:18',3,'# Orthogonal Lemma Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Abstract Algebra]], a **Orthogonal Lemma Theory** is a [[Realization]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Smooth Phase of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Orthogonal Lemma Theory satisfies standard Compact conditions in the context of [[Exact Operad]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Hull]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Affine Space for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Orthogonal Lemma Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Abstract Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Orthogonal Lemma Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.149.48',1),
(171,117,NULL,NULL,'2025-06-25 02:55:49','2025-06-25 02:55:49','2025-06-25 02:55:49',2,'# Filtered Automaton Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Abstract Algebra]], a **Filtered Automaton Theory** is a [[Cocycle]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Normal Site Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Filtered Automaton Theory satisfies standard Enriched conditions in the context of [[Higher Perfect Polytope]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Singularity]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Invariant Cluster]] admits a canonical structure of a Filtered Automaton Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Abstract Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Filtered Automaton Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n[[!redirects Filtered Automaton Theory (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.127.132',1),
(172,118,NULL,NULL,'2023-05-20 04:45:17','2023-05-20 04:45:17','2023-05-20 04:45:17',3,'# Local Isomorphism Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Category Theory]], a **Local Isomorphism Theory** is a [[Variety]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Stack of Topological Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Local Isomorphism Theory satisfies standard Connected conditions in the context of [[Infinite Quotient of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Manifold]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Finite Bimodule]] admits a canonical structure of a Local Isomorphism Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Local Isomorphism Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Local Isomorphism Theory (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.216.247',1),
(173,119,NULL,NULL,'2023-12-25 00:44:32','2023-12-25 00:44:32','2023-12-25 00:44:32',1,'# Geometric Lemma in Higher Category Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Operator Algebras]], a **Geometric Lemma in Higher Category Theory** is a [[Graph]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Relative Phase on Representation Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Geometric Lemma in Higher Category Theory satisfies standard Infinite conditions in the context of [[Uniform Matrix]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Covering]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Cyclic Grading]] admits a canonical structure of a Geometric Lemma in Higher Category Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Operator Algebras]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Geometric Lemma in Higher Category Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n[[!redirects Geometric Lemma in Higher Category Theory (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.76.153',1),
(174,120,NULL,NULL,'2022-06-02 21:09:11','2022-06-02 21:09:11','2022-06-02 21:09:11',1,'# Toric Relation of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Arithmetic Geometry]], a **Toric Relation of Schemes** is a [[Decomposition]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Homological Spectrum of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Toric Relation of Schemes satisfies standard Cohomological conditions in the context of [[Parabolic Patch on Number Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Kernel]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Manifold of Relative Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Toric Relation of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Arithmetic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Toric Relation of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.124.34',1),
(175,121,NULL,NULL,'2016-05-29 00:22:27','2016-05-29 00:22:27','2016-05-29 00:22:27',3,'# Connected Groupoid\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Complex Analysis]], a **Connected Groupoid** is a [[Identity]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Modular Cell for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Connected Groupoid satisfies standard Cofibrant conditions in the context of [[Higher Series for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Fraction]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Orthogonal Index]] admits a canonical structure of a Connected Groupoid.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Complex Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Connected Groupoid*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.10.123',1),
(176,121,NULL,NULL,'2016-05-29 00:22:27','2016-09-18 23:34:49','2016-09-18 23:34:49',1,'# Connected Groupoid\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Complex Analysis]], a **Connected Groupoid** is a [[Identity]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Modular Cell for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Connected Groupoid satisfies standard Cofibrant conditions in the context of [[Higher Series for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Fraction]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Orthogonal Index]] admits a canonical structure of a Connected Groupoid.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Complex Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Connected Groupoid*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.207.94',1),
(177,121,NULL,NULL,'2016-05-29 00:22:27','2016-08-04 07:16:15','2016-08-04 07:16:15',3,'# Connected Groupoid\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Complex Analysis]], a **Connected Groupoid** is a [[Identity]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Modular Cell for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Connected Groupoid satisfies standard Cofibrant conditions in the context of [[Higher Series for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Fraction]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Orthogonal Index]] admits a canonical structure of a Connected Groupoid.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Complex Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Connected Groupoid*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.35.236',1),
(178,121,NULL,NULL,'2016-05-29 00:22:27','2016-06-13 07:03:20','2016-06-13 07:03:20',3,'# Connected Groupoid\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Complex Analysis]], a **Connected Groupoid** is a [[Identity]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Modular Cell for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Connected Groupoid satisfies standard Cofibrant conditions in the context of [[Higher Series for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Fraction]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Orthogonal Index]] admits a canonical structure of a Connected Groupoid.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Complex Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Connected Groupoid*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.96.16',1),
(179,122,NULL,NULL,'2014-06-26 20:17:49','2014-06-26 20:17:49','2014-06-26 20:17:49',3,'# Higher Annihilating Contraction\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Higher Annihilating Contraction** is a [[Bimodule]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Super Extension Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Annihilating Contraction satisfies standard Stable conditions in the context of [[Complete System of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Object]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Bimodular Filter for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Annihilating Contraction.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Higher Annihilating Contraction*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.227.96',1),
(180,123,NULL,NULL,'2014-01-02 16:36:02','2014-01-02 16:36:02','2014-01-02 16:36:02',3,'# Internal Torsion of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Internal Torsion of Schemes** is a [[Extension]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Parabolic Unit for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Internal Torsion of Schemes satisfies standard Connected conditions in the context of [[Higher Discrete Group]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Model]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Connected Link]] admits a canonical structure of a Internal Torsion of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Internal Torsion of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Internal Torsion of Schemes (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.217.152',1),
(181,123,NULL,NULL,'2014-01-02 16:36:02','2014-06-03 16:48:23','2014-06-03 16:48:23',1,'# Internal Torsion of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Internal Torsion of Schemes** is a [[Extension]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Parabolic Unit for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Internal Torsion of Schemes satisfies standard Connected conditions in the context of [[Higher Discrete Group]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Model]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Connected Link]] admits a canonical structure of a Internal Torsion of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Internal Torsion of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Internal Torsion of Schemes (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.16.194',1),
(182,124,NULL,NULL,'2016-12-14 05:48:27','2016-12-14 05:48:27','2016-12-14 05:48:27',2,'# Solvable Radical of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Solvable Radical of Schemes** is a [[Degree]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Parabolic Patch on Number Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Solvable Radical of Schemes satisfies standard Simplicial conditions in the context of [[Orthogonal Lemma Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Orientation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Riemannian Path of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Solvable Radical of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Solvable Radical of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.163.250',1),
(183,125,NULL,NULL,'2017-12-31 07:12:46','2017-12-31 07:12:46','2017-12-31 07:12:46',2,'# Sheafified Extension of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Sheafified Extension of Schemes** is a [[Resonance]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Solvable Spinor in Mathematical Physics]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Sheafified Extension of Schemes satisfies standard Dual conditions in the context of [[Manifold of Relative Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Network]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Modular Suspension on Arithmetic Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Sheafified Extension of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Sheafified Extension of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Sheafified Extension of Schemes (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.120.210',1),
(184,126,NULL,NULL,'2022-01-16 09:49:30','2022-01-16 09:49:30','2022-01-16 09:49:30',1,'# Universal Orientation\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Number Theory]], a **Universal Orientation** is a [[Algebra]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Simplicial Formula Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Universal Orientation satisfies standard Categorical conditions in the context of [[Parabolic Vector for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Fraction]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Connected Class for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Universal Orientation.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Number Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Universal Orientation*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.225.224',1),
(185,127,NULL,NULL,'2017-06-01 12:58:45','2017-06-01 12:58:45','2017-06-01 12:58:45',3,'# Isometric Order Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Isometric Order Theory** is a [[Vector]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Weak Symmetry Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Isometric Order Theory satisfies standard Tensor conditions in the context of [[Gauge Kernel Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Polygon]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Unitary Degree]] admits a canonical structure of a Isometric Order Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Isometric Order Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.246.94',1),
(186,128,NULL,NULL,'2015-11-20 18:13:36','2015-11-20 18:13:36','2015-11-20 18:13:36',1,'# Stable Integral on Topology\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Stable Integral on Topology** is a [[Link]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Regular Path Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Stable Integral on Topology satisfies standard Complete conditions in the context of [[Topological Connection on Mathematical Physics]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Class]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Affine Translation in Functional Analysis]] admits a canonical structure of a Stable Integral on Topology.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Stable Integral on Topology*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.20.217',1),
(187,129,NULL,NULL,'2019-02-10 11:48:49','2019-02-10 11:48:49','2019-02-10 11:48:49',3,'# Weighted Immersion in Lie Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Weighted Immersion in Lie Theory** is a [[Identity]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Spectral Sheaf in Lie Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Weighted Immersion in Lie Theory satisfies standard Dynamical conditions in the context of [[Projective Retract of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Rotation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Symplectic Domain of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Weighted Immersion in Lie Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Weighted Immersion in Lie Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.110.4',1),
(188,130,NULL,NULL,'2016-02-29 13:28:43','2016-02-29 13:28:43','2016-02-29 13:28:43',2,'# Higher Topological Node\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Higher Topological Node** is a [[Presheaf]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Sheafified Module Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Topological Node satisfies standard Universal conditions in the context of [[Bimodular Kernel]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Endomorphism]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cell of Fundamental Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Topological Node.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Topological Node*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Higher Topological Node (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.182.39',1),
(189,131,NULL,NULL,'2024-08-06 02:34:18','2024-08-06 02:34:18','2024-08-06 02:34:18',1,'# Unitary Image of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Number Theory]], a **Unitary Image of Schemes** is a [[Induction]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Connected Volume of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Unitary Image of Schemes satisfies standard Topological conditions in the context of [[Fibrant Representation of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Model]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Strict Rank]] admits a canonical structure of a Unitary Image of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Number Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Unitary Image of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.63.171',1),
(190,132,NULL,NULL,'2019-02-16 17:02:56','2019-02-16 17:02:56','2019-02-16 17:02:56',3,'# Operad of Enriched Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Operad of Enriched Type** is a [[Order]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Monoidal Potential]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Operad of Enriched Type satisfies standard Symplectic conditions in the context of [[Geometric Lemma in Higher Category Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Subalgebra]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Noncommutative Duality]] admits a canonical structure of a Operad of Enriched Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Operad of Enriched Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.52.165',1),
(191,133,NULL,NULL,'2016-07-15 09:09:45','2016-07-15 09:09:45','2016-07-15 09:09:45',1,'# Toric Triangle of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Toric Triangle of Schemes** is a [[Stability]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Isometric Subobject of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Toric Triangle of Schemes satisfies standard Noncommutative conditions in the context of [[Higher Simple Orbit]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Set]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Internal Torsion on Higher Category Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Toric Triangle of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Toric Triangle of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.192.108',1),
(192,133,NULL,NULL,'2016-07-15 09:09:45','2016-12-03 19:33:17','2016-12-03 19:33:17',3,'# Toric Triangle of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Toric Triangle of Schemes** is a [[Stability]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Isometric Subobject of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Toric Triangle of Schemes satisfies standard Noncommutative conditions in the context of [[Higher Simple Orbit]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Set]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Internal Torsion on Higher Category Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Toric Triangle of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Toric Triangle of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.54.19',1),
(193,134,NULL,NULL,'2014-08-04 02:17:36','2014-08-04 02:17:36','2014-08-04 02:17:36',1,'# Higher Local Resonance\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Higher Local Resonance** is a [[Subobject]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Free Field]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Local Resonance satisfies standard Internal conditions in the context of [[Conformal Lemma Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Kernel]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Invariant Cluster]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Local Resonance.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Local Resonance*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.4.64',1),
(194,135,NULL,NULL,'2021-06-21 21:35:47','2021-06-21 21:35:47','2021-06-21 21:35:47',2,'# Higher Graded Flow\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Quantum Field Theory]], a **Higher Graded Flow** is a [[Model]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Noncommutative Series Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Graded Flow satisfies standard Equivariant conditions in the context of [[Conformal Submanifold on Symplectic Geometry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Spectral Sequence]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Separable Formula in Number Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Graded Flow.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Quantum Field Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Graded Flow*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.140.197',1),
(195,136,NULL,NULL,'2019-04-27 02:02:43','2019-04-27 02:02:43','2019-04-27 02:02:43',2,'# Parabolic Patch on Number Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Parabolic Patch on Number Theory** is a [[Contraction]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Strict Stack]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Parabolic Patch on Number Theory satisfies standard Fundamental conditions in the context of [[Symplectic Isomorphism]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Category]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Separable Sheaf on Differential Topology]] admits a canonical structure of a Parabolic Patch on Number Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Parabolic Patch on Number Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.167.48',1),
(196,136,NULL,NULL,'2019-04-27 02:02:43','2019-07-08 07:54:06','2019-07-08 07:54:06',2,'# Parabolic Patch on Number Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Parabolic Patch on Number Theory** is a [[Contraction]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Strict Stack]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Parabolic Patch on Number Theory satisfies standard Fundamental conditions in the context of [[Symplectic Isomorphism]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Category]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Separable Sheaf on Differential Topology]] admits a canonical structure of a Parabolic Patch on Number Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Parabolic Patch on Number Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.16.170',1),
(197,136,NULL,NULL,'2019-04-27 02:02:43','2019-10-11 22:27:35','2019-10-11 22:27:35',1,'# Parabolic Patch on Number Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Parabolic Patch on Number Theory** is a [[Contraction]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Strict Stack]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Parabolic Patch on Number Theory satisfies standard Fundamental conditions in the context of [[Symplectic Isomorphism]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Category]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Separable Sheaf on Differential Topology]] admits a canonical structure of a Parabolic Patch on Number Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Parabolic Patch on Number Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.90.247',1),
(198,137,NULL,NULL,'2021-09-19 19:40:05','2021-09-19 19:40:05','2021-09-19 19:40:05',3,'# Quantum Polygon Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Quantum Polygon Theory** is a [[Character]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Affine Bundle]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Quantum Polygon Theory satisfies standard Deformed conditions in the context of [[Abstract Filter]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Structure]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cohomological Geometry Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Quantum Polygon Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Quantum Polygon Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.181.79',1),
(199,137,NULL,NULL,'2021-09-19 19:40:05','2022-03-04 11:11:40','2022-03-04 11:11:40',2,'# Quantum Polygon Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Quantum Polygon Theory** is a [[Character]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Affine Bundle]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Quantum Polygon Theory satisfies standard Deformed conditions in the context of [[Abstract Filter]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Structure]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cohomological Geometry Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Quantum Polygon Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Quantum Polygon Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.124.79',1),
(200,138,NULL,NULL,'2015-09-11 09:17:10','2015-09-11 09:17:10','2015-09-11 09:17:10',1,'# Cohomology of Tensor Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Cohomology of Tensor Type** is a [[Geometry]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Twisted Relation in Differential Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Cohomology of Tensor Type satisfies standard Spectral conditions in the context of [[Universal Cluster Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Field]].\n\n[[!include Differential Topology Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Topological Group on Commutative Algebra]] admits a canonical structure of a Cohomology of Tensor Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Cohomology of Tensor Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.85.49',1),
(201,138,NULL,NULL,'2015-09-11 09:17:10','2016-01-05 13:59:01','2016-01-05 13:59:01',1,'# Cohomology of Tensor Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Cohomology of Tensor Type** is a [[Geometry]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Twisted Relation in Differential Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Cohomology of Tensor Type satisfies standard Spectral conditions in the context of [[Universal Cluster Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Field]].\n\n[[!include Differential Topology Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Topological Group on Commutative Algebra]] admits a canonical structure of a Cohomology of Tensor Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Cohomology of Tensor Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.226.197',1),
(202,139,NULL,NULL,'2017-06-24 21:54:44','2017-06-24 21:54:44','2017-06-24 21:54:44',1,'# Compact Triangulation on Symplectic Geometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Complex Analysis]], a **Compact Triangulation on Symplectic Geometry** is a [[Diagram]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Simplicial Operator in Algebraic Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Compact Triangulation on Symplectic Geometry satisfies standard Complete conditions in the context of [[Suspension of Dynamical Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Triangulation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Rational Curvature of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Compact Triangulation on Symplectic Geometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Complex Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Compact Triangulation on Symplectic Geometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.118.234',1),
(203,140,NULL,NULL,'2023-01-10 08:18:59','2023-01-10 08:18:59','2023-01-10 08:18:59',2,'# Higher Filtered Fibration\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Abstract Algebra]], a **Higher Filtered Fibration** is a [[Graph]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Projective Fraction Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Filtered Fibration satisfies standard Riemannian conditions in the context of [[Cofibrant Complex on Geometric Group Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Spectrum]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Internal Torsion on Higher Category Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Filtered Fibration.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Abstract Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Filtered Fibration*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.157.78',1),
(204,141,NULL,NULL,'2022-11-07 23:53:03','2022-11-07 23:53:03','2022-11-07 23:53:03',1,'# Asymptotic Germ\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Asymptotic Germ** is a [[Section]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Connected Cone for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Asymptotic Germ satisfies standard Free conditions in the context of [[Twisted Root for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Sheaf]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Solvable Tree]] admits a canonical structure of a Asymptotic Germ.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Asymptotic Germ*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.47.240',1),
(205,142,NULL,NULL,'2021-06-16 05:21:47','2021-06-16 05:21:47','2021-06-16 05:21:47',1,'# Orthogonal Object in Higher Category Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Abstract Algebra]], a **Orthogonal Object in Higher Category Theory** is a [[Differential]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Connected Link]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Orthogonal Object in Higher Category Theory satisfies standard Noncommutative conditions in the context of [[Perfect Restriction on Lie Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Conjecture]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Unitary Image of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Orthogonal Object in Higher Category Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Abstract Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Orthogonal Object in Higher Category Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.80.91',1),
(206,143,NULL,NULL,'2025-02-28 17:19:24','2025-02-28 17:19:24','2025-02-28 17:19:24',2,'# Weak Symmetry Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Category Theory]], a **Weak Symmetry Theory** is a [[Sequence]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Partition of Graded Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Weak Symmetry Theory satisfies standard Pseudo conditions in the context of [[Abelian Hierarchy of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Equation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Riemannian Target for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Weak Symmetry Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Weak Symmetry Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.7.10',1),
(207,144,NULL,NULL,'2017-10-02 17:16:04','2017-10-02 17:16:04','2017-10-02 17:16:04',2,'# Local Germ for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Local Germ for Bundles** is a [[Target]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Conformal Section on Lie Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Local Germ for Bundles satisfies standard Uniform conditions in the context of [[Differential Equation Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Conjecture]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Cohomological Grading]] admits a canonical structure of a Local Germ for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Local Germ for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Local Germ for Bundles (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.45.52',1),
(208,144,NULL,NULL,'2017-10-02 17:16:04','2017-11-07 10:47:51','2017-11-07 10:47:51',1,'# Local Germ for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Local Germ for Bundles** is a [[Target]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Conformal Section on Lie Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Local Germ for Bundles satisfies standard Uniform conditions in the context of [[Differential Equation Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Conjecture]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Cohomological Grading]] admits a canonical structure of a Local Germ for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Local Germ for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Local Germ for Bundles (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.7.197',1),
(209,144,NULL,NULL,'2017-10-02 17:16:04','2017-10-22 10:22:47','2017-10-22 10:22:47',2,'# Local Germ for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Local Germ for Bundles** is a [[Target]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Conformal Section on Lie Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Local Germ for Bundles satisfies standard Uniform conditions in the context of [[Differential Equation Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Conjecture]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Cohomological Grading]] admits a canonical structure of a Local Germ for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Local Germ for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Local Germ for Bundles (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.147.242',1),
(210,145,NULL,NULL,'2015-09-21 05:51:37','2015-09-21 05:51:37','2015-09-21 05:51:37',1,'# Conformal Wave\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Homotopy Theory]], a **Conformal Wave** is a [[Variety]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Regular Variety in Operator Algebras]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Conformal Wave satisfies standard Discrete conditions in the context of [[Formula of Enriched Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Spinor]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Lagrangian Coequalizer in Model Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Conformal Wave.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Homotopy Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Conformal Wave*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.178.110',1),
(211,146,NULL,NULL,'2025-07-14 05:58:06','2025-07-14 05:58:06','2025-07-14 05:58:06',1,'# Dual Symmetry Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Abstract Algebra]], a **Dual Symmetry Theory** is a [[Degree]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Affine Bundle]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Dual Symmetry Theory satisfies standard Affine conditions in the context of [[Sheafified Limit of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Identity]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Geometric Equivalence for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Dual Symmetry Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Abstract Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Dual Symmetry Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.210.44',1),
(212,147,NULL,NULL,'2018-08-03 04:58:46','2018-08-03 04:58:46','2018-08-03 04:58:46',1,'# Bimodular Retract for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Abstract Algebra]], a **Bimodular Retract for Bundles** is a [[Reduction]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Filtered Kernel on Differential Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Bimodular Retract for Bundles satisfies standard Complete conditions in the context of [[Smooth Cluster in Topology]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Flow]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Differential Infinity-Category in Homotopy Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Bimodular Retract for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Abstract Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Bimodular Retract for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.167.168',1),
(213,148,NULL,NULL,'2026-02-04 14:51:20','2026-02-04 14:51:20','2026-02-04 14:51:20',3,'# Orthogonal Determinant in Number Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Operator Algebras]], a **Orthogonal Determinant in Number Theory** is a [[Coequalizer]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Normal Deformation]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Orthogonal Determinant in Number Theory satisfies standard Exact conditions in the context of [[Tensor Ray]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Degree]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Spectral Sheaf in Lie Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Orthogonal Determinant in Number Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Operator Algebras]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Orthogonal Determinant in Number Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.102.177',1),
(214,148,NULL,NULL,'2026-02-04 14:51:20','2026-05-06 07:38:23','2026-05-06 07:38:23',2,'# Orthogonal Determinant in Number Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Operator Algebras]], a **Orthogonal Determinant in Number Theory** is a [[Coequalizer]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Normal Deformation]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Orthogonal Determinant in Number Theory satisfies standard Exact conditions in the context of [[Tensor Ray]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Degree]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Spectral Sheaf in Lie Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Orthogonal Determinant in Number Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Operator Algebras]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Orthogonal Determinant in Number Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.111.243',1),
