(217,148,NULL,NULL,'2026-02-04 14:51:20','2026-07-26 10:51:07','2026-07-26 10:51:07',3,'# Orthogonal Determinant in Number Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Operator Algebras]], a **Orthogonal Determinant in Number Theory** is a [[Coequalizer]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Normal Deformation]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Orthogonal Determinant in Number Theory satisfies standard Exact conditions in the context of [[Tensor Ray]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Degree]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Spectral Sheaf in Lie Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Orthogonal Determinant in Number Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Operator Algebras]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Orthogonal Determinant in Number Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.7.38',1),
(218,148,NULL,NULL,'2026-02-04 14:51:20','2026-02-18 13:58:52','2026-02-18 13:58:52',1,'# Orthogonal Determinant in Number Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Operator Algebras]], a **Orthogonal Determinant in Number Theory** is a [[Coequalizer]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Normal Deformation]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Orthogonal Determinant in Number Theory satisfies standard Exact conditions in the context of [[Tensor Ray]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Degree]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Spectral Sheaf in Lie Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Orthogonal Determinant in Number Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Operator Algebras]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Orthogonal Determinant in Number Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.39.249',1),
(219,149,NULL,NULL,'2014-12-02 11:06:40','2014-12-02 11:06:40','2014-12-02 11:06:40',3,'# Normal Site on Algebraic Topology\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Geometry]], a **Normal Site on Algebraic Topology** is a [[Image]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Sheafified Module Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Normal Site on Algebraic Topology satisfies standard Gauge conditions in the context of [[Quantum Coequalizer]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Theory]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Internal Order in Homotopy Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Normal Site on Algebraic Topology.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Normal Site on Algebraic Topology*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.60.59',1),
(220,149,NULL,NULL,'2014-12-02 11:06:40','2015-01-12 09:48:14','2015-01-12 09:48:14',2,'# Normal Site on Algebraic Topology\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Geometry]], a **Normal Site on Algebraic Topology** is a [[Image]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Sheafified Module Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Normal Site on Algebraic Topology satisfies standard Gauge conditions in the context of [[Quantum Coequalizer]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Theory]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Internal Order in Homotopy Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Normal Site on Algebraic Topology.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Normal Site on Algebraic Topology*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.245.54',1),
(221,150,NULL,NULL,'2020-08-30 04:30:18','2020-08-30 04:30:18','2020-08-30 04:30:18',3,'# Riemannian Path of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Number Theory]], a **Riemannian Path of Schemes** is a [[Functor]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Measure of Isometric Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Riemannian Path of Schemes satisfies standard Lax conditions in the context of [[Higher Differential System]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Homology]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Simplicial Tree of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Riemannian Path of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Number Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Riemannian Path of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.134.208',1),
(222,151,NULL,NULL,'2024-12-09 19:36:03','2024-12-09 19:36:03','2024-12-09 19:36:03',1,'# Operadic Groupoid for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Topology]], a **Operadic Groupoid for Bundles** is a [[Isometry]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Normal Gauging for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Operadic Groupoid for Bundles satisfies standard Compact conditions in the context of [[Class of Cyclic Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[System]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Sheafified Model]] admits a canonical structure of a Operadic Groupoid for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Operadic Groupoid for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.124.186',1),
(223,151,NULL,NULL,'2024-12-09 19:36:03','2025-04-23 15:04:21','2025-04-23 15:04:21',1,'# Operadic Groupoid for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Topology]], a **Operadic Groupoid for Bundles** is a [[Isometry]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Normal Gauging for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Operadic Groupoid for Bundles satisfies standard Compact conditions in the context of [[Class of Cyclic Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[System]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Sheafified Model]] admits a canonical structure of a Operadic Groupoid for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Operadic Groupoid for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.192.62',1),
(224,152,NULL,NULL,'2024-11-02 20:51:11','2024-11-02 20:51:11','2024-11-02 20:51:11',2,'# Super Homotopy Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Constructive Mathematics]], a **Super Homotopy Theory** is a [[Perturbation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Rational Curvature of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Super Homotopy Theory satisfies standard Derived conditions in the context of [[Higher Deformed Derivation]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Colimit]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Orthogonal Matrix in Topology]] admits a canonical structure of a Super Homotopy Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Constructive Mathematics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Super Homotopy Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.238.37',1),
(225,153,NULL,NULL,'2014-09-26 23:12:36','2014-09-26 23:12:36','2014-09-26 23:12:36',2,'# Strict Geometry for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Strict Geometry for Bundles** is a [[Unit]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Deformed Diagram on Algebraic Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Strict Geometry for Bundles satisfies standard Perfect conditions in the context of [[Homological Triangulation for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[State]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Conformal Amalgamation for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Strict Geometry for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Strict Geometry for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.232.102',1),
(226,154,NULL,NULL,'2018-08-09 23:03:20','2018-08-09 23:03:20','2018-08-09 23:03:20',3,'# Coalgebraic Theorem for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Quantum Field Theory]], a **Coalgebraic Theorem for Bundles** is a [[Algebra]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Node of Abstract Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Coalgebraic Theorem for Bundles satisfies standard Abstract conditions in the context of [[Conformal Submanifold on Symplectic Geometry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Lattice]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Topological Subobject on Noncommutative Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Coalgebraic Theorem for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Quantum Field Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Coalgebraic Theorem for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Coalgebraic Theorem for Bundles (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.11.136',1),
(227,155,NULL,NULL,'2022-11-02 07:38:30','2022-11-02 07:38:30','2022-11-02 07:38:30',1,'# Gauge Kernel Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Constructive Mathematics]], a **Gauge Kernel Theory** is a [[Partition]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Twisted Root for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Gauge Kernel Theory satisfies standard Cyclic conditions in the context of [[Local Orbit for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Fibration]].\n\n[[!include Constructive Mathematics Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Universal Splitting of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Gauge Kernel Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Constructive Mathematics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Gauge Kernel Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.74.229',1),
(228,156,NULL,NULL,'2018-12-19 05:19:11','2018-12-19 05:19:11','2018-12-19 05:19:11',1,'# Cyclic Discriminant in Symplectic Geometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Cyclic Discriminant in Symplectic Geometry** is a [[Bundle]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Lagrangian Operad Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Cyclic Discriminant in Symplectic Geometry satisfies standard Equivariant conditions in the context of [[Dual Restriction Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Ideal]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Quantum Polygon Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Cyclic Discriminant in Symplectic Geometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Cyclic Discriminant in Symplectic Geometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.207.4',1),
(229,157,NULL,NULL,'2022-02-03 08:40:22','2022-02-03 08:40:22','2022-02-03 08:40:22',2,'# Uniform Domain of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Higher Category Theory]], a **Uniform Domain of Schemes** is a [[Singularity]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Suspension of Dynamical Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Uniform Domain of Schemes satisfies standard Homotopical conditions in the context of [[Higher Discrete Group]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Ring]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Quantum Morphism]] admits a canonical structure of a Uniform Domain of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Higher Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Uniform Domain of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.81.113',1),
(230,158,NULL,NULL,'2017-05-19 14:08:20','2017-05-19 14:08:20','2017-05-19 14:08:20',1,'# Cartesian Object Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Cartesian Object Theory** is a [[Product]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Universal Model for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Cartesian Object Theory satisfies standard Dynamical conditions in the context of [[Exact Operad]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Pattern]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Equivariant Envelope]] admits a canonical structure of a Cartesian Object Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Cartesian Object Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.28.33',1),
(231,158,NULL,NULL,'2017-05-19 14:08:20','2017-07-27 15:51:35','2017-07-27 15:51:35',3,'# Cartesian Object Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Cartesian Object Theory** is a [[Product]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Universal Model for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Cartesian Object Theory satisfies standard Dynamical conditions in the context of [[Exact Operad]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Pattern]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Equivariant Envelope]] admits a canonical structure of a Cartesian Object Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Cartesian Object Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.173.142',1),
(232,159,NULL,NULL,'2016-05-12 00:49:20','2016-05-12 00:49:20','2016-05-12 00:49:20',3,'# Relative Phase on Representation Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Commutative Algebra]], a **Relative Phase on Representation Theory** is a [[Vertex]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Pseudo Cocycle on Geometric Group Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Relative Phase on Representation Theory satisfies standard Universal conditions in the context of [[Universal Sphere in Algebraic Geometry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Monad]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Relative Phase on Representation Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Commutative Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Relative Phase on Representation Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.87.114',1),
(233,160,NULL,NULL,'2022-03-25 02:14:10','2022-03-25 02:14:10','2022-03-25 02:14:10',1,'# Exact Operad\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Proof Theory]], a **Exact Operad** is a [[Object]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Relative Target for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Exact Operad satisfies standard Normal conditions in the context of [[Smooth Phase of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Bundle]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Solvable Spinor in Mathematical Physics]] admits a canonical structure of a Exact Operad.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Proof Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Exact Operad*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.229.193',1),
(234,161,NULL,NULL,'2020-11-27 09:06:04','2020-11-27 09:06:04','2020-11-27 09:06:04',1,'# Sheafified Model\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Complex Analysis]], a **Sheafified Model** is a [[Tensor]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Filtered Automaton Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Sheafified Model satisfies standard Deformed conditions in the context of [[Derived Discriminant]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Intersection]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Isotropic Orientation in Noncommutative Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Sheafified Model.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Complex Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Sheafified Model*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.80.188',1),
(235,162,NULL,NULL,'2020-02-23 03:40:54','2020-02-23 03:40:54','2020-02-23 03:40:54',2,'# Invariant Immersion\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Constructive Mathematics]], a **Invariant Immersion** is a [[Semigroup]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Twisted Relation in Differential Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Invariant Immersion satisfies standard Categorical conditions in the context of [[Symmetric Envelope on Differential Geometry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Quotient]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Operadic Scheme]] admits a canonical structure of a Invariant Immersion.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Constructive Mathematics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Invariant Immersion*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.2.216',1),
(236,162,NULL,NULL,'2020-02-23 03:40:54','2020-06-14 15:30:25','2020-06-14 15:30:25',3,'# Invariant Immersion\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Constructive Mathematics]], a **Invariant Immersion** is a [[Semigroup]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Twisted Relation in Differential Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Invariant Immersion satisfies standard Categorical conditions in the context of [[Symmetric Envelope on Differential Geometry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Quotient]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Operadic Scheme]] admits a canonical structure of a Invariant Immersion.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Constructive Mathematics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Invariant Immersion*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.74.43',1),
(237,163,NULL,NULL,'2026-02-05 21:16:40','2026-02-05 21:16:40','2026-02-05 21:16:40',2,'# Monoidal Relation of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Geometry]], a **Monoidal Relation of Schemes** is a [[Duality]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Riemannian Algorithm]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Monoidal Relation of Schemes satisfies standard Cofibrant conditions in the context of [[Differential Perturbation Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Measure]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Filtered Kernel on Differential Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Monoidal Relation of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Monoidal Relation of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Monoidal Relation of Schemes (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.28.239',1),
(238,164,NULL,NULL,'2022-11-25 22:51:55','2022-11-25 22:51:55','2022-11-25 22:51:55',1,'# Projective Fraction Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Category Theory]], a **Projective Fraction Theory** is a [[Target]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Compact Orientation on Homotopy Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Projective Fraction Theory satisfies standard Infinite conditions in the context of [[Derived Duality]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Realization]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Affine Intersection Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Projective Fraction Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Projective Fraction Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Projective Fraction Theory (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.221.231',1),
(239,165,NULL,NULL,'2019-09-15 09:06:36','2019-09-15 09:06:36','2019-09-15 09:06:36',2,'# Projective Automorphism of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Complex Analysis]], a **Projective Automorphism of Schemes** is a [[Suspension]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Toric Triangle of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Projective Automorphism of Schemes satisfies standard Local conditions in the context of [[Sheafified Limit of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Resonance]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cartesian Point for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Projective Automorphism of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Complex Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Projective Automorphism of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.92.185',1),
(240,166,NULL,NULL,'2014-03-25 15:33:21','2014-03-25 15:33:21','2014-03-25 15:33:21',3,'# Topological Subobject on Noncommutative Geometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Arithmetic Geometry]], a **Topological Subobject on Noncommutative Geometry** is a [[Ideal]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Deformation Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Topological Subobject on Noncommutative Geometry satisfies standard Linear conditions in the context of [[Dynamical Presheaf on Abstract Algebra]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Structure]].\n\n[[!include Arithmetic Geometry Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Annihilating Image for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Topological Subobject on Noncommutative Geometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Arithmetic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Topological Subobject on Noncommutative Geometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.116.105',1),
(241,167,NULL,NULL,'2015-07-12 06:44:51','2015-07-12 06:44:51','2015-07-12 06:44:51',1,'# Degree of Parabolic Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Proof Theory]], a **Degree of Parabolic Type** is a [[Determinant]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Exact Operad]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Degree of Parabolic Type satisfies standard Annihilating conditions in the context of [[Noncommutative Series Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[System]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Topological Homology Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Degree of Parabolic Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Proof Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Degree of Parabolic Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n[[!redirects Degree of Parabolic Type (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.100.212',1),
(242,168,NULL,NULL,'2022-09-30 03:07:06','2022-09-30 03:07:06','2022-09-30 03:07:06',2,'# Linear Set\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Geometry]], a **Linear Set** is a [[Curvature]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Parabolic Orientation for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Linear Set satisfies standard Proper conditions in the context of [[Graded Topos]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Reduction]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Algebraic Series on Model Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Linear Set.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Linear Set*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.136.134',1),
(243,168,NULL,NULL,'2022-09-30 03:07:06','2023-03-10 20:47:06','2023-03-10 20:47:06',2,'# Linear Set\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Geometry]], a **Linear Set** is a [[Curvature]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Parabolic Orientation for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Linear Set satisfies standard Proper conditions in the context of [[Graded Topos]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Reduction]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Algebraic Series on Model Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Linear Set.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Linear Set*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.121.132',1),
(244,168,NULL,NULL,'2022-09-30 03:07:06','2022-10-10 06:59:28','2022-10-10 06:59:28',1,'# Linear Set\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Geometry]], a **Linear Set** is a [[Curvature]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Parabolic Orientation for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Linear Set satisfies standard Proper conditions in the context of [[Graded Topos]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Reduction]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Algebraic Series on Model Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Linear Set.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Linear Set*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.38.108',1),
(245,168,NULL,NULL,'2022-09-30 03:07:06','2023-02-09 04:59:34','2023-02-09 04:59:34',3,'# Linear Set\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Geometry]], a **Linear Set** is a [[Curvature]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Parabolic Orientation for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Linear Set satisfies standard Proper conditions in the context of [[Graded Topos]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Reduction]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Algebraic Series on Model Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Linear Set.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Linear Set*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.22.122',1),
(246,168,NULL,NULL,'2022-09-30 03:07:06','2023-01-06 07:41:10','2023-01-06 07:41:10',1,'# Linear Set\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Geometry]], a **Linear Set** is a [[Curvature]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Parabolic Orientation for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Linear Set satisfies standard Proper conditions in the context of [[Graded Topos]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Reduction]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Algebraic Series on Model Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Linear Set.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Linear Set*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.193.156',1),
(247,168,NULL,NULL,'2022-09-30 03:07:06','2022-10-12 09:52:01','2022-10-12 09:52:01',1,'# Linear Set\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Geometry]], a **Linear Set** is a [[Curvature]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Parabolic Orientation for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Linear Set satisfies standard Proper conditions in the context of [[Graded Topos]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Reduction]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Algebraic Series on Model Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Linear Set.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Linear Set*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.183.226',1),
(248,169,NULL,NULL,'2023-12-13 10:06:08','2023-12-13 10:06:08','2023-12-13 10:06:08',1,'# Cohomological Stability\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Cohomological Stability** is a [[Skeleton]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Bimodular Stability]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Cohomological Stability satisfies standard Internal conditions in the context of [[Higher Asymptotic Cell]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Hierarchy]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Categorical Network in Algebraic Topology]] admits a canonical structure of a Cohomological Stability.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Cohomological Stability*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.5.26',1),
(249,170,NULL,NULL,'2015-06-03 02:52:21','2015-06-03 02:52:21','2015-06-03 02:52:21',3,'# Equivalence of Parabolic Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Equivalence of Parabolic Type** is a [[Framework]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Sheafified Object Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Equivalence of Parabolic Type satisfies standard Smooth conditions in the context of [[Higher Geometric Set]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Volume]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Projective Theorem in Number Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Equivalence of Parabolic Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Equivalence of Parabolic Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.45.15',1),
(250,170,NULL,NULL,'2015-06-03 02:52:21','2015-09-06 19:51:16','2015-09-06 19:51:16',1,'# Equivalence of Parabolic Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Equivalence of Parabolic Type** is a [[Framework]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Sheafified Object Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Equivalence of Parabolic Type satisfies standard Smooth conditions in the context of [[Higher Geometric Set]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Volume]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Projective Theorem in Number Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Equivalence of Parabolic Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Equivalence of Parabolic Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.12.166',1),
(251,171,NULL,NULL,'2016-03-01 08:24:52','2016-03-01 08:24:52','2016-03-01 08:24:52',1,'# Higher Lagrangian Isometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Arithmetic Geometry]], a **Higher Lagrangian Isometry** is a [[Potential]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Algebraic Group in Abstract Algebra]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Lagrangian Isometry satisfies standard Poincaré conditions in the context of [[Continuous Period Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Subgroup]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Coalgebraic Surface for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Lagrangian Isometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Arithmetic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Lagrangian Isometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.180.42',1),
(252,171,NULL,NULL,'2016-03-01 08:24:52','2016-04-14 17:12:13','2016-04-14 17:12:13',1,'# Higher Lagrangian Isometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Arithmetic Geometry]], a **Higher Lagrangian Isometry** is a [[Potential]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Algebraic Group in Abstract Algebra]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Lagrangian Isometry satisfies standard Poincaré conditions in the context of [[Continuous Period Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Subgroup]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Coalgebraic Surface for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Lagrangian Isometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Arithmetic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Lagrangian Isometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.196.214',1),
(253,172,NULL,NULL,'2017-04-12 12:56:08','2017-04-12 12:56:08','2017-04-12 12:56:08',1,'# Homotopical Knot for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Abstract Algebra]], a **Homotopical Knot for Bundles** is a [[Tree]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Connected Class for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Homotopical Knot for Bundles satisfies standard Separable conditions in the context of [[Linear Theory of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Approximation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Gauge Class of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Homotopical Knot for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Abstract Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Homotopical Knot for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.9.111',1),
(254,173,NULL,NULL,'2016-10-24 10:14:50','2016-10-24 10:14:50','2016-10-24 10:14:50',1,'# Geometric Triangulation of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Abstract Algebra]], a **Geometric Triangulation of Schemes** is a [[Threshold]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Metric of Smooth Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Geometric Triangulation of Schemes satisfies standard Fibrant conditions in the context of [[Invariant System on Number Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Polytope]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Connected Codimension]] admits a canonical structure of a Geometric Triangulation of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Abstract Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Geometric Triangulation of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.51.6',1),
(255,174,NULL,NULL,'2014-08-04 09:46:29','2014-08-04 09:46:29','2014-08-04 09:46:29',1,'# Higher Cohomological Point\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Higher Cohomological Point** is a [[Model]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Cartesian Point for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Cohomological Point satisfies standard Symmetric conditions in the context of [[Gauge Kernel Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Relation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Symplectic Node]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Cohomological Point.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Cohomological Point*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.197.81',1),
(256,175,NULL,NULL,'2019-01-01 14:21:11','2019-01-01 14:21:11','2019-01-01 14:21:11',2,'# Internal Framework\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Internal Framework** is a [[Scheme]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Fibrant Representation of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Internal Framework satisfies standard Exact conditions in the context of [[Graded Topos]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Derivation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Topological Connection on Mathematical Physics]] admits a canonical structure of a Internal Framework.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Internal Framework*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.42.98',1),
(257,176,NULL,NULL,'2019-07-23 17:28:32','2019-07-23 17:28:32','2019-07-23 17:28:32',1,'# Symplectic Boundary in Differential Geometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Homotopy Theory]], a **Symplectic Boundary in Differential Geometry** is a [[Realization]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Deformed Sheaf on Category Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Symplectic Boundary in Differential Geometry satisfies standard Poincaré conditions in the context of [[Cell of Fundamental Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Image]].\n\n[[!include Homotopy Theory Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Riemannian Target for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Symplectic Boundary in Differential Geometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Homotopy Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Symplectic Boundary in Differential Geometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.215.48',1),
(258,177,NULL,NULL,'2017-11-20 15:53:52','2017-11-20 15:53:52','2017-11-20 15:53:52',3,'# Free Field\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Number Theory]], a **Free Field** is a [[Metric]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Affine Bundle]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Free Field satisfies standard Equivariant conditions in the context of [[Universal Cluster Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Matrix]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Rational Radical in Abstract Algebra]] admits a canonical structure of a Free Field.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Number Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Free Field*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.84.109',1),
(259,178,NULL,NULL,'2024-03-05 23:46:18','2024-03-05 23:46:18','2024-03-05 23:46:18',2,'# Degree of Formal Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Degree of Formal Type** is a [[Variety]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Projective Retract of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Degree of Formal Type satisfies standard Discrete conditions in the context of [[Compact Triangulation]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Endomorphism]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Conformal Section on Lie Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Degree of Formal Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Degree of Formal Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.183.59',1),
(260,179,NULL,NULL,'2021-12-25 00:51:37','2021-12-25 00:51:37','2021-12-25 00:51:37',2,'# Separable Formula in Number Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Commutative Algebra]], a **Separable Formula in Number Theory** is a [[Wave]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Riemannian Path of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Separable Formula in Number Theory satisfies standard Poincaré conditions in the context of [[Equivariant Boundary]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Tree]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Smooth Triangle]] admits a canonical structure of a Separable Formula in Number Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Commutative Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Separable Formula in Number Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Separable Formula in Number Theory (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.92.221',1),
(261,180,NULL,NULL,'2023-06-26 00:14:16','2023-06-26 00:14:16','2023-06-26 00:14:16',1,'# Higher Isotropic Endomorphism\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Higher Category Theory]], a **Higher Isotropic Endomorphism** is a [[Grading]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Quantum Polygon Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Isotropic Endomorphism satisfies standard Semisimple conditions in the context of [[Unitary Polytope for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Immersion]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Noncommutative Pattern Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Isotropic Endomorphism.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Higher Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Isotropic Endomorphism*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.228.97',1),
(262,181,NULL,NULL,'2021-09-01 09:53:00','2021-09-01 09:53:00','2021-09-01 09:53:00',2,'# Differential Infinity-Category in Homotopy Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Category Theory]], a **Differential Infinity-Category in Homotopy Theory** is a [[Polytope]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Conformal Symmetry Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Differential Infinity-Category in Homotopy Theory satisfies standard Discrete conditions in the context of [[Filtered Morphism]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Stability]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Derived Measure Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Differential Infinity-Category in Homotopy Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Differential Infinity-Category in Homotopy Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.240.17',1),
(263,182,NULL,NULL,'2024-05-15 08:49:36','2024-05-15 08:49:36','2024-05-15 08:49:36',3,'# Holomorphic Section\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Holomorphic Section** is a [[Reduction]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Orthogonal Invariant of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Holomorphic Section satisfies standard Quantum conditions in the context of [[Higher Twisted Shift]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Envelope]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Monoidal Splitting Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Holomorphic Section.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Holomorphic Section*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.164.88',1),
(264,183,NULL,NULL,'2019-05-14 05:07:34','2019-05-14 05:07:34','2019-05-14 05:07:34',2,'# Linear Theory of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Linear Theory of Schemes** is a [[Model]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Dual Symmetry Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Linear Theory of Schemes satisfies standard Lax conditions in the context of [[Higher Strict Series]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Fiber]].\n\n[[!include Model Theory Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Orthogonal Quotient]] admits a canonical structure of a Linear Theory of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Linear Theory of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.234.205',1),
(265,184,NULL,NULL,'2015-07-07 16:32:20','2015-07-07 16:32:20','2015-07-07 16:32:20',1,'# Simplicial Surgery in Higher Category Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Lie Theory]], a **Simplicial Surgery in Higher Category Theory** is a [[Algebra]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Exact Spectrum in Abstract Algebra]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Simplicial Surgery in Higher Category Theory satisfies standard Geometric conditions in the context of [[Higher Solvable Flow]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Category]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Abelian Hierarchy of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Simplicial Surgery in Higher Category Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Lie Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Simplicial Surgery in Higher Category Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n[[!redirects Simplicial Surgery in Higher Category Theory (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.63.125',1),
(266,185,NULL,NULL,'2021-03-19 01:55:08','2021-03-19 01:55:08','2021-03-19 01:55:08',1,'# Conformal Symmetry Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Geometry]], a **Conformal Symmetry Theory** is a [[Lie Algebra]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Deformed Sheaf on Category Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Conformal Symmetry Theory satisfies standard Derived conditions in the context of [[Infinite Period of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Discriminant]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Free Framework in Differential Topology]] admits a canonical structure of a Conformal Symmetry Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Conformal Symmetry Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.56.94',1),
(267,186,NULL,NULL,'2020-05-24 22:27:04','2020-05-24 22:27:04','2020-05-24 22:27:04',1,'# Higher Twisted Shift\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Higher Twisted Shift** is a [[Hull]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Filtered Kernel on Differential Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Twisted Shift satisfies standard Equivariant conditions in the context of [[Derived Extension for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Graph]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Differential System]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Twisted Shift.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Twisted Shift*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.166.239',1),
(268,187,NULL,NULL,'2021-12-10 04:54:53','2021-12-10 04:54:53','2021-12-10 04:54:53',2,'# Internal Refinement\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Homotopy Theory]], a **Internal Refinement** is a [[Kernel]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Perfect Symmetry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Internal Refinement satisfies standard Quasi conditions in the context of [[Cartesian Point for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Category]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Semisimple Algorithm]] admits a canonical structure of a Internal Refinement.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Homotopy Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Internal Refinement*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.199.141',1),
(269,188,NULL,NULL,'2020-02-12 04:53:47','2020-02-12 04:53:47','2020-02-12 04:53:47',1,'# Cartesian Quotient on Higher Category Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Proof Theory]], a **Cartesian Quotient on Higher Category Theory** is a [[Class]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Graded Topos]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Cartesian Quotient on Higher Category Theory satisfies standard Lagrangian conditions in the context of [[Internal Torsion of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Object]].\n\n[[!include Proof Theory Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Universal Cluster Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Cartesian Quotient on Higher Category Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Proof Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Cartesian Quotient on Higher Category Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Cartesian Quotient on Higher Category Theory (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.171.178',1),
(270,189,NULL,NULL,'2021-09-12 02:50:09','2021-09-12 02:50:09','2021-09-12 02:50:09',2,'# Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Number Theory]], a **Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry** is a [[Invariant]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Nilpotent Tree on Model Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry satisfies standard Regular conditions in the context of [[Graded Shift]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Translation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Super Extension Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Number Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.152.252',1),
(271,189,NULL,NULL,'2021-09-12 02:50:09','2021-09-26 08:08:20','2021-09-26 08:08:20',3,'# Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Number Theory]], a **Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry** is a [[Invariant]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Nilpotent Tree on Model Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry satisfies standard Regular conditions in the context of [[Graded Shift]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Translation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Super Extension Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Number Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.118.161',1),
(272,189,NULL,NULL,'2021-09-12 02:50:09','2021-10-17 13:39:50','2021-10-17 13:39:50',2,'# Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Number Theory]], a **Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry** is a [[Invariant]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Nilpotent Tree on Model Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry satisfies standard Regular conditions in the context of [[Graded Shift]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Translation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Super Extension Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Number Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.111.177',1),
