(274,189,NULL,NULL,'2021-09-12 02:50:09','2021-10-08 04:19:10','2021-10-08 04:19:10',1,'# Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Number Theory]], a **Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry** is a [[Invariant]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Nilpotent Tree on Model Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry satisfies standard Regular conditions in the context of [[Graded Shift]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Translation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Super Extension Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Number Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.217.99',1),
(275,190,NULL,NULL,'2025-07-01 21:55:58','2025-07-01 21:55:58','2025-07-01 21:55:58',2,'# Noncommutative Link in Higher Category Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Noncommutative Link in Higher Category Theory** is a [[Kernel]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Universal Splitting of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Noncommutative Link in Higher Category Theory satisfies standard Dual conditions in the context of [[Torsion of Riemannian Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Potential]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Cofibrant Lemma]] admits a canonical structure of a Noncommutative Link in Higher Category Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Noncommutative Link in Higher Category Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.220.136',1),
(276,191,NULL,NULL,'2017-03-21 01:02:50','2017-03-21 01:02:50','2017-03-21 01:02:50',2,'# Universal Domain for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Universal Domain for Bundles** is a [[Operator]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Super Perturbation in Operator Algebras]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Universal Domain for Bundles satisfies standard Orthogonal conditions in the context of [[Separable Site for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Duality]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Quantum Adjunction of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Universal Domain for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Universal Domain for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Universal Domain for Bundles (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.38.231',1),
(277,191,NULL,NULL,'2017-03-21 01:02:50','2017-07-17 22:35:52','2017-07-17 22:35:52',3,'# Universal Domain for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Universal Domain for Bundles** is a [[Operator]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Super Perturbation in Operator Algebras]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Universal Domain for Bundles satisfies standard Orthogonal conditions in the context of [[Separable Site for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Duality]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Quantum Adjunction of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Universal Domain for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Universal Domain for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Universal Domain for Bundles (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.77.124',1),
(278,192,NULL,NULL,'2023-12-30 21:42:15','2023-12-30 21:42:15','2023-12-30 21:42:15',1,'# State of Quasi Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **State of Quasi Type** is a [[Polygon]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Coalgebraic Patch Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA State of Quasi Type satisfies standard Compact conditions in the context of [[Higher Unitary Degree]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Algorithm]].\n\n[[!include Mathematical Physics Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Formal Hierarchy on Noncommutative Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a State of Quasi Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on State of Quasi Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.125.84',1),
(279,193,NULL,NULL,'2014-05-04 22:21:52','2014-05-04 22:21:52','2014-05-04 22:21:52',1,'# Parabolic Orientation for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Commutative Algebra]], a **Parabolic Orientation for Bundles** is a [[Spinor]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Symmetric Envelope on Differential Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Parabolic Orientation for Bundles satisfies standard Homological conditions in the context of [[Symplectic Coequalizer of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Representation]].\n\n[[!include Commutative Algebra Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Pseudo Reduction for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Parabolic Orientation for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Commutative Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Parabolic Orientation for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.60.96',1),
(280,193,NULL,NULL,'2014-05-04 22:21:52','2014-09-13 03:13:02','2014-09-13 03:13:02',3,'# Parabolic Orientation for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Commutative Algebra]], a **Parabolic Orientation for Bundles** is a [[Spinor]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Symmetric Envelope on Differential Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Parabolic Orientation for Bundles satisfies standard Homological conditions in the context of [[Symplectic Coequalizer of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Representation]].\n\n[[!include Commutative Algebra Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Pseudo Reduction for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Parabolic Orientation for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Commutative Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Parabolic Orientation for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.128.161',1),
(281,193,NULL,NULL,'2014-05-04 22:21:52','2014-06-01 21:49:59','2014-06-01 21:49:59',3,'# Parabolic Orientation for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Commutative Algebra]], a **Parabolic Orientation for Bundles** is a [[Spinor]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Symmetric Envelope on Differential Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Parabolic Orientation for Bundles satisfies standard Homological conditions in the context of [[Symplectic Coequalizer of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Representation]].\n\n[[!include Commutative Algebra Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Pseudo Reduction for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Parabolic Orientation for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Commutative Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Parabolic Orientation for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.89.237',1),
(282,194,NULL,NULL,'2015-03-01 16:32:21','2015-03-01 16:32:21','2015-03-01 16:32:21',1,'# Higher Homotopical Germ\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Higher Homotopical Germ** is a [[Ensemble]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Theory of Proper Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Homotopical Germ satisfies standard Higher conditions in the context of [[Uniform Domain of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Topos]].\n\n[[!include Mathematical Physics Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Relative Phase on Representation Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Homotopical Germ.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Homotopical Germ*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.32.28',1),
(283,195,NULL,NULL,'2016-04-27 19:19:56','2016-04-27 19:19:56','2016-04-27 19:19:56',2,'# Higher Operadic Germ\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Higher Operadic Germ** is a [[Submanifold]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Bivector of Asymptotic Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Operadic Germ satisfies standard Smooth conditions in the context of [[Toric Relation of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Framework]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Twisted Root for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Operadic Germ.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Operadic Germ*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.184.110',1),
(284,196,NULL,NULL,'2023-11-12 11:10:32','2023-11-12 11:10:32','2023-11-12 11:10:32',3,'# Separable Site for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Quantum Field Theory]], a **Separable Site for Bundles** is a [[Boundary]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Isometric Boundary]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Separable Site for Bundles satisfies standard Abelian conditions in the context of [[Infinite Automorphism of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Site]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Normal Realization for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Separable Site for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Quantum Field Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Separable Site for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.50.44',1),
(285,197,NULL,NULL,'2024-03-06 20:30:04','2024-03-06 20:30:04','2024-03-06 20:30:04',2,'# Infinite Automorphism of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Infinite Automorphism of Schemes** is a [[Monad]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Toric Unit]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Infinite Automorphism of Schemes satisfies standard Cartesian conditions in the context of [[Modular Cell for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Approximation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Derived Discriminant]] admits a canonical structure of a Infinite Automorphism of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Infinite Automorphism of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.168.51',1),
(286,198,NULL,NULL,'2015-09-23 12:41:10','2015-09-23 12:41:10','2015-09-23 12:41:10',2,'# Dynamical Presheaf on Abstract Algebra\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Arithmetic Geometry]], a **Dynamical Presheaf on Abstract Algebra** is a [[Equivalence]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Solvable Radical of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Dynamical Presheaf on Abstract Algebra satisfies standard Weighted conditions in the context of [[Stable Triangle of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Theory]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Super Quotient]] admits a canonical structure of a Dynamical Presheaf on Abstract Algebra.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Arithmetic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Dynamical Presheaf on Abstract Algebra*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.147.14',1),
(287,199,NULL,NULL,'2024-06-24 17:17:23','2024-06-24 17:17:23','2024-06-24 17:17:23',1,'# Fundamental Lattice on Functional Analysis\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Fundamental Lattice on Functional Analysis** is a [[Relation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Quantum Morphism]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Fundamental Lattice on Functional Analysis satisfies standard Discrete conditions in the context of [[Abelian Operad on Arithmetic Geometry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Fraction]].\n\n[[!include Mathematical Physics Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Orthogonal Determinant in Number Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Fundamental Lattice on Functional Analysis.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Fundamental Lattice on Functional Analysis*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.104.118',1),
(288,200,NULL,NULL,'2017-05-12 23:18:12','2017-05-12 23:18:12','2017-05-12 23:18:12',1,'# Higher Semisimple Algorithm\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Number Theory]], a **Higher Semisimple Algorithm** is a [[Hierarchy]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Nilpotent Curvature Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Semisimple Algorithm satisfies standard Discrete conditions in the context of [[Filter of Separable Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Fiber]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Complex of Noncommutative Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Semisimple Algorithm.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Number Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Semisimple Algorithm*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.202.163',1),
(289,201,NULL,NULL,'2016-12-28 01:54:01','2016-12-28 01:54:01','2016-12-28 01:54:01',1,'# Cohomological Geometry Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Quantum Field Theory]], a **Cohomological Geometry Theory** is a [[Patch]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Exact Operad]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Cohomological Geometry Theory satisfies standard Toric conditions in the context of [[Theory of Proper Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Envelope]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Perfect Polytope]] admits a canonical structure of a Cohomological Geometry Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Quantum Field Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Cohomological Geometry Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.128.254',1),
(290,202,NULL,NULL,'2018-11-19 11:32:41','2018-11-19 11:32:41','2018-11-19 11:32:41',3,'# Projective Retract of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Projective Retract of Schemes** is a [[Sequence]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Linear Quantum Group]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Projective Retract of Schemes satisfies standard Cohomological conditions in the context of [[Isometric Order Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Fiber]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Weak Cohomology on Lie Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Projective Retract of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Projective Retract of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Projective Retract of Schemes (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.9.9',1),
(291,202,NULL,NULL,'2018-11-19 11:32:41','2018-12-10 09:08:34','2018-12-10 09:08:34',2,'# Projective Retract of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Projective Retract of Schemes** is a [[Sequence]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Linear Quantum Group]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Projective Retract of Schemes satisfies standard Cohomological conditions in the context of [[Isometric Order Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Fiber]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Weak Cohomology on Lie Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Projective Retract of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Projective Retract of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Projective Retract of Schemes (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.24.178',1),
(292,203,NULL,NULL,'2014-12-13 14:48:17','2014-12-13 14:48:17','2014-12-13 14:48:17',1,'# Uniform Polygon Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Uniform Polygon Theory** is a [[Colimit]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Fundamental Filter on Topology]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Uniform Polygon Theory satisfies standard Frobenius conditions in the context of [[Geometric Algorithm of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Variety]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Wave of Relative Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Uniform Polygon Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Uniform Polygon Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.236.228',1),
(293,204,NULL,NULL,'2015-05-09 20:00:04','2015-05-09 20:00:04','2015-05-09 20:00:04',2,'# Operadic Approximation for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Operadic Approximation for Bundles** is a [[Contraction]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Solvable Sheaf of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Operadic Approximation for Bundles satisfies standard Isometric conditions in the context of [[Topological Transformation of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Sheaf]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Normal Layer in Proof Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Operadic Approximation for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Operadic Approximation for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.124.254',1),
(294,205,NULL,NULL,'2025-06-26 11:07:24','2025-06-26 11:07:24','2025-06-26 11:07:24',2,'# Higher Filtered Network\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Arithmetic Geometry]], a **Higher Filtered Network** is a [[Morphology]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Quantum Polygon Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Filtered Network satisfies standard Riemannian conditions in the context of [[Deformed Diagram on Algebraic Geometry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Resolution]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Partition of Graded Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Filtered Network.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Arithmetic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Filtered Network*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.252.23',1),
(295,206,NULL,NULL,'2019-04-09 14:23:34','2019-04-09 14:23:34','2019-04-09 14:23:34',3,'# Holomorphic Subalgebra Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Holomorphic Subalgebra Theory** is a [[Structure]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Super Extension Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Holomorphic Subalgebra Theory satisfies standard Homological conditions in the context of [[Cartesian Quotient on Higher Category Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Homology]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Symmetric Splitting Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Holomorphic Subalgebra Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Holomorphic Subalgebra Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.5.254',1),
(296,206,NULL,NULL,'2019-04-09 14:23:34','2019-10-02 07:03:00','2019-10-02 07:03:00',1,'# Holomorphic Subalgebra Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Holomorphic Subalgebra Theory** is a [[Structure]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Super Extension Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Holomorphic Subalgebra Theory satisfies standard Homological conditions in the context of [[Cartesian Quotient on Higher Category Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Homology]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Symmetric Splitting Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Holomorphic Subalgebra Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Holomorphic Subalgebra Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.12.48',1),
(297,207,NULL,NULL,'2021-08-07 05:16:25','2021-08-07 05:16:25','2021-08-07 05:16:25',1,'# Perfect Restriction on Lie Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Perfect Restriction on Lie Theory** is a [[Stratification]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Linear Set]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Perfect Restriction on Lie Theory satisfies standard Uniform conditions in the context of [[Isotropic Graph of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Boundary]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Riemannian Node]] admits a canonical structure of a Perfect Restriction on Lie Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Perfect Restriction on Lie Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.231.25',1),
(298,208,NULL,NULL,'2022-04-06 05:42:12','2022-04-06 05:42:12','2022-04-06 05:42:12',2,'# Nilpotent Tree on Model Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Geometry]], a **Nilpotent Tree on Model Theory** is a [[Product]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Weighted Immersion in Lie Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Nilpotent Tree on Model Theory satisfies standard Pseudo conditions in the context of [[Higher Perfect Fiber]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Measure]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Topological Subobject on Noncommutative Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Nilpotent Tree on Model Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Nilpotent Tree on Model Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Nilpotent Tree on Model Theory (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.60.38',1),
(299,209,NULL,NULL,'2024-08-09 11:16:21','2024-08-09 11:16:21','2024-08-09 11:16:21',2,'# Invariant System on Number Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Constructive Mathematics]], a **Invariant System on Number Theory** is a [[Image]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Theory of Proper Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Invariant System on Number Theory satisfies standard Graded conditions in the context of [[Stable Triangle of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Category]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Homotopical Decomposition]] admits a canonical structure of a Invariant System on Number Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Constructive Mathematics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Invariant System on Number Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.84.163',1),
(300,210,NULL,NULL,'2018-06-15 02:04:52','2018-06-15 02:04:52','2018-06-15 02:04:52',2,'# Higher Holomorphic Graph\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Homotopy Theory]], a **Higher Holomorphic Graph** is a [[Monad]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Normal Realization for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Holomorphic Graph satisfies standard Asymptotic conditions in the context of [[Higher Cofibrant Lemma]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Cluster]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Super Surgery]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Holomorphic Graph.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Homotopy Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Holomorphic Graph*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.78.123',1),
(301,210,NULL,NULL,'2018-06-15 02:04:52','2018-06-22 08:31:51','2018-06-22 08:31:51',1,'# Higher Holomorphic Graph\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Homotopy Theory]], a **Higher Holomorphic Graph** is a [[Monad]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Normal Realization for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Holomorphic Graph satisfies standard Asymptotic conditions in the context of [[Higher Cofibrant Lemma]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Cluster]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Super Surgery]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Holomorphic Graph.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Homotopy Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Holomorphic Graph*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.60.141',1),
(302,210,NULL,NULL,'2018-06-15 02:04:52','2018-06-26 04:39:44','2018-06-26 04:39:44',1,'# Higher Holomorphic Graph\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Homotopy Theory]], a **Higher Holomorphic Graph** is a [[Monad]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Normal Realization for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Holomorphic Graph satisfies standard Asymptotic conditions in the context of [[Higher Cofibrant Lemma]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Cluster]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Super Surgery]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Holomorphic Graph.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Homotopy Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Holomorphic Graph*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.233.133',1),
(303,211,NULL,NULL,'2016-04-25 17:33:18','2016-04-25 17:33:18','2016-04-25 17:33:18',3,'# Weighted Fiber in Complex Analysis\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Abstract Algebra]], a **Weighted Fiber in Complex Analysis** is a [[Subcategory]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Tensor Spectrum Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Weighted Fiber in Complex Analysis satisfies standard Semisimple conditions in the context of [[Invariant System on Number Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Determinant]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cyclic Discriminant in Symplectic Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Weighted Fiber in Complex Analysis.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Abstract Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Weighted Fiber in Complex Analysis*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.28.245',1),
(304,211,NULL,NULL,'2016-04-25 17:33:18','2016-10-19 23:46:32','2016-10-19 23:46:32',2,'# Weighted Fiber in Complex Analysis\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Abstract Algebra]], a **Weighted Fiber in Complex Analysis** is a [[Subcategory]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Tensor Spectrum Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Weighted Fiber in Complex Analysis satisfies standard Semisimple conditions in the context of [[Invariant System on Number Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Determinant]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cyclic Discriminant in Symplectic Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Weighted Fiber in Complex Analysis.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Abstract Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Weighted Fiber in Complex Analysis*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.42.76',1),
(305,212,NULL,NULL,'2019-12-24 15:08:06','2019-12-24 15:08:06','2019-12-24 15:08:06',2,'# Regular Variety in Operator Algebras\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Operator Algebras]], a **Regular Variety in Operator Algebras** is a [[Path]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Annihilating Contraction]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Regular Variety in Operator Algebras satisfies standard Riemannian conditions in the context of [[Modular Suspension on Arithmetic Geometry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Vertex]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Simplicial Ring]] admits a canonical structure of a Regular Variety in Operator Algebras.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Operator Algebras]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Regular Variety in Operator Algebras*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.103.1',1),
(306,212,NULL,NULL,'2019-12-24 15:08:06','2020-03-14 13:31:25','2020-03-14 13:31:25',1,'# Regular Variety in Operator Algebras\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Operator Algebras]], a **Regular Variety in Operator Algebras** is a [[Path]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Annihilating Contraction]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Regular Variety in Operator Algebras satisfies standard Riemannian conditions in the context of [[Modular Suspension on Arithmetic Geometry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Vertex]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Simplicial Ring]] admits a canonical structure of a Regular Variety in Operator Algebras.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Operator Algebras]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Regular Variety in Operator Algebras*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.39.50',1),
(307,213,NULL,NULL,'2015-10-26 19:30:00','2015-10-26 19:30:00','2015-10-26 19:30:00',2,'# Formula of Enriched Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Formula of Enriched Type** is a [[Tensor]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Formal Sphere in Topology]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Formula of Enriched Type satisfies standard Operadic conditions in the context of [[Conformal Wave]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Connection]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Algebraic Group in Abstract Algebra]] admits a canonical structure of a Formula of Enriched Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Formula of Enriched Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Formula of Enriched Type (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.145.103',1),
(308,214,NULL,NULL,'2018-06-03 05:37:42','2018-06-03 05:37:42','2018-06-03 05:37:42',2,'# Geometric Topos for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Operator Algebras]], a **Geometric Topos for Bundles** is a [[Spectral Sequence]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Fundamental Filter on Topology]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Geometric Topos for Bundles satisfies standard Uniform conditions in the context of [[Quasi Model Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Path]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Solvable Structure]] admits a canonical structure of a Geometric Topos for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Operator Algebras]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Geometric Topos for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n[[!redirects Geometric Topos for Bundles (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.24.114',1),
(309,215,NULL,NULL,'2018-04-14 18:02:55','2018-04-14 18:02:55','2018-04-14 18:02:55',3,'# Higher Complete Fiber\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Higher Complete Fiber** is a [[Subcategory]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Conformal Amalgamation for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Complete Fiber satisfies standard Lax conditions in the context of [[Rational Curvature of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Gauging]].\n\n[[!include Geometric Group Theory Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Differential Matrix on Geometric Group Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Complete Fiber.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Higher Complete Fiber*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.74.80',1),
(310,216,NULL,NULL,'2019-08-31 11:10:51','2019-08-31 11:10:51','2019-08-31 11:10:51',2,'# Isometric Equation Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Isometric Equation Theory** is a [[Adjunction]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Universal Cluster Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Isometric Equation Theory satisfies standard Solvable conditions in the context of [[Compact Formula]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Isomorphism]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Asymptotic Torsion]] admits a canonical structure of a Isometric Equation Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Isometric Equation Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.191.214',1),
(311,217,NULL,NULL,'2024-01-25 17:39:34','2024-01-25 17:39:34','2024-01-25 17:39:34',3,'# Universal Refinement\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Homotopy Theory]], a **Universal Refinement** is a [[Triangle]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Manifold of Relative Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Universal Refinement satisfies standard Invariant conditions in the context of [[Higher Unitary Degree]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Patch]].\n\n[[!include Homotopy Theory Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Filtered Fibration]] admits a canonical structure of a Universal Refinement.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Homotopy Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Universal Refinement*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.100.38',1),
(312,218,NULL,NULL,'2023-11-02 20:40:00','2023-11-02 20:40:00','2023-11-02 20:40:00',2,'# Higher Asymptotic Cell\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Arithmetic Geometry]], a **Higher Asymptotic Cell** is a [[Polytope]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Connected Cone for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Asymptotic Cell satisfies standard Perfect conditions in the context of [[Degree of Parabolic Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Spectrum]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cartesian Quotient on Higher Category Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Asymptotic Cell.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Arithmetic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Asymptotic Cell*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.34.22',1),
(313,219,NULL,NULL,'2025-11-11 11:21:56','2025-11-11 11:21:56','2025-11-11 11:21:56',2,'# Higher Unitary Degree\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Homotopy Theory]], a **Higher Unitary Degree** is a [[Set]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Complete Partition on Homotopy Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Unitary Degree satisfies standard Derived conditions in the context of [[Higher Deformation Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Volume]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Asymptotic Germ]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Unitary Degree.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Homotopy Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Unitary Degree*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.63.87',1),
(314,220,NULL,NULL,'2025-01-22 08:29:40','2025-01-22 08:29:40','2025-01-22 08:29:40',2,'# Conformal Amalgamation for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Commutative Algebra]], a **Conformal Amalgamation for Bundles** is a [[Codimension]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Cyclic Variety Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Conformal Amalgamation for Bundles satisfies standard Perfect conditions in the context of [[Operadic Groupoid for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Projection]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Weak Cohomology on Lie Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Conformal Amalgamation for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Commutative Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Conformal Amalgamation for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.69.35',1),
(315,221,NULL,NULL,'2015-04-03 16:12:05','2015-04-03 16:12:05','2015-04-03 16:12:05',2,'# Higher Perfect Polytope\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Higher Perfect Polytope** is a [[Surface]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Noncommutative Transformation]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Perfect Polytope satisfies standard Monoidal conditions in the context of [[Nilpotent Ring Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Embedding]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Simplicial Tree of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Perfect Polytope.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Perfect Polytope*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.243.22',1),
(316,221,NULL,NULL,'2015-04-03 16:12:05','2015-07-17 11:11:39','2015-07-17 11:11:39',3,'# Higher Perfect Polytope\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Higher Perfect Polytope** is a [[Surface]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Noncommutative Transformation]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Perfect Polytope satisfies standard Monoidal conditions in the context of [[Nilpotent Ring Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Embedding]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Simplicial Tree of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Perfect Polytope.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Perfect Polytope*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.241.233',1),
(317,222,NULL,NULL,'2018-11-28 14:22:03','2018-11-28 14:22:03','2018-11-28 14:22:03',2,'# Dynamical Cell on Functional Analysis\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Dynamical Cell on Functional Analysis** is a [[Amalgamation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Gauge Class of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Dynamical Cell on Functional Analysis satisfies standard Deformed conditions in the context of [[Higher Semisimple Algorithm]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Grading]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Annihilating Point of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Dynamical Cell on Functional Analysis.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Dynamical Cell on Functional Analysis*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.35.49',1),
(318,223,NULL,NULL,'2016-05-21 21:18:19','2016-05-21 21:18:19','2016-05-21 21:18:19',3,'# Connected Class for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Arithmetic Geometry]], a **Connected Class for Bundles** is a [[Principal Bundle]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Reduction of Fundamental Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Connected Class for Bundles satisfies standard Finite conditions in the context of [[Operad of Enriched Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Embedding]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Super Homotopy Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Connected Class for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Arithmetic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Connected Class for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.209.186',1),
(319,223,NULL,NULL,'2016-05-21 21:18:19','2016-07-26 11:09:32','2016-07-26 11:09:32',3,'# Connected Class for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Arithmetic Geometry]], a **Connected Class for Bundles** is a [[Principal Bundle]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Reduction of Fundamental Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Connected Class for Bundles satisfies standard Finite conditions in the context of [[Operad of Enriched Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Embedding]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Super Homotopy Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Connected Class for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Arithmetic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Connected Class for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.121.27',1),
(320,223,NULL,NULL,'2016-05-21 21:18:19','2016-11-07 00:29:29','2016-11-07 00:29:29',2,'# Connected Class for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Arithmetic Geometry]], a **Connected Class for Bundles** is a [[Principal Bundle]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Reduction of Fundamental Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Connected Class for Bundles satisfies standard Finite conditions in the context of [[Operad of Enriched Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Embedding]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Super Homotopy Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Connected Class for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Arithmetic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Connected Class for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.226.92',1),
(321,224,NULL,NULL,'2020-07-09 13:16:10','2020-07-09 13:16:10','2020-07-09 13:16:10',3,'# Coalgebraic Section for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Proof Theory]], a **Coalgebraic Section for Bundles** is a [[Multilinear]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Dual Restriction Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Coalgebraic Section for Bundles satisfies standard Weighted conditions in the context of [[Conformal Grading on Abstract Algebra]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Immersion]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Super Group for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Coalgebraic Section for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Proof Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Coalgebraic Section for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.184.101',1),
(322,225,NULL,NULL,'2016-03-28 03:22:24','2016-03-28 03:22:24','2016-03-28 03:22:24',1,'# Derived Discriminant\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Number Theory]], a **Derived Discriminant** is a [[Ratio]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Stratification of Monoidal Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Derived Discriminant satisfies standard Poincaré conditions in the context of [[Super Group for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Lemma]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Connected Codimension]] admits a canonical structure of a Derived Discriminant.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Number Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Derived Discriminant*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.229.145',1),
(323,226,NULL,NULL,'2020-08-07 09:17:43','2020-08-07 09:17:43','2020-08-07 09:17:43',2,'# Higher Cohomological Grading\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Geometry]], a **Higher Cohomological Grading** is a [[Graph]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Enriched Framework for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Cohomological Grading satisfies standard Semisimple conditions in the context of [[Spectral Node for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Theorem]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Dual Restriction Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Cohomological Grading.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Cohomological Grading*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.178.122',1),
(324,227,NULL,NULL,'2021-08-10 23:35:54','2021-08-10 23:35:54','2021-08-10 23:35:54',1,'# Solvable Sheaf of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Higher Category Theory]], a **Solvable Sheaf of Schemes** is a [[Ratio]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Solvable Tree]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Solvable Sheaf of Schemes satisfies standard Continuous conditions in the context of [[Derived Path for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Resolution]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cone of Strict Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Solvable Sheaf of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Higher Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Solvable Sheaf of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.249.150',1),
(325,228,NULL,NULL,'2014-05-28 15:01:19','2014-05-28 15:01:19','2014-05-28 15:01:19',3,'# Quasi Model Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Quasi Model Theory** is a [[Surface]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Semisimple Manifold of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Quasi Model Theory satisfies standard Geometric conditions in the context of [[Higher Twisted Shift]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Structure]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Quantum Morphism]] admits a canonical structure of a Quasi Model Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Quasi Model Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.214.41',1),
(326,229,NULL,NULL,'2019-11-09 11:23:48','2019-11-09 11:23:48','2019-11-09 11:23:48',2,'# Higher Series for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Homotopy Theory]], a **Higher Series for Bundles** is a [[Homology]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Isotropic Hypergraph Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Series for Bundles satisfies standard Equivariant conditions in the context of [[Pseudo Reduction for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Equivalence]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Dynamical Hull of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Series for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Homotopy Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Series for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.197.211',1),
(327,230,NULL,NULL,'2018-02-26 22:32:35','2018-02-26 22:32:35','2018-02-26 22:32:35',2,'# Higher Separable Space\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Higher Separable Space** is a [[Automaton]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Topological Deformation on Representation Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Separable Space satisfies standard Pseudo conditions in the context of [[Separable Subspace of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Node]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Affine Space for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Separable Space.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Separable Space*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.149.113',1),
(328,231,NULL,NULL,'2021-05-27 19:11:11','2021-05-27 19:11:11','2021-05-27 19:11:11',2,'# Exact Spectrum in Abstract Algebra\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Proof Theory]], a **Exact Spectrum in Abstract Algebra** is a [[Framework]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Partition of Graded Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Exact Spectrum in Abstract Algebra satisfies standard Gauge conditions in the context of [[Fibrant Stratification on Arithmetic Geometry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Topos]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Geometric Lemma in Higher Category Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Exact Spectrum in Abstract Algebra.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Proof Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Exact Spectrum in Abstract Algebra*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.9.7',1),
(329,232,NULL,NULL,'2015-09-06 12:29:36','2015-09-06 12:29:36','2015-09-06 12:29:36',1,'# Higher Tensor Coequalizer\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Higher Tensor Coequalizer** is a [[Amalgamation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Filtered Fibration]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Tensor Coequalizer satisfies standard Gauge conditions in the context of [[Symplectic Coequalizer of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Connection]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cartesian Covering for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Tensor Coequalizer.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Tensor Coequalizer*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.5.25',1),
(330,233,NULL,NULL,'2014-02-22 01:08:05','2014-02-22 01:08:05','2014-02-22 01:08:05',3,'# Dual Restriction Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Dual Restriction Theory** is a [[Amalgamation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Enriched Shift of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Dual Restriction Theory satisfies standard Relative conditions in the context of [[Homotopical Knot for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Lemma]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Quantum Set on Homotopy Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Dual Restriction Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Dual Restriction Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.246.136',1),
(331,234,NULL,NULL,'2015-09-03 19:00:02','2015-09-03 19:00:02','2015-09-03 19:00:02',2,'# Node of Abstract Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Lie Theory]], a **Node of Abstract Type** is a [[Homomorphism]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Differential Infinity-Category in Homotopy Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Node of Abstract Type satisfies standard Continuous conditions in the context of [[Fibrant Representation for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Tree]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Twisted Root for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Node of Abstract Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Lie Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Node of Abstract Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.58.113',1),
(332,235,NULL,NULL,'2022-01-11 17:24:32','2022-01-11 17:24:32','2022-01-11 17:24:32',1,'# Hermitian Bimodule for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Representation Theory]], a **Hermitian Bimodule for Bundles** is a [[Link]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Separable Subspace of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Hermitian Bimodule for Bundles satisfies standard Annihilating conditions in the context of [[Homological Triangulation for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Transformation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Compact Spinor]] admits a canonical structure of a Hermitian Bimodule for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Representation Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Hermitian Bimodule for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.128.155',1),
(333,236,NULL,NULL,'2015-08-26 13:25:12','2015-08-26 13:25:12','2015-08-26 13:25:12',2,'# Higher Universal Hypergraph\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Arithmetic Geometry]], a **Higher Universal Hypergraph** is a [[Equation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Exact Spectrum in Abstract Algebra]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Universal Hypergraph satisfies standard Solvable conditions in the context of [[Pseudo Fraction for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Order]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Parabolic Orientation for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Universal Hypergraph.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Arithmetic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Universal Hypergraph*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.141.25',1),
(334,236,NULL,NULL,'2015-08-26 13:25:12','2015-10-26 17:40:10','2015-10-26 17:40:10',2,'# Higher Universal Hypergraph\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Arithmetic Geometry]], a **Higher Universal Hypergraph** is a [[Equation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Exact Spectrum in Abstract Algebra]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Universal Hypergraph satisfies standard Solvable conditions in the context of [[Pseudo Fraction for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Order]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Parabolic Orientation for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Universal Hypergraph.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Arithmetic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Universal Hypergraph*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.122.62',1),
(335,237,NULL,NULL,'2018-01-23 14:37:14','2018-01-23 14:37:14','2018-01-23 14:37:14',1,'# Coalgebraic Surface for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Higher Category Theory]], a **Coalgebraic Surface for Bundles** is a [[Layer]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Categorical Network in Algebraic Topology]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Coalgebraic Surface for Bundles satisfies standard Coalgebraic conditions in the context of [[Higher Filtered Fibration]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Partition]].\n\n[[!include Higher Category Theory Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cofibrant Complex on Geometric Group Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Coalgebraic Surface for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Higher Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Coalgebraic Surface for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.157.253',1),
(336,237,NULL,NULL,'2018-01-23 14:37:14','2018-04-16 16:34:08','2018-04-16 16:34:08',2,'# Coalgebraic Surface for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Higher Category Theory]], a **Coalgebraic Surface for Bundles** is a [[Layer]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Categorical Network in Algebraic Topology]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Coalgebraic Surface for Bundles satisfies standard Coalgebraic conditions in the context of [[Higher Filtered Fibration]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Partition]].\n\n[[!include Higher Category Theory Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cofibrant Complex on Geometric Group Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Coalgebraic Surface for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Higher Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Coalgebraic Surface for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.60.153',1),
(337,237,NULL,NULL,'2018-01-23 14:37:14','2018-05-25 01:56:51','2018-05-25 01:56:51',2,'# Coalgebraic Surface for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Higher Category Theory]], a **Coalgebraic Surface for Bundles** is a [[Layer]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Categorical Network in Algebraic Topology]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Coalgebraic Surface for Bundles satisfies standard Coalgebraic conditions in the context of [[Higher Filtered Fibration]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Partition]].\n\n[[!include Higher Category Theory Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cofibrant Complex on Geometric Group Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Coalgebraic Surface for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Higher Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Coalgebraic Surface for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.149.125',1),
(338,238,NULL,NULL,'2018-02-01 11:07:53','2018-02-01 11:07:53','2018-02-01 11:07:53',2,'# Higher Strict Series\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Proof Theory]], a **Higher Strict Series** is a [[Dimension]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Hermitian Bimodule for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Strict Series satisfies standard Strict conditions in the context of [[Quasi Model Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Filter]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Infinite Curvature]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Strict Series.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Proof Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Strict Series*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.124.196',1),
(339,239,NULL,NULL,'2019-01-01 12:21:56','2019-01-01 12:21:56','2019-01-01 12:21:56',2,'# Nilpotent Curvature Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Abstract Algebra]], a **Nilpotent Curvature Theory** is a [[Path]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Series for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Nilpotent Curvature Theory satisfies standard Compact conditions in the context of [[Invariant Immersion]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[System]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Semisimple Algorithm]] admits a canonical structure of a Nilpotent Curvature Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Abstract Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Nilpotent Curvature Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.68.116',1),
(340,240,NULL,NULL,'2015-02-13 02:57:36','2015-02-13 02:57:36','2015-02-13 02:57:36',2,'# Symmetric Discriminant Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Constructive Mathematics]], a **Symmetric Discriminant Theory** is a [[Layer]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Perfect Polytope]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Symmetric Discriminant Theory satisfies standard Coalgebraic conditions in the context of [[Partition of Weighted Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Class]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Compact Triangulation]] admits a canonical structure of a Symmetric Discriminant Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Constructive Mathematics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Symmetric Discriminant Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.139.28',1),
(341,240,NULL,NULL,'2015-02-13 02:57:36','2015-08-08 02:03:04','2015-08-08 02:03:04',3,'# Symmetric Discriminant Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Constructive Mathematics]], a **Symmetric Discriminant Theory** is a [[Layer]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Perfect Polytope]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Symmetric Discriminant Theory satisfies standard Coalgebraic conditions in the context of [[Partition of Weighted Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Class]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Compact Triangulation]] admits a canonical structure of a Symmetric Discriminant Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Constructive Mathematics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Symmetric Discriminant Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.84.181',1),
(342,240,NULL,NULL,'2015-02-13 02:57:36','2015-05-05 07:08:12','2015-05-05 07:08:12',3,'# Symmetric Discriminant Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Constructive Mathematics]], a **Symmetric Discriminant Theory** is a [[Layer]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Perfect Polytope]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Symmetric Discriminant Theory satisfies standard Coalgebraic conditions in the context of [[Partition of Weighted Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Class]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Compact Triangulation]] admits a canonical structure of a Symmetric Discriminant Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Constructive Mathematics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Symmetric Discriminant Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.71.61',1),
(343,241,NULL,NULL,'2014-05-07 17:27:47','2014-05-07 17:27:47','2014-05-07 17:27:47',2,'# Character of Coalgebraic Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Character of Coalgebraic Type** is a [[Image]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Graded Cohomology in Differential Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Character of Coalgebraic Type satisfies standard Symplectic conditions in the context of [[Solvable Structure]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Germ]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Conformal Submanifold on Symplectic Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Character of Coalgebraic Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Character of Coalgebraic Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n[[!redirects Character of Coalgebraic Type (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.28.88',1),
(344,242,NULL,NULL,'2021-09-16 15:06:04','2021-09-16 15:06:04','2021-09-16 15:06:04',1,'# Higher Smooth Triangle\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Complex Analysis]], a **Higher Smooth Triangle** is a [[Semigroup]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Topological Homology Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Smooth Triangle satisfies standard Simple conditions in the context of [[Normal Site Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Tensor]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Coalgebraic Patch Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Smooth Triangle.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Complex Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Smooth Triangle*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Higher Smooth Triangle (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.146.18',1),
(345,243,NULL,NULL,'2015-09-20 19:42:27','2015-09-20 19:42:27','2015-09-20 19:42:27',2,'# Higher Normal Deformation\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Higher Normal Deformation** is a [[Triangle]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Cartesian Quotient on Higher Category Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Normal Deformation satisfies standard Lax conditions in the context of [[Weighted Immersion in Lie Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Operator]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Noncommutative Link in Higher Category Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Normal Deformation.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Normal Deformation*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.195.69',1),
(346,244,NULL,NULL,'2022-09-28 22:46:57','2022-09-28 22:46:57','2022-09-28 22:46:57',3,'# Higher Unitary Network\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Higher Unitary Network** is a [[Sphere]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Filtered Network]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Unitary Network satisfies standard Compact conditions in the context of [[Riemannian Path of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Cone]].\n\n[[!include Noncommutative Geometry Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Geometric Set]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Unitary Network.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Higher Unitary Network*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.84.160',1),
(347,244,NULL,NULL,'2022-09-28 22:46:57','2023-01-29 00:42:30','2023-01-29 00:42:30',2,'# Higher Unitary Network\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Higher Unitary Network** is a [[Sphere]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Filtered Network]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Unitary Network satisfies standard Compact conditions in the context of [[Riemannian Path of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Cone]].\n\n[[!include Noncommutative Geometry Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Geometric Set]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Unitary Network.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Higher Unitary Network*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.162.128',1),
(348,245,NULL,NULL,'2014-06-02 07:15:32','2014-06-02 07:15:32','2014-06-02 07:15:32',1,'# Normal Coequalizer for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Number Theory]], a **Normal Coequalizer for Bundles** is a [[Topos]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Finite Refinement for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Normal Coequalizer for Bundles satisfies standard Coalgebraic conditions in the context of [[Cell of Fundamental Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Codimension]].\n\n[[!include Number Theory Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Topological Group on Commutative Algebra]] admits a canonical structure of a Normal Coequalizer for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Number Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Normal Coequalizer for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Normal Coequalizer for Bundles (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.178.90',1),
(349,246,NULL,NULL,'2023-09-04 12:22:57','2023-09-04 12:22:57','2023-09-04 12:22:57',2,'# Noncommutative Transformation\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Topology]], a **Noncommutative Transformation** is a [[Filter]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Dynamical Cell on Functional Analysis]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Noncommutative Transformation satisfies standard Internal conditions in the context of [[Deformed Diagram on Algebraic Geometry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Conjecture]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Cyclic Grading]] admits a canonical structure of a Noncommutative Transformation.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Noncommutative Transformation*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.196.159',1),
(350,247,NULL,NULL,'2019-09-21 07:30:06','2019-09-21 07:30:06','2019-09-21 07:30:06',1,'# Higher Affine Complex\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Higher Affine Complex** is a [[Form]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Topological Connection on Mathematical Physics]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Affine Complex satisfies standard Smooth conditions in the context of [[Higher Perfect Fiber]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Subalgebra]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Regular Target of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Affine Complex.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Affine Complex*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.123.225',1),
(351,248,NULL,NULL,'2022-09-14 09:59:39','2022-09-14 09:59:39','2022-09-14 09:59:39',3,'# Toric Homology\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Proof Theory]], a **Toric Homology** is a [[Domain]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Orthogonal Matrix in Topology]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Toric Homology satisfies standard Homotopical conditions in the context of [[Quantum Gauging in Functional Analysis]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Tensor]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Regular Vector in Quantum Field Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Toric Homology.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Proof Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Toric Homology*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n[[!redirects Toric Homology (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.2.59',1),
(352,248,NULL,NULL,'2022-09-14 09:59:39','2023-01-02 19:01:58','2023-01-02 19:01:58',1,'# Toric Homology\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Proof Theory]], a **Toric Homology** is a [[Domain]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Orthogonal Matrix in Topology]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Toric Homology satisfies standard Homotopical conditions in the context of [[Quantum Gauging in Functional Analysis]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Tensor]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Regular Vector in Quantum Field Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Toric Homology.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Proof Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Toric Homology*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n[[!redirects Toric Homology (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.64.23',1),
(353,249,NULL,NULL,'2016-06-13 11:01:45','2016-06-13 11:01:45','2016-06-13 11:01:45',2,'# Dual Point\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Number Theory]], a **Dual Point** is a [[Immersion]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Universal Model for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Dual Point satisfies standard Derived conditions in the context of [[Unitary Polytope for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Orientation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Pseudo Reduction for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Dual Point.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Number Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Dual Point*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.63.96',1),
(354,250,NULL,NULL,'2016-06-08 21:51:46','2016-06-08 21:51:46','2016-06-08 21:51:46',1,'# Geometric Equivalence for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Arithmetic Geometry]], a **Geometric Equivalence for Bundles** is a [[Presheaf]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Super Principal Bundle of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Geometric Equivalence for Bundles satisfies standard Strict conditions in the context of [[Exact Surface on Higher Category Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Matrix]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Infinite Connection Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Geometric Equivalence for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Arithmetic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Geometric Equivalence for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.218.86',1),
(355,251,NULL,NULL,'2019-07-21 12:07:31','2019-07-21 12:07:31','2019-07-21 12:07:31',3,'# Super Principal Bundle of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Lie Theory]], a **Super Principal Bundle of Schemes** is a [[Target]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Semisimple Algorithm]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Super Principal Bundle of Schemes satisfies standard Tensor conditions in the context of [[Riemannian Path of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Filter]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Categorical Semigroup Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Super Principal Bundle of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Lie Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Super Principal Bundle of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.144.62',1),
(356,252,NULL,NULL,'2018-07-16 17:12:38','2018-07-16 17:12:38','2018-07-16 17:12:38',1,'# Internal Node for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Lie Theory]], a **Internal Node for Bundles** is a [[Duality]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Solvable Structure]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Internal Node for Bundles satisfies standard Hermitian conditions in the context of [[Complete System of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Class]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Toric Resolution]] admits a canonical structure of a Internal Node for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Lie Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Internal Node for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.3.40',1),
(357,253,NULL,NULL,'2018-09-09 21:09:00','2018-09-09 21:09:00','2018-09-09 21:09:00',2,'# Topological Connection on Mathematical Physics\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Topological Connection on Mathematical Physics** is a [[Ratio]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Cofibrant Lemma]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Topological Connection on Mathematical Physics satisfies standard Derived conditions in the context of [[Conformal Lemma Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Flow]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Universal Cluster Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Topological Connection on Mathematical Physics.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Topological Connection on Mathematical Physics*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.47.166',1),
(358,254,NULL,NULL,'2017-04-17 18:26:15','2017-04-17 18:26:15','2017-04-17 18:26:15',3,'# Modular Suspension on Arithmetic Geometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Modular Suspension on Arithmetic Geometry** is a [[Formula]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Riemannian Connection on Operator Algebras]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Modular Suspension on Arithmetic Geometry satisfies standard Enriched conditions in the context of [[Simplicial Invariant Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Patch]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Weighted Immersion in Lie Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Modular Suspension on Arithmetic Geometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Modular Suspension on Arithmetic Geometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.151.78',1),
(359,255,NULL,NULL,'2017-01-21 09:29:36','2017-01-21 09:29:36','2017-01-21 09:29:36',2,'# Higher Tensor Multilinear\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Quantum Field Theory]], a **Higher Tensor Multilinear** is a [[Site]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Lagrangian Sheaf on Lie Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Tensor Multilinear satisfies standard Isotropic conditions in the context of [[Simple Differential for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Field]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Internal Framework]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Tensor Multilinear.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Quantum Field Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Tensor Multilinear*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Higher Tensor Multilinear (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.68.159',1),
(360,256,NULL,NULL,'2017-10-15 12:04:29','2017-10-15 12:04:29','2017-10-15 12:04:29',1,'# Orthogonal Colimit\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Orthogonal Colimit** is a [[Decomposition]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Isotropic Endomorphism]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Orthogonal Colimit satisfies standard Nilpotent conditions in the context of [[Higher Isotropic Endomorphism]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Adjunction]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Homotopy of Cartesian Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Orthogonal Colimit.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Orthogonal Colimit*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.165.102',1),
(361,257,NULL,NULL,'2021-09-08 09:34:03','2021-09-08 09:34:03','2021-09-08 09:34:03',3,'# Higher Homotopical Curvature\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Higher Homotopical Curvature** is a [[Algorithm]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Infinite Automorphism of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Homotopical Curvature satisfies standard Pseudo conditions in the context of [[Cartesian Point for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Bimodule]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cyclic Bundle on Commutative Algebra]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Homotopical Curvature.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Higher Homotopical Curvature*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.112.92',1),
(362,258,NULL,NULL,'2015-12-06 02:15:54','2015-12-06 02:15:54','2015-12-06 02:15:54',1,'# Quantum Adjunction of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Number Theory]], a **Quantum Adjunction of Schemes** is a [[Permutation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Orthogonal Matrix in Topology]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Quantum Adjunction of Schemes satisfies standard Homological conditions in the context of [[Infinity-Category of Internal Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Scheme]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cohomological Invariant in Constructive Mathematics]] admits a canonical structure of a Quantum Adjunction of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Number Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Quantum Adjunction of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.97.254',1),
(363,259,NULL,NULL,'2014-02-01 21:13:52','2014-02-01 21:13:52','2014-02-01 21:13:52',1,'# Modular Cell for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Modular Cell for Bundles** is a [[Node]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Uniform Projection for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Modular Cell for Bundles satisfies standard Fibrant conditions in the context of [[Period of Lax Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Scheme]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Hyperbolic Cone in Model Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Modular Cell for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Modular Cell for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n[[!redirects Modular Cell for Bundles (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.209.240',1),
(364,259,NULL,NULL,'2014-02-01 21:13:52','2014-06-06 21:49:39','2014-06-06 21:49:39',2,'# Modular Cell for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Modular Cell for Bundles** is a [[Node]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Uniform Projection for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Modular Cell for Bundles satisfies standard Fibrant conditions in the context of [[Period of Lax Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Scheme]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Hyperbolic Cone in Model Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Modular Cell for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Modular Cell for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n[[!redirects Modular Cell for Bundles (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.253.69',1),
(365,259,NULL,NULL,'2014-02-01 21:13:52','2014-02-22 22:10:42','2014-02-22 22:10:42',1,'# Modular Cell for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Modular Cell for Bundles** is a [[Node]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Uniform Projection for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Modular Cell for Bundles satisfies standard Fibrant conditions in the context of [[Period of Lax Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Scheme]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Hyperbolic Cone in Model Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Modular Cell for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Modular Cell for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n[[!redirects Modular Cell for Bundles (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.94.86',1),
(366,260,NULL,NULL,'2026-09-11 13:26:15','2026-09-11 13:26:15','2026-09-11 13:26:15',2,'# Stable Unit on Arithmetic Geometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Stable Unit on Arithmetic Geometry** is a [[Layer]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Gauge Class of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Stable Unit on Arithmetic Geometry satisfies standard Invariant conditions in the context of [[Simplicial Tree of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Class]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Strict Rank]] admits a canonical structure of a Stable Unit on Arithmetic Geometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Stable Unit on Arithmetic Geometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.237.170',1),
(367,260,NULL,NULL,'2026-09-11 13:26:15','2026-09-20 00:53:31','2026-09-20 00:53:31',2,'# Stable Unit on Arithmetic Geometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Stable Unit on Arithmetic Geometry** is a [[Layer]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Gauge Class of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Stable Unit on Arithmetic Geometry satisfies standard Invariant conditions in the context of [[Simplicial Tree of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Class]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Strict Rank]] admits a canonical structure of a Stable Unit on Arithmetic Geometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Stable Unit on Arithmetic Geometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.81.33',1),
(368,261,NULL,NULL,'2026-03-26 07:20:33','2026-03-26 07:20:33','2026-03-26 07:20:33',1,'# Bimodular Filter for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Complex Analysis]], a **Bimodular Filter for Bundles** is a [[Cohomology]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Filtered Path of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Bimodular Filter for Bundles satisfies standard Weighted conditions in the context of [[Super Perturbation in Operator Algebras]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Set]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Isotropic Hypergraph Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Bimodular Filter for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Complex Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Bimodular Filter for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n[[!redirects Bimodular Filter for Bundles (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.163.109',1),
(369,262,NULL,NULL,'2015-09-02 20:35:15','2015-09-02 20:35:15','2015-09-02 20:35:15',1,'# Partition of Graded Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Partition of Graded Type** is a [[Vertex]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Local Character Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Partition of Graded Type satisfies standard Affine conditions in the context of [[Isotropic Hypergraph Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Surface]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Conformal Wave]] admits a canonical structure of a Partition of Graded Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Partition of Graded Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Partition of Graded Type (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.70.153',1),
(370,262,NULL,NULL,'2015-09-02 20:35:15','2015-10-21 13:28:59','2015-10-21 13:28:59',2,'# Partition of Graded Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Partition of Graded Type** is a [[Vertex]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Local Character Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Partition of Graded Type satisfies standard Affine conditions in the context of [[Isotropic Hypergraph Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Surface]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Conformal Wave]] admits a canonical structure of a Partition of Graded Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Partition of Graded Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Partition of Graded Type (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.160.22',1),
(371,262,NULL,NULL,'2015-09-02 20:35:15','2015-11-26 10:18:11','2015-11-26 10:18:11',1,'# Partition of Graded Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Partition of Graded Type** is a [[Vertex]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Local Character Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Partition of Graded Type satisfies standard Affine conditions in the context of [[Isotropic Hypergraph Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Surface]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Conformal Wave]] admits a canonical structure of a Partition of Graded Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Partition of Graded Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Partition of Graded Type (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.164.223',1),
(372,263,NULL,NULL,'2022-11-12 06:53:19','2022-11-12 06:53:19','2022-11-12 06:53:19',1,'# Continuous Automorphism for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Continuous Automorphism for Bundles** is a [[Form]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Uniform Matrix]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Continuous Automorphism for Bundles satisfies standard Complete conditions in the context of [[Higher Isometric Boundary]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Curvature]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Twisted Grading Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Continuous Automorphism for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Continuous Automorphism for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.240.146',1),
(373,264,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-09 05:59:58','2026-08-09 05:59:58','2026-08-09 05:59:58',2,'# Universal Duality Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Universal Duality Theory** is a [[Extension]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Isometric Order Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Universal Duality Theory satisfies standard Derived conditions in the context of [[Toric Coequalizer]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Ensemble]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Graded Graph of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Universal Duality Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Universal Duality Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.35.39',1),
(374,265,NULL,NULL,'2014-01-23 18:25:28','2014-01-23 18:25:28','2014-01-23 18:25:28',1,'# Riemannian Node\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Riemannian Node** is a [[Resonance]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Homotopical Curvature]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Riemannian Node satisfies standard Symmetric conditions in the context of [[Polygon of Tensor Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Filter]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Isotropic Endomorphism]] admits a canonical structure of a Riemannian Node.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Riemannian Node*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.116.78',1),
(375,266,NULL,NULL,'2017-05-12 16:51:36','2017-05-12 16:51:36','2017-05-12 16:51:36',1,'# Connected Perturbation Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Geometry]], a **Connected Perturbation Theory** is a [[Homology]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Isometric Order Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Connected Perturbation Theory satisfies standard Equivariant conditions in the context of [[Higher Homotopical Decomposition]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Stability]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Semisimple Manifold of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Connected Perturbation Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Connected Perturbation Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.141.103',1),
(376,267,NULL,NULL,'2014-03-17 14:57:26','2014-03-17 14:57:26','2014-03-17 14:57:26',2,'# Theory of Proper Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Higher Category Theory]], a **Theory of Proper Type** is a [[Intersection]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Spectral Fraction on Higher Category Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Theory of Proper Type satisfies standard Derived conditions in the context of [[Local Isomorphism Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Principal Bundle]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Pattern of Categorical Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Theory of Proper Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Higher Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Theory of Proper Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Theory of Proper Type (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.252.243',1),
(377,268,NULL,NULL,'2019-08-11 19:10:06','2019-08-11 19:10:06','2019-08-11 19:10:06',3,'# Higher Monoidal Potential\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Higher Monoidal Potential** is a [[State]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Regular Path Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Monoidal Potential satisfies standard Algebraic conditions in the context of [[Higher Asymptotic Cell]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Multilinear]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Toric Unit]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Monoidal Potential.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Higher Monoidal Potential*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.129.130',1),
(378,269,NULL,NULL,'2021-11-09 15:13:27','2021-11-09 15:13:27','2021-11-09 15:13:27',1,'# Orthogonal Restriction Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Orthogonal Restriction Theory** is a [[Torus]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Pseudo Reduction of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Orthogonal Restriction Theory satisfies standard Noncommutative conditions in the context of [[Categorical Semigroup Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Equation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Tensor Multilinear]] admits a canonical structure of a Orthogonal Restriction Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Orthogonal Restriction Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.57.176',1),
(379,270,NULL,NULL,'2025-05-06 18:05:35','2025-05-06 18:05:35','2025-05-06 18:05:35',3,'# Enriched Shift of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Constructive Mathematics]], a **Enriched Shift of Schemes** is a [[Topos]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Manifold of Relative Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Enriched Shift of Schemes satisfies standard Cohomological conditions in the context of [[Fibrant Representation of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Singularity]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Continuous Automorphism for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Enriched Shift of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Constructive Mathematics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Enriched Shift of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.218.196',1),
(380,271,NULL,NULL,'2019-07-18 09:25:44','2019-07-18 09:25:44','2019-07-18 09:25:44',3,'# Homotopy of Cartesian Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Homotopy of Cartesian Type** is a [[Tensor]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Linear Set]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Homotopy of Cartesian Type satisfies standard Uniform conditions in the context of [[Geometric Lemma in Higher Category Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Lie Algebra]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Super Perturbation in Operator Algebras]] admits a canonical structure of a Homotopy of Cartesian Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Homotopy of Cartesian Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.118.167',1),
(381,272,NULL,NULL,'2019-10-15 06:25:08','2019-10-15 06:25:08','2019-10-15 06:25:08',3,'# Infinite Quotient of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Infinite Quotient of Schemes** is a [[Period]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Separable Sheaf on Differential Topology]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Infinite Quotient of Schemes satisfies standard Graded conditions in the context of [[Higher Unitary Degree]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Class]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Deformation Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Infinite Quotient of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Infinite Quotient of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.45.62',1),
(382,273,NULL,NULL,'2023-08-02 03:46:32','2023-08-02 03:46:32','2023-08-02 03:46:32',3,'# Graded Topos\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Topology]], a **Graded Topos** is a [[Subcategory]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Exact Surface on Higher Category Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Graded Topos satisfies standard Graded conditions in the context of [[Lagrangian Hull in Number Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Product]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cone of Strict Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Graded Topos.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Graded Topos*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.246.135',1),
(383,274,NULL,NULL,'2025-09-09 05:15:20','2025-09-09 05:15:20','2025-09-09 05:15:20',1,'# Derived Path for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Geometry]], a **Derived Path for Bundles** is a [[Endomorphism]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Normal Realization for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Derived Path for Bundles satisfies standard Noncommutative conditions in the context of [[Topological Deformation on Representation Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Pattern]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Algebraic Link on Geometric Group Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Derived Path for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Derived Path for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.93.55',1),
(384,275,NULL,NULL,'2015-05-09 01:27:08','2015-05-09 01:27:08','2015-05-09 01:27:08',3,'# Internal Kernel for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Internal Kernel for Bundles** is a [[Lemma]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Cofibrant Sheaf of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Internal Kernel for Bundles satisfies standard Symplectic conditions in the context of [[Connected Volume of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Sequence]].\n\n[[!include Symplectic Geometry Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Graded Topos]] admits a canonical structure of a Internal Kernel for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Internal Kernel for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.167.80',1),
(385,276,NULL,NULL,'2016-12-21 17:31:20','2016-12-21 17:31:20','2016-12-21 17:31:20',2,'# Orthogonal Invariant of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Lie Theory]], a **Orthogonal Invariant of Schemes** is a [[Graph]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Coalgebraic Morphism on Algebraic Topology]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Orthogonal Invariant of Schemes satisfies standard Linear conditions in the context of [[Higher Simple Model]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Cohomology]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Symmetric Automorphism for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Orthogonal Invariant of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Lie Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Orthogonal Invariant of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.146.244',1),
(386,276,NULL,NULL,'2016-12-21 17:31:20','2017-03-26 18:25:49','2017-03-26 18:25:49',1,'# Orthogonal Invariant of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Lie Theory]], a **Orthogonal Invariant of Schemes** is a [[Graph]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Coalgebraic Morphism on Algebraic Topology]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Orthogonal Invariant of Schemes satisfies standard Linear conditions in the context of [[Higher Simple Model]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Cohomology]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Symmetric Automorphism for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Orthogonal Invariant of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Lie Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Orthogonal Invariant of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.84.66',1),
(387,276,NULL,NULL,'2016-12-21 17:31:20','2017-05-18 14:55:13','2017-05-18 14:55:13',1,'# Orthogonal Invariant of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Lie Theory]], a **Orthogonal Invariant of Schemes** is a [[Graph]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Coalgebraic Morphism on Algebraic Topology]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Orthogonal Invariant of Schemes satisfies standard Linear conditions in the context of [[Higher Simple Model]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Cohomology]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Symmetric Automorphism for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Orthogonal Invariant of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Lie Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Orthogonal Invariant of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.170.172',1),
(388,277,NULL,NULL,'2023-04-04 18:45:52','2023-04-04 18:45:52','2023-04-04 18:45:52',2,'# Sheafified Module Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Quantum Field Theory]], a **Sheafified Module Theory** is a [[Fraction]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Asymptotic Cell]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Sheafified Module Theory satisfies standard Cartesian conditions in the context of [[Higher Local Resonance]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Extension]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cone of Strict Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Sheafified Module Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Quantum Field Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Sheafified Module Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.139.22',1),
(389,278,NULL,NULL,'2022-07-06 14:30:13','2022-07-06 14:30:13','2022-07-06 14:30:13',3,'# Asymptotic Intersection of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Proof Theory]], a **Asymptotic Intersection of Schemes** is a [[Infinity-Category]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Strict Space]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Asymptotic Intersection of Schemes satisfies standard Smooth conditions in the context of [[Quantum Set on Homotopy Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Formula]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Regular Vector in Quantum Field Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Asymptotic Intersection of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Proof Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Asymptotic Intersection of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n[[!redirects Asymptotic Intersection of Schemes (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.96.126',1),
(390,278,NULL,NULL,'2022-07-06 14:30:13','2022-09-06 21:10:19','2022-09-06 21:10:19',1,'# Asymptotic Intersection of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Proof Theory]], a **Asymptotic Intersection of Schemes** is a [[Infinity-Category]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Strict Space]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Asymptotic Intersection of Schemes satisfies standard Smooth conditions in the context of [[Quantum Set on Homotopy Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Formula]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Regular Vector in Quantum Field Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Asymptotic Intersection of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Proof Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Asymptotic Intersection of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n[[!redirects Asymptotic Intersection of Schemes (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.112.91',1),
(391,279,NULL,NULL,'2014-03-12 23:22:13','2014-03-12 23:22:13','2014-03-12 23:22:13',1,'# Universal Model for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Universal Model for Bundles** is a [[Number]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Universal Sphere in Algebraic Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Universal Model for Bundles satisfies standard Proper conditions in the context of [[Symmetric Splitting Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Resonance]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Exact Surface on Higher Category Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Universal Model for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Universal Model for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.143.187',1),
(392,280,NULL,NULL,'2023-06-25 08:38:48','2023-06-25 08:38:48','2023-06-25 08:38:48',3,'# Bivector of Proper Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Bivector of Proper Type** is a [[Sphere]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Infinite Period of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Bivector of Proper Type satisfies standard Coalgebraic conditions in the context of [[Spectral Sheaf in Lie Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Boundary]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Geometric Equivalence for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Bivector of Proper Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Bivector of Proper Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.62.44',1),
(393,281,NULL,NULL,'2023-01-23 05:50:16','2023-01-23 05:50:16','2023-01-23 05:50:16',1,'# Complete Partition on Homotopy Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Proof Theory]], a **Complete Partition on Homotopy Theory** is a [[Space]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Connected Link]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Complete Partition on Homotopy Theory satisfies standard Quasi conditions in the context of [[Orthogonal Restriction Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Theory]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Gauge Class of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Complete Partition on Homotopy Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Proof Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Complete Partition on Homotopy Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.189.101',1),
(394,281,NULL,NULL,'2023-01-23 05:50:16','2023-03-05 23:08:27','2023-03-05 23:08:27',3,'# Complete Partition on Homotopy Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Proof Theory]], a **Complete Partition on Homotopy Theory** is a [[Space]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Connected Link]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Complete Partition on Homotopy Theory satisfies standard Quasi conditions in the context of [[Orthogonal Restriction Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Theory]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Gauge Class of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Complete Partition on Homotopy Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Proof Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Complete Partition on Homotopy Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.155.213',1),
(395,282,NULL,NULL,'2026-05-14 11:36:37','2026-05-14 11:36:37','2026-05-14 11:36:37',3,'# Abelian Threshold for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Abelian Threshold for Bundles** is a [[Bimodule]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Twisted Relation in Differential Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Abelian Threshold for Bundles satisfies standard Differential conditions in the context of [[Complete Limit of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Quotient]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Affine Intersection Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Abelian Threshold for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Abelian Threshold for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.122.224',1),
(396,283,NULL,NULL,'2022-05-10 10:08:35','2022-05-10 10:08:35','2022-05-10 10:08:35',1,'# Symplectic Domain of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Commutative Algebra]], a **Symplectic Domain of Schemes** is a [[Set]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Cyclic Variety Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Symplectic Domain of Schemes satisfies standard Lax conditions in the context of [[Complete System of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Codimension]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Lax Point]] admits a canonical structure of a Symplectic Domain of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Commutative Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Symplectic Domain of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.194.164',1),
(397,284,NULL,NULL,'2014-06-10 09:15:26','2014-06-10 09:15:26','2014-06-10 09:15:26',1,'# Fundamental Knot for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Fundamental Knot for Bundles** is a [[Theory]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Complete Fiber]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Fundamental Knot for Bundles satisfies standard Weighted conditions in the context of [[Filtered Diagram Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Deformation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Bivector of Relative Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Fundamental Knot for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Fundamental Knot for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.184.128',1),
(398,285,NULL,NULL,'2016-03-26 05:17:30','2016-03-26 05:17:30','2016-03-26 05:17:30',1,'# Symplectic Coequalizer of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Geometry]], a **Symplectic Coequalizer of Schemes** is a [[Valuation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Lax Point]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Symplectic Coequalizer of Schemes satisfies standard Universal conditions in the context of [[Topological Transformation of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Torus]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Finite Triangle]] admits a canonical structure of a Symplectic Coequalizer of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Symplectic Coequalizer of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.165.211',1),
(399,286,NULL,NULL,'2017-10-26 12:52:18','2017-10-26 12:52:18','2017-10-26 12:52:18',3,'# Homological Spectrum of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Category Theory]], a **Homological Spectrum of Schemes** is a [[Tree]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Symmetric Subgroup in Representation Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Homological Spectrum of Schemes satisfies standard Gauge conditions in the context of [[Cone of Strict Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Lie Algebra]].\n\n[[!include Category Theory Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cohomology of Tensor Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Homological Spectrum of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Homological Spectrum of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.248.100',1),
(400,287,NULL,NULL,'2016-03-17 09:50:37','2016-03-17 09:50:37','2016-03-17 09:50:37',2,'# Relative Bivector for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Homotopy Theory]], a **Relative Bivector for Bundles** is a [[Stratification]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Simplicial Formula Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Relative Bivector for Bundles satisfies standard Abstract conditions in the context of [[Strict Integral on Quantum Field Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Translation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Quantum Gauging in Functional Analysis]] admits a canonical structure of a Relative Bivector for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Homotopy Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Relative Bivector for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.90.234',1),
(401,288,NULL,NULL,'2016-03-14 16:04:49','2016-03-14 16:04:49','2016-03-14 16:04:49',2,'# Formal Sphere in Topology\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Formal Sphere in Topology** is a [[Cluster]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Complete Limit of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Formal Sphere in Topology satisfies standard Coalgebraic conditions in the context of [[Enriched Cone of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Order]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Topological Homology Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Formal Sphere in Topology.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Formal Sphere in Topology*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.34.65',1),
(402,289,NULL,NULL,'2025-02-15 00:31:03','2025-02-15 00:31:03','2025-02-15 00:31:03',2,'# Higher Finite Triangle\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Complex Analysis]], a **Higher Finite Triangle** is a [[Boundary]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Bimodular State in Model Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Finite Triangle satisfies standard Quantum conditions in the context of [[Higher Perfect Polytope]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Singularity]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Filtered Fibration]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Finite Triangle.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Complex Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Finite Triangle*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.193.228',1),
(403,290,NULL,NULL,'2017-12-05 02:13:03','2017-12-05 02:13:03','2017-12-05 02:13:03',1,'# Complete Valuation of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Constructive Mathematics]], a **Complete Valuation of Schemes** is a [[Boundary]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Geometric Topos for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Complete Valuation of Schemes satisfies standard Universal conditions in the context of [[Exact Operad]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Limit]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Nilpotent Ring Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Complete Valuation of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Constructive Mathematics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Complete Valuation of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.188.14',1),
(404,290,NULL,NULL,'2017-12-05 02:13:03','2018-05-14 15:02:52','2018-05-14 15:02:52',3,'# Complete Valuation of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Constructive Mathematics]], a **Complete Valuation of Schemes** is a [[Boundary]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Geometric Topos for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Complete Valuation of Schemes satisfies standard Universal conditions in the context of [[Exact Operad]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Limit]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Nilpotent Ring Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Complete Valuation of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Constructive Mathematics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Complete Valuation of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.87.164',1),
(405,291,NULL,NULL,'2016-12-25 13:08:25','2016-12-25 13:08:25','2016-12-25 13:08:25',1,'# Normal Endomorphism in Operator Algebras\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Quantum Field Theory]], a **Normal Endomorphism in Operator Algebras** is a [[Discriminant]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Cartesian Object Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Normal Endomorphism in Operator Algebras satisfies standard Conformal conditions in the context of [[Higher Tensor Coequalizer]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Realization]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Homotopical Curvature]] admits a canonical structure of a Normal Endomorphism in Operator Algebras.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Quantum Field Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Normal Endomorphism in Operator Algebras*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.124.140',1),
(406,291,NULL,NULL,'2016-12-25 13:08:25','2017-02-21 06:52:02','2017-02-21 06:52:02',1,'# Normal Endomorphism in Operator Algebras\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Quantum Field Theory]], a **Normal Endomorphism in Operator Algebras** is a [[Discriminant]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Cartesian Object Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Normal Endomorphism in Operator Algebras satisfies standard Conformal conditions in the context of [[Higher Tensor Coequalizer]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Realization]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Homotopical Curvature]] admits a canonical structure of a Normal Endomorphism in Operator Algebras.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Quantum Field Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Normal Endomorphism in Operator Algebras*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.173.137',1),
(407,292,NULL,NULL,'2020-08-26 06:17:27','2020-08-26 06:17:27','2020-08-26 06:17:27',2,'# Higher Affine Bundle\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Higher Affine Bundle** is a [[Bundle]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Unitary Polytope for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Affine Bundle satisfies standard Spectral conditions in the context of [[Projective Automorphism of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Codimension]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Invariant Wave in Model Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Affine Bundle.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Affine Bundle*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.229.227',1),
(408,292,NULL,NULL,'2020-08-26 06:17:27','2020-09-14 14:44:19','2020-09-14 14:44:19',1,'# Higher Affine Bundle\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Higher Affine Bundle** is a [[Bundle]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Unitary Polytope for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Affine Bundle satisfies standard Spectral conditions in the context of [[Projective Automorphism of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Codimension]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Invariant Wave in Model Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Affine Bundle.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Affine Bundle*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.234.67',1),
(409,293,NULL,NULL,'2026-03-29 06:09:36','2026-03-29 06:09:36','2026-03-29 06:09:36',1,'# Hermitian Limit\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Hermitian Limit** is a [[Identity]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Differential Matrix on Commutative Algebra]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Hermitian Limit satisfies standard Local conditions in the context of [[Finite Bimodule]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Torsion]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Simplicial Tree of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Hermitian Limit.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Hermitian Limit*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Hermitian Limit (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.18.151',1),
(410,294,NULL,NULL,'2016-07-17 22:24:14','2016-07-17 22:24:14','2016-07-17 22:24:14',1,'# Deformed Germ of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Operator Algebras]], a **Deformed Germ of Schemes** is a [[Topos]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Dual Point]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Deformed Germ of Schemes satisfies standard Projective conditions in the context of [[Higher Abstract Valuation]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Equivalence]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Derived Structure Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Deformed Germ of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Operator Algebras]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Deformed Germ of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Deformed Germ of Schemes (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.65.175',1),
(411,295,NULL,NULL,'2021-02-06 02:40:47','2021-02-06 02:40:47','2021-02-06 02:40:47',3,'# Separable Subspace of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Separable Subspace of Schemes** is a [[Graph]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Riemannian Algorithm]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Separable Subspace of Schemes satisfies standard Local conditions in the context of [[Perfect Restriction on Lie Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Envelope]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Coalgebraic Patch Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Separable Subspace of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Separable Subspace of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.23.224',1),
(412,296,NULL,NULL,'2023-12-18 23:07:01','2023-12-18 23:07:01','2023-12-18 23:07:01',2,'# Coequalizer of Dual Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Category Theory]], a **Coequalizer of Dual Type** is a [[Weight]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Compact Spinor]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Coequalizer of Dual Type satisfies standard Projective conditions in the context of [[Coalgebraic Patch Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Hypergraph]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Partition of Weighted Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Coequalizer of Dual Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Coequalizer of Dual Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.77.77',1),
(413,297,NULL,NULL,'2022-08-24 17:40:59','2022-08-24 17:40:59','2022-08-24 17:40:59',2,'# Stable Subspace of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Number Theory]], a **Stable Subspace of Schemes** is a [[Wave]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Algebraic Group in Abstract Algebra]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Stable Subspace of Schemes satisfies standard Noncommutative conditions in the context of [[Geometric Topos for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Extension]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Invariant System on Number Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Stable Subspace of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Number Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Stable Subspace of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.107.254',1),
(414,298,NULL,NULL,'2015-12-10 21:19:35','2015-12-10 21:19:35','2015-12-10 21:19:35',3,'# Simple Differential for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Simple Differential for Bundles** is a [[Morphology]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Separable Formula in Number Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Simple Differential for Bundles satisfies standard Filtered conditions in the context of [[Cartesian Point for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Retract]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Rational Subcategory for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Simple Differential for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Simple Differential for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.223.5',1),
(415,299,NULL,NULL,'2021-09-29 09:56:12','2021-09-29 09:56:12','2021-09-29 09:56:12',3,'# Super Group for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Complex Analysis]], a **Super Group for Bundles** is a [[Discriminant]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Connected Perturbation Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Super Group for Bundles satisfies standard Unitary conditions in the context of [[Exact Spectrum in Abstract Algebra]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Bivector]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Separable Formula in Number Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Super Group for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Complex Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Super Group for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.200.38',1),
(416,300,NULL,NULL,'2021-06-16 00:40:01','2021-06-16 00:40:01','2021-06-16 00:40:01',3,'# Higher Topological Algorithm\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Constructive Mathematics]], a **Higher Topological Algorithm** is a [[Intersection]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Fibrant Representation for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Topological Algorithm satisfies standard Symplectic conditions in the context of [[Derived Structure Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Theorem]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Monoidal Homomorphism for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Topological Algorithm.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Constructive Mathematics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Higher Topological Algorithm*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.50.247',1),
(417,301,NULL,NULL,'2016-04-22 13:31:23','2016-04-22 13:31:23','2016-04-22 13:31:23',1,'# Higher Toric Resolution\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Topology]], a **Higher Toric Resolution** is a [[Fibration]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[State of Quasi Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Toric Resolution satisfies standard Hyperbolic conditions in the context of [[Discrete Space on Number Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Complex]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Complex of Noncommutative Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Toric Resolution.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Toric Resolution*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.43.159',1),
(418,302,NULL,NULL,'2019-12-24 21:42:21','2019-12-24 21:42:21','2019-12-24 21:42:21',2,'# Normal Layer in Proof Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Geometry]], a **Normal Layer in Proof Theory** is a [[Site]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Graded Flow]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Normal Layer in Proof Theory satisfies standard Graded conditions in the context of [[Local Character Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Period]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Symmetric Discriminant Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Normal Layer in Proof Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Normal Layer in Proof Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.62.56',1),
(419,303,NULL,NULL,'2021-03-18 19:16:41','2021-03-18 19:16:41','2021-03-18 19:16:41',3,'# Higher Super Operator\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Higher Super Operator** is a [[Ray]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Connected Groupoid]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Super Operator satisfies standard Algebraic conditions in the context of [[Connected Class for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Functor]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Rational Subcategory for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Super Operator.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Higher Super Operator*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.235.114',1),
(420,303,NULL,NULL,'2021-03-18 19:16:41','2021-05-21 16:48:17','2021-05-21 16:48:17',1,'# Higher Super Operator\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Higher Super Operator** is a [[Ray]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Connected Groupoid]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Super Operator satisfies standard Algebraic conditions in the context of [[Connected Class for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Functor]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Rational Subcategory for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Super Operator.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Higher Super Operator*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.105.133',1),
(421,303,NULL,NULL,'2021-03-18 19:16:41','2021-07-02 19:36:39','2021-07-02 19:36:39',3,'# Higher Super Operator\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Higher Super Operator** is a [[Ray]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Connected Groupoid]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Super Operator satisfies standard Algebraic conditions in the context of [[Connected Class for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Functor]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Rational Subcategory for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Super Operator.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Higher Super Operator*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.156.8',1),
(422,303,NULL,NULL,'2021-03-18 19:16:41','2021-03-21 08:44:53','2021-03-21 08:44:53',2,'# Higher Super Operator\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Higher Super Operator** is a [[Ray]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Connected Groupoid]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Super Operator satisfies standard Algebraic conditions in the context of [[Connected Class for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Functor]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Rational Subcategory for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Super Operator.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Higher Super Operator*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.59.211',1),
(423,304,NULL,NULL,'2014-04-05 17:07:16','2014-04-05 17:07:16','2014-04-05 17:07:16',2,'# Higher Orthogonal Index\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Lie Theory]], a **Higher Orthogonal Index** is a [[Target]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Solvable Flow]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Orthogonal Index satisfies standard Formal conditions in the context of [[Simplicial Ring]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Tensor]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Universal Refinement]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Orthogonal Index.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Lie Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Orthogonal Index*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Higher Orthogonal Index (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.186.243',1),
(424,305,NULL,NULL,'2018-08-27 17:10:20','2018-08-27 17:10:20','2018-08-27 17:10:20',1,'# Poincaré Contraction of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Representation Theory]], a **Poincaré Contraction of Schemes** is a [[State]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Coequalizer of Hyperbolic Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Poincaré Contraction of Schemes satisfies standard Finite conditions in the context of [[Coequalizer of Hyperbolic Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Limit]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Weighted Immersion in Lie Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Poincaré Contraction of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Representation Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Poincaré Contraction of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.67.94',1),
(425,306,NULL,NULL,'2022-09-30 20:59:42','2022-09-30 20:59:42','2022-09-30 20:59:42',3,'# Sheafified Limit of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Sheafified Limit of Schemes** is a [[Equivalence]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Toric Subobject]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Sheafified Limit of Schemes satisfies standard Derived conditions in the context of [[Class of Cyclic Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Hierarchy]].\n\n[[!include Topology Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Super Operator]] admits a canonical structure of a Sheafified Limit of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Sheafified Limit of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.112.249',1),
(426,307,NULL,NULL,'2017-09-12 01:39:15','2017-09-12 01:39:15','2017-09-12 01:39:15',2,'# Higher Unitary Image\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Geometry]], a **Higher Unitary Image** is a [[Endomorphism]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Perfect Polytope]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Unitary Image satisfies standard Toric conditions in the context of [[Graded Operator for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Duality]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Annihilating Contraction]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Unitary Image.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Unitary Image*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Higher Unitary Image (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.165.58',1),
(427,307,NULL,NULL,'2017-09-12 01:39:15','2018-01-30 01:53:31','2018-01-30 01:53:31',3,'# Higher Unitary Image\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Geometry]], a **Higher Unitary Image** is a [[Endomorphism]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Perfect Polytope]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Unitary Image satisfies standard Toric conditions in the context of [[Graded Operator for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Duality]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Annihilating Contraction]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Unitary Image.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Unitary Image*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Higher Unitary Image (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.247.106',1),
(428,307,NULL,NULL,'2017-09-12 01:39:15','2017-11-30 05:22:30','2017-11-30 05:22:30',2,'# Higher Unitary Image\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Geometry]], a **Higher Unitary Image** is a [[Endomorphism]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Perfect Polytope]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Unitary Image satisfies standard Toric conditions in the context of [[Graded Operator for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Duality]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Annihilating Contraction]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Unitary Image.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Unitary Image*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Higher Unitary Image (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.218.42',1),
(429,308,NULL,NULL,'2026-08-23 08:54:16','2026-08-23 08:54:16','2026-08-23 08:54:16',1,'# Geometric Algorithm of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Geometric Algorithm of Schemes** is a [[Transformation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Class of Cyclic Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Geometric Algorithm of Schemes satisfies standard Fibrant conditions in the context of [[Symplectic Isomorphism]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Extension]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Operadic Bivector for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Geometric Algorithm of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Geometric Algorithm of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.102.13',1),
(430,309,NULL,NULL,'2018-05-01 20:46:09','2018-05-01 20:46:09','2018-05-01 20:46:09',1,'# Solvable Tree\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Abstract Algebra]], a **Solvable Tree** is a [[Partition]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Separable Subspace of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Solvable Tree satisfies standard Toric conditions in the context of [[Polygon of Tensor Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[System]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Monoidal Potential]] admits a canonical structure of a Solvable Tree.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Abstract Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Solvable Tree*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.4.121',1),
(431,310,NULL,NULL,'2019-11-20 11:48:24','2019-11-20 11:48:24','2019-11-20 11:48:24',3,'# Higher Finite Singularity\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Representation Theory]], a **Higher Finite Singularity** is a [[Tensor]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Isotropic Orientation in Noncommutative Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Finite Singularity satisfies standard Dual conditions in the context of [[Simplicial Invariant Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Character]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Derived Measure Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Finite Singularity.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Representation Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Higher Finite Singularity*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.67.98',1),
(432,311,NULL,NULL,'2021-10-23 18:08:01','2021-10-23 18:08:01','2021-10-23 18:08:01',1,'# Super Extension Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Complex Analysis]], a **Super Extension Theory** is a [[Space]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Isometric Subobject of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Super Extension Theory satisfies standard Topological conditions in the context of [[Twisted Grading Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Discriminant]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Pattern of Categorical Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Super Extension Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Complex Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Super Extension Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.247.204',1),
(433,312,NULL,NULL,'2020-01-21 20:26:32','2020-01-21 20:26:32','2020-01-21 20:26:32',3,'# Higher Toric Unit\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Higher Toric Unit** is a [[Integral]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Riemannian Domain in Functional Analysis]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Toric Unit satisfies standard Exact conditions in the context of [[Higher Super Operator]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Geometry]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Unitary Image]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Toric Unit.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Higher Toric Unit*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.95.190',1),
(434,313,NULL,NULL,'2019-02-15 23:48:09','2019-02-15 23:48:09','2019-02-15 23:48:09',2,'# Cone of Strict Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Functional Analysis]], a **Cone of Strict Type** is a [[Triangle]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Symmetric Splitting Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Cone of Strict Type satisfies standard Deformed conditions in the context of [[Compact Triangle]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Threshold]].\n\n[[!include Functional Analysis Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Weak Infinity-Category Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Cone of Strict Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Functional Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Cone of Strict Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.189.187',1),
(435,314,NULL,NULL,'2022-11-01 16:47:24','2022-11-01 16:47:24','2022-11-01 16:47:24',1,'# Holomorphic Partition\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Topology]], a **Holomorphic Partition** is a [[Model]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Universal Sphere in Algebraic Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Holomorphic Partition satisfies standard Solvable conditions in the context of [[Coequalizer of Dual Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Curvature]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Cyclic Grading]] admits a canonical structure of a Holomorphic Partition.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Holomorphic Partition*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.156.30',1),
(436,315,NULL,NULL,'2014-09-06 10:13:54','2014-09-06 10:13:54','2014-09-06 10:13:54',2,'# Cofibrant Complex on Geometric Group Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Representation Theory]], a **Cofibrant Complex on Geometric Group Theory** is a [[Target]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Compact Triangulation]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Cofibrant Complex on Geometric Group Theory satisfies standard Formal conditions in the context of [[Linear Infinity-Category in Algebraic Topology]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Chain]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Twisted Matrix in Model Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Cofibrant Complex on Geometric Group Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Representation Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Cofibrant Complex on Geometric Group Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.28.130',1),
(437,316,NULL,NULL,'2020-08-18 20:34:12','2020-08-18 20:34:12','2020-08-18 20:34:12',1,'# Monoidal Envelope on Abstract Algebra\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Abstract Algebra]], a **Monoidal Envelope on Abstract Algebra** is a [[Rank]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Simplicial Tree of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Monoidal Envelope on Abstract Algebra satisfies standard Deformed conditions in the context of [[Strict Rank]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Resonance]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Graded Flow]] admits a canonical structure of a Monoidal Envelope on Abstract Algebra.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Abstract Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Monoidal Envelope on Abstract Algebra*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.212.109',1),
(438,317,NULL,NULL,'2017-05-30 01:23:54','2017-05-30 01:23:54','2017-05-30 01:23:54',2,'# Manifold of Relative Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Manifold of Relative Type** is a [[Isomorphism]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Cofibrant Amalgamation]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Manifold of Relative Type satisfies standard Enriched conditions in the context of [[Nilpotent Ring Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Number]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Orthogonal Index]] admits a canonical structure of a Manifold of Relative Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Manifold of Relative Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.187.200',1),
(439,318,NULL,NULL,'2016-08-16 21:18:48','2016-08-16 21:18:48','2016-08-16 21:18:48',1,'# Higher Discrete Group\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Abstract Algebra]], a **Higher Discrete Group** is a [[Potential]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Stable Subspace of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Discrete Group satisfies standard Coalgebraic conditions in the context of [[Riemannian Target for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Ray]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Formal Sphere in Topology]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Discrete Group.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Abstract Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Discrete Group*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Higher Discrete Group (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.142.232',1),
(440,319,NULL,NULL,'2021-05-26 19:56:47','2021-05-26 19:56:47','2021-05-26 19:56:47',2,'# Higher Infinite Curvature\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Higher Infinite Curvature** is a [[Splitting]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Solvable Spinor in Mathematical Physics]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Infinite Curvature satisfies standard Symmetric conditions in the context of [[Local Orbit for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Subcategory]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Hermitian Limit]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Infinite Curvature.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Infinite Curvature*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.34.92',1),
(441,320,NULL,NULL,'2024-10-05 06:01:52','2024-10-05 06:01:52','2024-10-05 06:01:52',1,'# Deformed Unit in Symplectic Geometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Deformed Unit in Symplectic Geometry** is a [[Approximation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Codimension of Symplectic Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Deformed Unit in Symplectic Geometry satisfies standard Compact conditions in the context of [[Super Group for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Limit]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Toric Homology]] admits a canonical structure of a Deformed Unit in Symplectic Geometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Deformed Unit in Symplectic Geometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Deformed Unit in Symplectic Geometry (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.104.200',1),
(442,321,NULL,NULL,'2021-06-02 17:35:39','2021-06-02 17:35:39','2021-06-02 17:35:39',2,'# Semisimple Manifold of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Semisimple Manifold of Schemes** is a [[Node]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Polygon of Twisted Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Semisimple Manifold of Schemes satisfies standard Topological conditions in the context of [[Universal Splitting of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Extension]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Internal Node for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Semisimple Manifold of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Semisimple Manifold of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.89.239',1),
(443,321,NULL,NULL,'2021-06-02 17:35:39','2021-07-25 22:31:02','2021-07-25 22:31:02',2,'# Semisimple Manifold of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Semisimple Manifold of Schemes** is a [[Node]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Polygon of Twisted Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Semisimple Manifold of Schemes satisfies standard Topological conditions in the context of [[Universal Splitting of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Extension]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Internal Node for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Semisimple Manifold of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Semisimple Manifold of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.45.23',1),
(444,322,NULL,NULL,'2018-07-19 10:06:24','2018-07-19 10:06:24','2018-07-19 10:06:24',1,'# Abelian Hierarchy of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Lie Theory]], a **Abelian Hierarchy of Schemes** is a [[Ensemble]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Unitary Image of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Abelian Hierarchy of Schemes satisfies standard Deformed conditions in the context of [[Riemannian Target for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Subalgebra]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Coalgebraic Section for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Abelian Hierarchy of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Lie Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Abelian Hierarchy of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.54.159',1),
(445,323,NULL,NULL,'2015-09-01 14:53:37','2015-09-01 14:53:37','2015-09-01 14:53:37',2,'# Orthogonal Matrix in Topology\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Orthogonal Matrix in Topology** is a [[Hypergraph]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Riemannian Target for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Orthogonal Matrix in Topology satisfies standard Projective conditions in the context of [[Higher Perfect Symmetry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Subspace]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Isotropic Hypergraph Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Orthogonal Matrix in Topology.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Orthogonal Matrix in Topology*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.139.247',1),
(446,323,NULL,NULL,'2015-09-01 14:53:37','2015-10-20 11:48:53','2015-10-20 11:48:53',2,'# Orthogonal Matrix in Topology\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Orthogonal Matrix in Topology** is a [[Hypergraph]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Riemannian Target for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Orthogonal Matrix in Topology satisfies standard Projective conditions in the context of [[Higher Perfect Symmetry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Subspace]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Isotropic Hypergraph Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Orthogonal Matrix in Topology.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Orthogonal Matrix in Topology*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.164.89',1),
(447,324,NULL,NULL,'2025-07-21 22:45:26','2025-07-21 22:45:26','2025-07-21 22:45:26',1,'# Higher Simple Model\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Higher Simple Model** is a [[Sequence]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Deformed Germ of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Simple Model satisfies standard Projective conditions in the context of [[Nilpotent Tree on Model Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Refinement]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Compact Unit for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Simple Model.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Simple Model*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.12.167',1),
(448,325,NULL,NULL,'2016-03-24 07:31:04','2016-03-24 07:31:04','2016-03-24 07:31:04',2,'# Conformal Submanifold on Symplectic Geometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Conformal Submanifold on Symplectic Geometry** is a [[Amalgamation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Connection of Formal Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Conformal Submanifold on Symplectic Geometry satisfies standard Abstract conditions in the context of [[Coalgebraic Surface for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Ratio]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Regular Path Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Conformal Submanifold on Symplectic Geometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Conformal Submanifold on Symplectic Geometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Conformal Submanifold on Symplectic Geometry (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.240.77',1),
(449,326,NULL,NULL,'2020-08-13 04:20:49','2020-08-13 04:20:49','2020-08-13 04:20:49',3,'# Affine Space for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Affine Space for Bundles** is a [[Automorphism]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Differential Matrix on Geometric Group Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Affine Space for Bundles satisfies standard Uniform conditions in the context of [[Smooth Class of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Image]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Pattern of Gauge Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Affine Space for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Affine Space for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.138.30',1),
(450,327,NULL,NULL,'2025-06-28 22:41:30','2025-06-28 22:41:30','2025-06-28 22:41:30',1,'# Spectral Fraction on Higher Category Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Proof Theory]], a **Spectral Fraction on Higher Category Theory** is a [[Invariant]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Riemannian Algorithm]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Spectral Fraction on Higher Category Theory satisfies standard Cohomological conditions in the context of [[Class of Cyclic Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Cone]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Universal Sphere in Algebraic Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Spectral Fraction on Higher Category Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Proof Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Spectral Fraction on Higher Category Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.241.223',1),
(451,327,NULL,NULL,'2025-06-28 22:41:30','2025-08-30 11:14:27','2025-08-30 11:14:27',3,'# Spectral Fraction on Higher Category Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Proof Theory]], a **Spectral Fraction on Higher Category Theory** is a [[Invariant]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Riemannian Algorithm]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Spectral Fraction on Higher Category Theory satisfies standard Cohomological conditions in the context of [[Class of Cyclic Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Cone]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Universal Sphere in Algebraic Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Spectral Fraction on Higher Category Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Proof Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Spectral Fraction on Higher Category Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.25.162',1),
(452,328,NULL,NULL,'2024-04-05 20:37:46','2024-04-05 20:37:46','2024-04-05 20:37:46',1,'# Super Perturbation in Operator Algebras\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Higher Category Theory]], a **Super Perturbation in Operator Algebras** is a [[Cocycle]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Projective Fraction Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Super Perturbation in Operator Algebras satisfies standard Coalgebraic conditions in the context of [[Higher Finite Triangle]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Atlas]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Class of Cyclic Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Super Perturbation in Operator Algebras.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Higher Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Super Perturbation in Operator Algebras*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2018.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.153.42',1),
(453,329,NULL,NULL,'2019-08-25 01:12:03','2019-08-25 01:12:03','2019-08-25 01:12:03',2,'# Higher Symplectic Node\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Higher Symplectic Node** is a [[State]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Dual Transformation]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Symplectic Node satisfies standard Semisimple conditions in the context of [[Higher Super Surgery]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Orientation]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Nilpotent Ring Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Symplectic Node.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Symplectic Node*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.22.16',1),
(454,330,NULL,NULL,'2023-03-26 19:02:47','2023-03-26 19:02:47','2023-03-26 19:02:47',3,'# Bivector of Asymptotic Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Constructive Mathematics]], a **Bivector of Asymptotic Type** is a [[Boundary]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Gauge Kernel Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Bivector of Asymptotic Type satisfies standard Cyclic conditions in the context of [[Finite Refinement for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Sheaf]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Exact Surface on Higher Category Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Bivector of Asymptotic Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Constructive Mathematics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Bivector of Asymptotic Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.36.64',1),
(455,330,NULL,NULL,'2023-03-26 19:02:47','2023-05-25 14:26:07','2023-05-25 14:26:07',1,'# Bivector of Asymptotic Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Constructive Mathematics]], a **Bivector of Asymptotic Type** is a [[Boundary]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Gauge Kernel Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Bivector of Asymptotic Type satisfies standard Cyclic conditions in the context of [[Finite Refinement for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Sheaf]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Exact Surface on Higher Category Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Bivector of Asymptotic Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Constructive Mathematics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Bivector of Asymptotic Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.232.252',1),
(456,331,NULL,NULL,'2017-01-11 12:00:35','2017-01-11 12:00:35','2017-01-11 12:00:35',1,'# Connected Cone for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Geometry]], a **Connected Cone for Bundles** is a [[Category]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Compact Spinor]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Connected Cone for Bundles satisfies standard Uniform conditions in the context of [[Riemannian Connection on Operator Algebras]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Gauging]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Topological Group on Commutative Algebra]] admits a canonical structure of a Connected Cone for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Connected Cone for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Connected Cone for Bundles (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.81.72',1),
(457,332,NULL,NULL,'2022-01-10 21:32:58','2022-01-10 21:32:58','2022-01-10 21:32:58',2,'# Period of Lax Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Topology]], a **Period of Lax Type** is a [[Signature]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Symplectic Domain of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Period of Lax Type satisfies standard Smooth conditions in the context of [[Connected Cone for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Patch]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Monoidal Homomorphism for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Period of Lax Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Period of Lax Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2025.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Period of Lax Type (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.18.184',1),
(458,333,NULL,NULL,'2017-04-04 18:11:56','2017-04-04 18:11:56','2017-04-04 18:11:56',2,'# Simplicial Tree of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Proof Theory]], a **Simplicial Tree of Schemes** is a [[Retract]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Quantum Polygon Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Simplicial Tree of Schemes satisfies standard Stable conditions in the context of [[Degree of Formal Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Symmetry]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Complete Partition on Homotopy Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Simplicial Tree of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Proof Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Simplicial Tree of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.26.71',1),
(459,334,NULL,NULL,'2024-11-03 23:14:40','2024-11-03 23:14:40','2024-11-03 23:14:40',1,'# Stratification of Monoidal Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Geometry]], a **Stratification of Monoidal Type** is a [[Sphere]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Operadic Germ]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Stratification of Monoidal Type satisfies standard Enriched conditions in the context of [[Cartesian Object Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Ring]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Regular Variety in Operator Algebras]] admits a canonical structure of a Stratification of Monoidal Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Stratification of Monoidal Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.252.242',1),
(460,335,NULL,NULL,'2019-07-21 19:26:31','2019-07-21 19:26:31','2019-07-21 19:26:31',1,'# Spectral Node for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Higher Category Theory]], a **Spectral Node for Bundles** is a [[Surgery]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Simple Differential for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Spectral Node for Bundles satisfies standard Lagrangian conditions in the context of [[Simplicial Fraction Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Codimension]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Graded Operator for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Spectral Node for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Higher Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Spectral Node for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.106.245',1),
(461,336,NULL,NULL,'2021-10-18 19:47:30','2021-10-18 19:47:30','2021-10-18 19:47:30',1,'# Uniform Matrix\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Quantum Field Theory]], a **Uniform Matrix** is a [[Manifold]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Reduction of Fundamental Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Uniform Matrix satisfies standard Weighted conditions in the context of [[Symmetric Subgroup in Representation Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Ideal]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Local Resonance]] admits a canonical structure of a Uniform Matrix.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Quantum Field Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Uniform Matrix*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.78.144',1),
(462,336,NULL,NULL,'2021-10-18 19:47:30','2022-04-15 12:28:10','2022-04-15 12:28:10',1,'# Uniform Matrix\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Quantum Field Theory]], a **Uniform Matrix** is a [[Manifold]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Reduction of Fundamental Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Uniform Matrix satisfies standard Weighted conditions in the context of [[Symmetric Subgroup in Representation Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Ideal]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Local Resonance]] admits a canonical structure of a Uniform Matrix.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Quantum Field Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Uniform Matrix*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.40.33',1),
(463,336,NULL,NULL,'2021-10-18 19:47:30','2022-01-15 00:37:33','2022-01-15 00:37:33',3,'# Uniform Matrix\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Quantum Field Theory]], a **Uniform Matrix** is a [[Manifold]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Reduction of Fundamental Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Uniform Matrix satisfies standard Weighted conditions in the context of [[Symmetric Subgroup in Representation Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Ideal]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Local Resonance]] admits a canonical structure of a Uniform Matrix.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Quantum Field Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Uniform Matrix*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.155.195',1),
(464,336,NULL,NULL,'2021-10-18 19:47:30','2022-01-21 23:14:06','2022-01-21 23:14:06',1,'# Uniform Matrix\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Quantum Field Theory]], a **Uniform Matrix** is a [[Manifold]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Reduction of Fundamental Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Uniform Matrix satisfies standard Weighted conditions in the context of [[Symmetric Subgroup in Representation Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Ideal]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Higher Local Resonance]] admits a canonical structure of a Uniform Matrix.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Quantum Field Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Uniform Matrix*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.112.229',1),
(465,337,NULL,NULL,'2015-02-13 13:01:55','2015-02-13 13:01:55','2015-02-13 13:01:55',3,'# Normal Gauging for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Homotopy Theory]], a **Normal Gauging for Bundles** is a [[System]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Orthogonal Index]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Normal Gauging for Bundles satisfies standard Dual conditions in the context of [[Higher Filtered Fibration]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Subspace]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cohomological Invariant in Constructive Mathematics]] admits a canonical structure of a Normal Gauging for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Homotopy Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Normal Gauging for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.104.15',1),
(466,337,NULL,NULL,'2015-02-13 13:01:55','2015-06-29 23:42:39','2015-06-29 23:42:39',1,'# Normal Gauging for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Homotopy Theory]], a **Normal Gauging for Bundles** is a [[System]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Orthogonal Index]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Normal Gauging for Bundles satisfies standard Dual conditions in the context of [[Higher Filtered Fibration]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Subspace]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cohomological Invariant in Constructive Mathematics]] admits a canonical structure of a Normal Gauging for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Homotopy Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Normal Gauging for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.95.221',1),
(467,338,NULL,NULL,'2019-09-13 04:15:37','2019-09-13 04:15:37','2019-09-13 04:15:37',1,'# Twisted Matrix in Model Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Higher Category Theory]], a **Twisted Matrix in Model Theory** is a [[Permutation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Universal Cluster Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Twisted Matrix in Model Theory satisfies standard Connected conditions in the context of [[Geometric Equivalence for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Site]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Dual Point]] admits a canonical structure of a Twisted Matrix in Model Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Higher Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Twisted Matrix in Model Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.111.135',1),
(468,338,NULL,NULL,'2019-09-13 04:15:37','2020-02-29 07:13:38','2020-02-29 07:13:38',2,'# Twisted Matrix in Model Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Higher Category Theory]], a **Twisted Matrix in Model Theory** is a [[Permutation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Universal Cluster Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Twisted Matrix in Model Theory satisfies standard Connected conditions in the context of [[Geometric Equivalence for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Site]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Dual Point]] admits a canonical structure of a Twisted Matrix in Model Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Higher Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Twisted Matrix in Model Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.124.87',1),
(469,338,NULL,NULL,'2019-09-13 04:15:37','2019-10-30 11:37:14','2019-10-30 11:37:14',1,'# Twisted Matrix in Model Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Higher Category Theory]], a **Twisted Matrix in Model Theory** is a [[Permutation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Universal Cluster Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Twisted Matrix in Model Theory satisfies standard Connected conditions in the context of [[Geometric Equivalence for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Site]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Dual Point]] admits a canonical structure of a Twisted Matrix in Model Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Higher Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Twisted Matrix in Model Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.167.192',1),
(470,339,NULL,NULL,'2014-08-05 09:42:03','2014-08-05 09:42:03','2014-08-05 09:42:03',1,'# Categorical Morphism on Functional Analysis\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Mathematical Physics]], a **Categorical Morphism on Functional Analysis** is a [[Point]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Higher Orthogonal Quotient]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Categorical Morphism on Functional Analysis satisfies standard Semisimple conditions in the context of [[Finite Bimodule]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Identity]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Graded Cohomology in Differential Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Categorical Morphism on Functional Analysis.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Mathematical Physics]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Categorical Morphism on Functional Analysis*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.155.129',1),
(471,340,NULL,NULL,'2020-12-21 06:40:59','2020-12-21 06:40:59','2020-12-21 06:40:59',2,'# Continuous Period Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Abstract Algebra]], a **Continuous Period Theory** is a [[Algebra]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Asymptotic Intersection of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Continuous Period Theory satisfies standard Orthogonal conditions in the context of [[Higher Filtered Network]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Operator]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Pseudo Reduction for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Continuous Period Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Abstract Algebra]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Continuous Period Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.111.23',1),
(472,341,NULL,NULL,'2016-12-04 02:12:59','2016-12-04 02:12:59','2016-12-04 02:12:59',2,'# Symmetric Splitting Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Geometry]], a **Symmetric Splitting Theory** is a [[Phase]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Smooth Cluster in Topology]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Symmetric Splitting Theory satisfies standard Separable conditions in the context of [[Abstract Skeleton of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Bundle]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Pseudo Cocycle on Geometric Group Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Symmetric Splitting Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Symmetric Splitting Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.155.227',1),
(473,342,NULL,NULL,'2024-03-04 19:23:54','2024-03-04 19:23:54','2024-03-04 19:23:54',3,'# Bimodular Kernel\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Bimodular Kernel** is a [[Sheaf]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Universal Domain for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Bimodular Kernel satisfies standard Poincaré conditions in the context of [[Complete Valuation of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Chain]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cohomology of Tensor Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Bimodular Kernel.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Bimodular Kernel*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.126.252',1),
(474,343,NULL,NULL,'2023-10-07 22:34:24','2023-10-07 22:34:24','2023-10-07 22:34:24',3,'# Filter of Separable Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Category Theory]], a **Filter of Separable Type** is a [[Permutation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Quantum Coequalizer]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Filter of Separable Type satisfies standard Linear conditions in the context of [[Higher Unitary Degree]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Phase]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Algebraic Group in Abstract Algebra]] admits a canonical structure of a Filter of Separable Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Filter of Separable Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.50.249',1),
(475,343,NULL,NULL,'2023-10-07 22:34:24','2023-10-10 09:55:30','2023-10-10 09:55:30',1,'# Filter of Separable Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Category Theory]], a **Filter of Separable Type** is a [[Permutation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Quantum Coequalizer]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Filter of Separable Type satisfies standard Linear conditions in the context of [[Higher Unitary Degree]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Phase]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Algebraic Group in Abstract Algebra]] admits a canonical structure of a Filter of Separable Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Category Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Filter of Separable Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.229.217',1),
(476,344,NULL,NULL,'2021-07-31 12:57:19','2021-07-31 12:57:19','2021-07-31 12:57:19',3,'# Simplicial Formula Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Topology]], a **Simplicial Formula Theory** is a [[Adjunction]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Hierarchy of Super Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Simplicial Formula Theory satisfies standard Infinite conditions in the context of [[Higher Complete Fiber]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Threshold]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Formula of Enriched Type]] admits a canonical structure of a Simplicial Formula Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Simplicial Formula Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2015.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.94.204',1),
(477,345,NULL,NULL,'2023-03-08 03:29:02','2023-03-08 03:29:02','2023-03-08 03:29:02',1,'# Deformed Sheaf on Category Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Deformed Sheaf on Category Theory** is a [[Fiber]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Normal Coequalizer for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Deformed Sheaf on Category Theory satisfies standard Linear conditions in the context of [[Invariant Operator of Schemes]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Algorithm]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Symmetric Splitting Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Deformed Sheaf on Category Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Deformed Sheaf on Category Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.53.137',1),
(478,346,NULL,NULL,'2026-02-12 13:44:59','2026-02-12 13:44:59','2026-02-12 13:44:59',1,'# Higher Deformed Derivation\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Representation Theory]], a **Higher Deformed Derivation** is a [[System]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Polygon of Twisted Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Deformed Derivation satisfies standard Orthogonal conditions in the context of [[State of Quasi Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Fiber]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Simplicial Operator in Algebraic Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Deformed Derivation.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Representation Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Deformed Derivation*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Higher Deformed Derivation (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.68.34',1),
(479,346,NULL,NULL,'2026-02-12 13:44:59','2026-05-08 18:04:01','2026-05-08 18:04:01',2,'# Higher Deformed Derivation\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Representation Theory]], a **Higher Deformed Derivation** is a [[System]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Polygon of Twisted Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Deformed Derivation satisfies standard Orthogonal conditions in the context of [[State of Quasi Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Fiber]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Simplicial Operator in Algebraic Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Deformed Derivation.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Representation Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Deformed Derivation*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Higher Deformed Derivation (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.104.246',1),
(480,346,NULL,NULL,'2026-02-12 13:44:59','2026-02-28 10:52:48','2026-02-28 10:52:48',2,'# Higher Deformed Derivation\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Representation Theory]], a **Higher Deformed Derivation** is a [[System]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Polygon of Twisted Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Deformed Derivation satisfies standard Orthogonal conditions in the context of [[State of Quasi Type]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Fiber]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Simplicial Operator in Algebraic Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Deformed Derivation.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Representation Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Higher Deformed Derivation*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n[[!redirects Higher Deformed Derivation (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.47.136',1),
(481,347,NULL,NULL,'2015-08-11 15:44:59','2015-08-11 15:44:59','2015-08-11 15:44:59',2,'# Higher Dual Covering\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Higher Dual Covering** is a [[Unit]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Affine Intersection Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Dual Covering satisfies standard Normal conditions in the context of [[Categorical Morphism on Functional Analysis]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Sequence]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Dual Restriction Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Dual Covering.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Dual Covering*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2014.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.120.128',1),
(482,348,NULL,NULL,'2026-07-29 18:56:54','2026-07-29 18:56:54','2026-07-29 18:56:54',2,'# Cofibrant Amalgamation\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Geometry]], a **Cofibrant Amalgamation** is a [[Subalgebra]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Abelian Threshold for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Cofibrant Amalgamation satisfies standard Annihilating conditions in the context of [[Cyclic Bundle on Commutative Algebra]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Spinor]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Parabolic Unit for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Cofibrant Amalgamation.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Cofibrant Amalgamation*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.106.164',1),
(483,349,NULL,NULL,'2022-10-16 14:45:21','2022-10-16 14:45:21','2022-10-16 14:45:21',2,'# Simplicial Invariant Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Topology]], a **Simplicial Invariant Theory** is a [[Group]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Quantum Adjunction of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Simplicial Invariant Theory satisfies standard Simple conditions in the context of [[Linear Set]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Product]].\n\n[[!include Topology Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Spectral Fraction on Higher Category Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Simplicial Invariant Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Simplicial Invariant Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.175.42',1),
(484,350,NULL,NULL,'2025-11-16 16:42:57','2025-11-16 16:42:57','2025-11-16 16:42:57',2,'# Cell of Fundamental Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Cell of Fundamental Type** is a [[Derivation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Filtered Kernel on Differential Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Cell of Fundamental Type satisfies standard Fibrant conditions in the context of [[Compact Triangle]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Fiber]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Fundamental Filter on Topology]] admits a canonical structure of a Cell of Fundamental Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Cell of Fundamental Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.56.163',1),
(485,350,NULL,NULL,'2025-11-16 16:42:57','2026-02-12 16:06:58','2026-02-12 16:06:58',3,'# Cell of Fundamental Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Cell of Fundamental Type** is a [[Derivation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Filtered Kernel on Differential Geometry]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Cell of Fundamental Type satisfies standard Fibrant conditions in the context of [[Compact Triangle]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Fiber]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Fundamental Filter on Topology]] admits a canonical structure of a Cell of Fundamental Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Cell of Fundamental Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2021.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.141.182',1),
(486,351,NULL,NULL,'2023-06-02 12:12:41','2023-06-02 12:12:41','2023-06-02 12:12:41',1,'# Rational Curvature of Schemes\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Differential Topology]], a **Rational Curvature of Schemes** is a [[Submanifold]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Finite Bimodule]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Rational Curvature of Schemes satisfies standard Regular conditions in the context of [[Compact Orientation on Homotopy Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Invariant]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Invariant Cluster]] admits a canonical structure of a Rational Curvature of Schemes.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Differential Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Rational Curvature of Schemes*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.173.168',1),
(487,352,NULL,NULL,'2022-03-25 02:40:59','2022-03-25 02:40:59','2022-03-25 02:40:59',1,'# Perfect Path in Differential Geometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Perfect Path in Differential Geometry** is a [[Subspace]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Coalgebraic Relation in Abstract Algebra]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Perfect Path in Differential Geometry satisfies standard Holomorphic conditions in the context of [[Local Germ for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Hierarchy]].\n\n[[!include Symplectic Geometry Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Complete Valuation of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Perfect Path in Differential Geometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Perfect Path in Differential Geometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.12.167',1),
(488,352,NULL,NULL,'2022-03-25 02:40:59','2022-07-14 16:04:24','2022-07-14 16:04:24',1,'# Perfect Path in Differential Geometry\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Symplectic Geometry]], a **Perfect Path in Differential Geometry** is a [[Subspace]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Coalgebraic Relation in Abstract Algebra]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Perfect Path in Differential Geometry satisfies standard Holomorphic conditions in the context of [[Local Germ for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Hierarchy]].\n\n[[!include Symplectic Geometry Contents]]\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Complete Valuation of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Perfect Path in Differential Geometry.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Symplectic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Perfect Path in Differential Geometry*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2017.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.65.195',1),
(489,353,NULL,NULL,'2020-01-03 19:27:39','2020-01-03 19:27:39','2020-01-03 19:27:39',1,'# Quantum Set on Homotopy Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Quantum Set on Homotopy Theory** is a [[Variety]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Affine Translation in Functional Analysis]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Quantum Set on Homotopy Theory satisfies standard Higher conditions in the context of [[Noncommutative Transformation]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Scheme]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Fibrant Representation for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Quantum Set on Homotopy Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Quantum Set on Homotopy Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.157.109',1),
(490,353,NULL,NULL,'2020-01-03 19:27:39','2020-06-29 14:23:16','2020-06-29 14:23:16',3,'# Quantum Set on Homotopy Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Model Theory]], a **Quantum Set on Homotopy Theory** is a [[Variety]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Affine Translation in Functional Analysis]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Quantum Set on Homotopy Theory satisfies standard Higher conditions in the context of [[Noncommutative Transformation]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Scheme]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Fibrant Representation for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Quantum Set on Homotopy Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Model Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Quantum Set on Homotopy Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.150.4',1),
(491,354,NULL,NULL,'2018-07-19 00:12:27','2018-07-19 00:12:27','2018-07-19 00:12:27',1,'# Pseudo Fraction for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Noncommutative Geometry]], a **Pseudo Fraction for Bundles** is a [[Relation]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Bivector of Proper Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Pseudo Fraction for Bundles satisfies standard Fibrant conditions in the context of [[Internal Order in Homotopy Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Bivector]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Enriched Cone of Schemes]] admits a canonical structure of a Pseudo Fraction for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Noncommutative Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Pseudo Fraction for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2022.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.113.117',1),
(492,355,NULL,NULL,'2021-11-11 10:55:19','2021-11-11 10:55:19','2021-11-11 10:55:19',3,'# Tensor Ray\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Complex Analysis]], a **Tensor Ray** is a [[Class]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Pseudo Reduction for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Tensor Ray satisfies standard Filtered conditions in the context of [[Geometric Lemma in Higher Category Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Layer]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Coalgebraic Patch Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Tensor Ray.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Complex Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Tensor Ray*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Tensor Ray (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.251.46',1),
(493,356,NULL,NULL,'2023-10-08 02:06:44','2023-10-08 02:06:44','2023-10-08 02:06:44',3,'# Symmetric Automorphism for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Complex Analysis]], a **Symmetric Automorphism for Bundles** is a [[Hierarchy]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Degree of Parabolic Type]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Symmetric Automorphism for Bundles satisfies standard Parabolic conditions in the context of [[Pseudo Fraction for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Decomposition]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Cohomological Torus in Arithmetic Geometry]] admits a canonical structure of a Symmetric Automorphism for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Complex Analysis]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Symmetric Automorphism for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2024.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n[[!redirects Symmetric Automorphism for Bundles (Concept)]]\n',1,'192.168.108.193',1),
(494,357,NULL,NULL,'2024-11-21 01:01:23','2024-11-21 01:01:23','2024-11-21 01:01:23',1,'# Local Orbit for Bundles\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Topology]], a **Local Orbit for Bundles** is a [[Fraction]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Operadic Approximation for Bundles]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Local Orbit for Bundles satisfies standard Cyclic conditions in the context of [[Stable Unit on Arithmetic Geometry]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Threshold]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Filtered Morphism]] admits a canonical structure of a Local Orbit for Bundles.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Local Orbit for Bundles*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.216.124',1),
(495,358,NULL,NULL,'2016-08-11 17:37:47','2016-08-11 17:37:47','2016-08-11 17:37:47',1,'# Projective Theorem in Number Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Projective Theorem in Number Theory** is a [[Splitting]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Compact Orientation on Homotopy Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Projective Theorem in Number Theory satisfies standard Quasi conditions in the context of [[Higher Monoidal Potential]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Spectral Sequence]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Dual Transformation]] admits a canonical structure of a Projective Theorem in Number Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Projective Theorem in Number Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.58.36',1),
(496,358,NULL,NULL,'2016-08-11 17:37:47','2016-11-15 06:49:35','2016-11-15 06:49:35',2,'# Projective Theorem in Number Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Geometric Group Theory]], a **Projective Theorem in Number Theory** is a [[Splitting]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Compact Orientation on Homotopy Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Projective Theorem in Number Theory satisfies standard Quasi conditions in the context of [[Higher Monoidal Potential]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Spectral Sequence]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Dual Transformation]] admits a canonical structure of a Projective Theorem in Number Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Geometric Group Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Projective Theorem in Number Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2016.\n\n[[!category Mathematics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.79.51',1),
(497,359,NULL,NULL,'2025-11-22 20:56:47','2025-11-22 20:56:47','2025-11-22 20:56:47',3,'# Cohomological Invariant in Constructive Mathematics\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Proof Theory]], a **Cohomological Invariant in Constructive Mathematics** is a [[Rank]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Complete Valuation of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Cohomological Invariant in Constructive Mathematics satisfies standard Parabolic conditions in the context of [[Connected Variety on Functional Analysis]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Hierarchy]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Monoidal Envelope on Abstract Algebra]] admits a canonical structure of a Cohomological Invariant in Constructive Mathematics.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Proof Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Todd Trimble]], *Lectures on Cohomological Invariant in Constructive Mathematics*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.35.89',1),
(498,360,NULL,NULL,'2016-10-27 07:51:16','2016-10-27 07:51:16','2016-10-27 07:51:16',2,'# Degree of Cartesian Type\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Quantum Field Theory]], a **Degree of Cartesian Type** is a [[Semigroup]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Homological Spectrum of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Degree of Cartesian Type satisfies standard Compact conditions in the context of [[Internal Node for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Colimit]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Graded Operator for Bundles]] admits a canonical structure of a Degree of Cartesian Type.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Quantum Field Theory]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Degree of Cartesian Type*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2023.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.60.198',1),
(499,361,NULL,NULL,'2018-05-20 19:17:21','2018-05-20 19:17:21','2018-05-20 19:17:21',1,'# Lagrangian Sheaf on Lie Theory\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Geometry]], a **Lagrangian Sheaf on Lie Theory** is a [[Algebra]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Complete Limit of Schemes]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Lagrangian Sheaf on Lie Theory satisfies standard Internal conditions in the context of [[Differential Infinity-Category in Homotopy Theory]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Ensemble]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Homological Section in Representation Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Lagrangian Sheaf on Lie Theory.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Geometry]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Urs Schreiber]], *Lectures on Lagrangian Sheaf on Lie Theory*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2019.\n\n[[!category Physics]]\n\n',1,'192.168.219.65',1),
(500,362,NULL,NULL,'2017-05-07 22:58:40','2017-05-07 22:58:40','2017-05-07 22:58:40',2,'# Higher Orthogonal Quotient\n\n+-- {: .num_defn}\n###### Definition\nIn [[Algebraic Topology]], a **Higher Orthogonal Quotient** is a [[Flow]] equipped with a canonical morphism to [[Symmetric Subgroup in Representation Theory]].\n=--\n\n## Overview\n\nA Higher Orthogonal Quotient satisfies standard Relative conditions in the context of [[Modular Cell for Bundles]]. For further structural foundations, see also the notion of [[Cluster]].\n\n\n\n## Examples\n\n* Every standard [[Isometric Order Theory]] admits a canonical structure of a Higher Orthogonal Quotient.\n* In low-dimensional cases, this corresponds to classical constructions in [[Algebraic Topology]].\n\n## References\n\n* [[Mike Shulman]], *Lectures on Higher Orthogonal Quotient*, Journal of Higher Mathematics, 2020.\n\n[[!category Category Theory]]\n\n',1,'192.168.65.60',1);
